var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#Front-Workers-with-a-Gateway","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"ReactantServerGateway is a gRPC reverse proxy that puts several workers behind one KServe V2 endpoint. The node supervisor already embeds one whenever a node has two or more workers (Run a Node); this page runs the gateway on its own, over workers you start yourself. That is the shape for workers the supervisor does not manage, such as workers on other machines.\n\nThe gateway also decides where each model's traffic goes. This page uses lpt_packing, the mode that places every model on a chosen number of workers, so a model's requests concentrate on few devices and coalesce into full batches.","title":"Front Workers with a Gateway"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#Install","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add([\"ReactantServerGateway\", \"ReactantServer\", \"ReactantServerClient\",\n         \"ReactantServerExport\", \"Lux\"])\n\nThe gateway itself does not load Reactant; only the workers do.","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#Setup:-two-models","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"Export two small MLPs from Export a Model to a Bundle, differing only in their random initialization, into one model repository:\n\nusing Lux, Random, ReactantServerExport\n\nmodel = Lux.Chain(Lux.Dense(4 => 8, tanh), Lux.Dense(8 => 3))\nparams = Dict{String, Any}()\nfor (name, seed) in (\"mlp_a\" => 1, \"mlp_b\" => 2)\n    ps, st = Lux.setup(Random.Xoshiro(seed), model)\n    params[name] = (ps, st)\n    export_bundle(\n        :lux, model, ps, st, randn(Float32, 4, 1);\n        dir = joinpath(\"models\", name), name = name, batch_sizes = [1, 8]\n    )\nend","title":"Setup: two models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#Start-two-workers","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"One node file describes both workers. Worker i listens on base_port + i. lpt_packing moves scheduling decisions to the gateway, so the workers run the first-in, first-out fifo discipline instead of reordering requests themselves:\n\nwrite(\n    \"node.yaml\", \"\"\"\n    model_repo: $(abspath(\"models\"))\n    base_port: 8080\n    metrics_base_port: 9100\n    global:\n      runtime: { backend: cpu }\n      scheduler: { discipline: fifo }\n      endpoints: { host: 127.0.0.1 }\n    workers:\n      - { name: worker0 }\n      - { name: worker1 }\n    \"\"\"\n)\n\nEach worker is its own process, started with serve and the worker's name. From two shells:\n\njulia --project --threads=auto,1 -e 'using ReactantServer; serve(\"node.yaml\"; worker = \"worker0\")'\njulia --project --threads=auto,1 -e 'using ReactantServer; serve(\"node.yaml\"; worker = \"worker1\")'\n\nTo keep this page one runnable script, it starts the same commands from Julia, with their output shown in this session (run without wait would otherwise discard it):\n\nfunction start(code; threads = \"auto,1\")\n    cmd = `$(Base.julia_cmd()) --project=$(Base.active_project()) --threads=$threads -e $code`\n    return run(pipeline(cmd; stdout, stderr); wait = false)\nend\n\nprocesses = [\n    start(\"using ReactantServer; serve(\\\"node.yaml\\\"; worker = \\\"worker0\\\")\"),\n    start(\"using ReactantServer; serve(\\\"node.yaml\\\"; worker = \\\"worker1\\\")\"),\n]\n\nWait until both workers have compiled their models and answer /readyz on their metrics ports:\n\nusing Downloads\n\nfunction wait_ready(url; timeout = 1800)\n    deadline = time() + timeout\n    while time() < deadline\n        any(process_exited, processes) && error(\"a process exited before $url was ready\")\n        r = Downloads.request(url; throw = false)\n        r isa Downloads.Response && r.status == 200 && return true\n        sleep(2)\n    end\n    return error(\"$url was not ready after $timeout seconds\")\nend\n\nwait_ready(\"http://127.0.0.1:9100/readyz\")\nwait_ready(\"http://127.0.0.1:9101/readyz\")","title":"Start two workers"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#Configure-the-gateway","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"A gateway.yml lists the gateway's own listen addresses and the worker endpoints. The gateway learns which models each worker serves by asking the workers, so it needs no model list. metrics_endpoints and worker_names are optional: with them, the gateway's /metrics also carries every worker's series, labeled by worker name.\n\nwrite(\n    \"gateway.yml\", \"\"\"\n    listen:\n      grpc: 127.0.0.1:8001\n      metrics: 127.0.0.1:8002\n    endpoints: [\"127.0.0.1:8080\", \"127.0.0.1:8081\"]\n    metrics_endpoints: [\"127.0.0.1:9100\", \"127.0.0.1:9101\"]\n    worker_names: [worker0, worker1]\n    scheduling:\n      mode: lpt_packing\n      default_replicas: 1      # place each model on one worker\n    \"\"\"\n)\n\nlpt_packing requires every worker to serve the same models with a fifo or edf discipline, and checks both at startup. Multi-GPU Gateway documents the other modes, round_robin and least_outstanding, which have no such preconditions, and every scheduling setting.","title":"Configure the gateway"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#Start-the-gateway","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"The gateway is a process of its own too. serve_gateway loads gateway.yml and serves until stopped; from a shell:\n\njulia --project --threads=auto -e 'using ReactantServerGateway; serve_gateway(\"gateway.yml\")'\n\npush!(processes, start(\"using ReactantServerGateway; serve_gateway(\\\"gateway.yml\\\")\"; threads = \"auto\"))\nwait_ready(\"http://127.0.0.1:8002/readyz\")","title":"Start the gateway"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#Query-through-it","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"Clients address the gateway's port, and it routes each request by model name. The first request a freshly started gateway or worker handles also compiles its request path, which can take longer than the client's default 10 second deadline, so this allows a minute:\n\nusing ReactantServerClient\n\nkserve_init()\nmodels = Dict(name => KServeModel(\"grpc://127.0.0.1:8001\", name; deadline = 60) for name in (\"mlp_a\", \"mlp_b\"))\nx = randn(Random.Xoshiro(3), Float32, 4, 8)\ny_a = InferOutput(\"output\", infer_sync(models[\"mlp_a\"], [InferInput(\"input\", x)]), Float32)\ny_b = InferOutput(\"output\", infer_sync(models[\"mlp_b\"], [InferInput(\"input\", x)]), Float32)\ny_a ≈ first(model(x, params[\"mlp_a\"]...))                    # true","title":"Query through it"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#See-the-placement","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"The gateway reports its placement on /metrics: gateway_model_replicas counts the workers each model is placed on, and gateway_placement_weight gives its share of the model's traffic per worker.\n\nmetrics = String(take!(Downloads.download(\"http://127.0.0.1:8002/metrics\", IOBuffer())))\nforeach(println, filter(startswith(\"gateway_model_replicas\"), split(metrics, \"\\n\")))\n\nAs load changes, the gateway repacks and may move models between workers.","title":"See the placement"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/gateway/#Stop","page":"Front Workers with a Gateway","text":"kserve_shutdown()\nforeach(kill, processes)\nforeach(wait, processes)\n\nMulti-GPU Gateway is the reference for the gateway's configuration, its routing and scheduling modes, giving a busy model more replicas, its runtime control RPCs, and its metrics.","title":"Stop"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/#Container-Releases","page":"Overview","text":"Each published image ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver:X.Y.Z is built from the tagged ReactantServer-vX.Y.Z tree, its committed deploy/Manifest.toml, and the base image and Julia distribution pinned in MODULE.bazel. Each release has a page listing what it ships, what it requires of the host, its digests, and what changed from the release before it.\n\nRelease Published Julia Reactant Accelerators Changes from previous\n0.1.1 2026-10-14 1.12.7 0.2.287 CUDA 13.1.1, driver 590.44.01 or newer ReactantServer packages only\n0.1.0 2026-10-11 1.12.7 0.2.287 CUDA 13.1.1, driver 590.44.01 or newer First release\n\nJulia, the base image, and the compiler and runtime packages move only through bazel run //deploy:relock or a new base image digest, never as a side effect of a release, so consecutive releases often share them. Changes from previous names what moved. A release that changes only ReactantServer's own packages keeps the numerics stack; any other change is a change to revalidate (see Reproducibility & Regulated Use).","title":"Container Releases"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/#Accelerators","page":"Overview","text":"Each release page has a section for every accelerator its image supports, listing what the image bundles for it and what it requires of the host. Released images support CUDA only.","title":"Accelerators"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/#CUDA","page":"Overview","text":"The CUDA that runs a model comes from Reactant_jll, not from the base image. The image ships the Reactant_jll artifact selected for REACTANT_GPU=cuda and the release's REACTANT_GPU_VERSION, which carries ptxas, fatbinary, libdevice and libnccl.so.2 and links cuDNN, cuBLAS, cuBLASLt and NVSHMEM into libReactantExtra.so. A release page's CUDA rows are the versions that artifact was built against. Reactant loads libnvJitLink.so.13, libnvrtc.so.13 and libcupti.so at run time, and those come from the base image's CUDA. The base also carries its own cuDNN and cuBLAS packages, which Reactant does not load.\n\nNVIDIA driver. The base image sets NVIDIA_REQUIRE_CUDA, which the NVIDIA Container Toolkit checks when the container starts, so the host driver must support the base image's CUDA release. The toolkit itself is required as described in Deployment.\n\nGPU. The minimum compute capability is the oldest target the Reactant_jll artifact is built for.","title":"CUDA"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#Project-Philosophy","page":"Philosophy","text":"This document describes who the project is for, what it optimizes for, and where it is headed. It exists to set expectations for users and contributors, and to serve as a reference when evaluating proposed changes.","title":"Project Philosophy"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#Mission","page":"Philosophy","text":"Make serving compiled models elegant: a hackable, Julia-first inference stack that maximizes the economic efficiency of GPU-based inference, built first for small and mid-size labs.\n\nThe efficiency half is concrete. GPU memory is what limits how many models a GPU can serve, so serving infrastructure that wastes memory wastes money. For the foreseeable future that means serving the largest number of models per GPU at a given quality of service, and serving models larger than a GPU's memory on the hardware a lab already has. The elegance half is what makes that power usable by a small team: the whole stack is plain Julia, legible end to end, adoptable off the shelf, and open to being bent toward a workload nobody anticipated. The project's purpose is to give the people who care about both arguments the tools to act on them.","title":"Mission"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#Who-this-is-for","page":"Philosophy","text":"Small and mid-size labs and engineering organizations who need big-tech-level efficiency without big-tech scale. Concretely, this includes:\n\nStartups and small companies serving production ML where each GPU is a meaningful fraction of infrastructure spend.\nScientific computing and research groups operating within bounded compute budgets.\nOn-premise deployments where hardware is already purchased and underutilization is wasted capacity.\nCloud deployments where GPU-hours are the dominant cost line.\nTeams running large language models locally, on hardware they own or rent, next to the rest of their model catalog.\n\nThe common thread is that these organizations measure their bottlenecks, optimize their stacks, and cannot solve serving problems by spending their way out. They benefit from compiler-grade optimization but lack the headcount to build it themselves.\n\nThe audience is engineers and operators who understand what is happening inside their systems. The project gives them tools; it does not abstract those tools beyond recognition or hide the system's behavior behind convenient defaults.\n\nLarger organizations can use it too. There is no reason the project cannot run as a component of a large-scale system, and the control-plane seam exists for exactly that. The difference is in what ships in the box: for small and mid-size labs the project provides streamlined, off-the-shelf deployments; at large scale it provides the seams and leaves the surrounding tooling to the organizations that already build their own.","title":"Who this is for"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#One-system,-three-deployment-scales","page":"Philosophy","text":"The mission is served at three deployment scales, and the project treats each as a first-class use case with its own complete answer:\n\nSmall: one GPU, one process. A single worker is the entire deployment. The fair scheduler shares the GPU across models by weighted deficit and learned cost, batch coalescing turns queue depth into throughput, and the weight cache serves a catalog larger than device memory. The worker speaks the full KServe V2 gRPC surface itself: one Julia process, one YAML file, nothing else to operate.\nMedium: a few GPUs, one extra process. One worker per GPU behind the gateway. In its LPT-packing mode the gateway derives model placement from live measurements, concentrating each model's traffic on few workers so batches actually fill while balancing compute load and weight memory across GPUs. The workers switch to the simpler FIFO discipline and the placement intelligence moves upstream. There is still no external infrastructure to stand up: the gateway is one more Julia process and one more YAML file, and there is no placement file to maintain.\nLarge: bring your own control plane. Workers in explicit mode cede model lifecycle and residency to the operator's own control plane through a small gRPC control surface (status, residency, and policy RPCs), observable through the same KServe readiness APIs and Prometheus metrics the smaller tiers use. The project supplies the seam; the organization supplies the tooling that encodes needs only it can know. The first two tiers are off-the-shelf solutions; this one is deliberately a building block.\n\nThe principle that binds the tiers is non-interference: the project tries to serve these different use cases, but one use case must not crowd out, degrade, or overcomplicate the others. Each tier is strictly additive. A single-GPU deployment never pays for the gateway in dependencies, configuration, or runtime cost; a gateway deployment never requires a control plane; each step up is selected by a configuration value, not a different architecture. A feature that helps one tier is welcome in the form that leaves the other tiers untouched, and suspect in any form that does not.","title":"One system, three deployment scales"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#Local-LLM-serving","page":"Philosophy","text":"Serving large language models on local hardware is a goal of the project. The pressure that drives many models onto one GPU applies equally to one model that does not fit on it: device memory is the binding cost, and the answer in both cases is to treat device memory as a working set over a larger store rather than a permanent home for every weight.\n\nThe direction builds on mechanisms the server already has. The weight cache keeps weights in host RAM and moves them onto the GPU when a model runs, and a bundle can already carry several compiled programs that share one set of weights. From there, the project aims to:\n\nSplit one model into several compiled programs. A model too large for device memory is partitioned into stages, each an ahead-of-time compiled executable holding its own slice of the weights, run in sequence.\nStream weights from storage. Each stage's weights are loaded as the stage is about to run and released after, with the next transfer overlapping the current stage's compute, so the size of a servable model is bounded by storage rather than by host RAM or device memory.\nMove weights directly from storage into device memory. Where the hardware supports it, weights travel from local storage to the GPU without a copy through host memory.\n\nThe machinery specific to generation, such as the state carried between decoding steps, follows the non-interference principle above: it is added in the form that leaves the serving path for every other model untouched.\n\nLocal means the deployments the project already serves: one GPU or a few, operated by the team that uses them. Hosted LLM services with large numbers of concurrent users across many GPUs have different bottlenecks, and engines such as vLLM, TGI, and TensorRT-LLM are purpose-built for them.","title":"Local LLM serving"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#Where-other-tools-fit-better","page":"Philosophy","text":"The project is built for a specific design point. Naming where its edges are helps users choose quickly.\n\nHyperscale platform requirements. Organizations operating at the scale of thousands of GPUs need multi-tenant isolation, complex traffic shaping, and deep integration with bespoke internal platforms. The project supports large deployments through the control-plane seam described above, which lets an organization bring exactly that machinery, and keeps it out of the core, where every smaller deployment would pay for it.\nHosted, high-concurrency LLM services. As described above, the project's LLM focus is local serving.\nA managed service. The project is infrastructure for builders who run their own stack. Teams that would rather not operate one are better served by a managed inference service.\nServing models without converting them. Every model is lowered by Reactant to a device executable (today via StableHLO/XLA). Teams that need to serve PyTorch, TensorFlow, and ONNX models side by side unconverted are better served by Triton or similar.\nEager-mode experimentation. The project serves compiled programs in production. Interactive iteration with dynamic graphs belongs in the training framework.","title":"Where other tools fit better"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#What-the-project-optimizes-for","page":"Philosophy","text":"In approximate priority order:\n\nEconomic efficiency per GPU. Models served per GPU, the largest model a GPU can serve, watts per inference, dollars per million requests. These are the metrics that matter for the target audience.\nPredictable, deterministic resource use. Pre-allocated memory pools, static buffer assignment, no surprise allocations. Predictability matters for regulated deployments and for operators who need to size their hardware confidently.\nCompiler-grade optimization. Whole-program compiler optimization (XLA today), kernel fusion across operations, layout planning. This is the leverage that lets small teams approach big-tech efficiency.\nA hackable, Julia-first stack. The serving path, scheduler, gateway, client, and export tooling are plain Julia. There is no opaque core wrapped in scripting glue: the code a lab reads is the code that runs, so behavior can be inspected with the language's own tools and modified without a second toolchain. Elegance here is not aesthetics; it is the property that makes the system adoptable off the shelf and bendable when the shelf does not fit.\nOperator clarity. Operators should be able to read configuration files, inspect bundles, understand scheduler behavior, and debug failures without reverse-engineering the system. The architecture is legible.\nVerification scope. The codebase is small enough to audit. Dependencies are bounded and stable. The system is suitable for regulated environments without heroic compliance effort.","title":"What the project optimizes for"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#What-the-project-explicitly-does-not-optimize-for","page":"Philosophy","text":"Convenience over efficiency. When a choice trades runtime cost for developer convenience, efficiency wins by default. Users who prefer the opposite tradeoff have other tools.\nMaximum generality. The project is opinionated about its design point. It does not try to be the inference server for every use case.\nCompatibility with all upstream frameworks. Models Reactant can trace and lower work (today via StableHLO/XLA); others do not. The conversion tooling makes this boundary as friendly as possible, but the boundary is real.\nFeatureful APIs. The server's API surface is small on purpose. Each addition is weighed against the cost of the cognitive load it imposes on every user, not just the users who would benefit.","title":"What the project explicitly does not optimize for"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#What-features-will-not-be-considered","page":"Philosophy","text":"Some categories of changes are off the table, regardless of who proposes them. Stating this explicitly is not standoffish; it is honest about the project's identity. Users and potential contributors deserve to know what to expect.\n\nScale-specific features in the shared core. A feature that serves one deployment scale belongs behind the seam that scale already uses (a configuration switch, the gateway, the control-plane RPCs), not in the core where every other deployment carries its weight. A feature useful at the scale of thousands of GPUs but with no benefit at the scale of one to fifty is better implemented in the control plane an organization brings; the project will extend that seam before it will absorb the feature.\n\nFeatures that abstract the project to death. Every layer of abstraction has a cost: cognitive load on users, complexity in the codebase, surface area for bugs, longer onboarding for new contributors. The project will not add layers of abstraction that primarily exist to support uncommon use cases. The cost falls on everyone; the benefit accrues to a few.\n\nFeatures that add significant cognitive load for minimal benefit. Configuration options that require deep understanding to set correctly, APIs that have many ways to express the same thing, behaviors that depend on subtle interactions between settings. If a feature's correct use requires extensive documentation or expertise that most users will not have, the feature is suspect even if it is technically sound.\n\nFeatures driven by users who have not otherwise contributed. This is not a hard rule, and well-argued proposals from any source will be considered on their merits. But proposals from organizations that benefit from the project without contributing to it are weighed against proposals from contributors who have done the work. The project's direction is set by people who care about the same problem, not by feature requests from anonymous bystanders.\n\nFeatures that compromise the core mission for marginal users. Every project gets pulled toward feature accumulation by users with edge cases. The project will resist this. Features that work against the economic efficiency goal will not be added even if individual users would find them convenient. The integrity of the mission is more valuable than any single feature.","title":"What features will not be considered"},{"category":"section","location":"design/philosophy/#What-the-project-will-consider","page":"Philosophy","text":"Proposals are evaluated against the mission, not against the proposer's preferences. A few categories of contributions are particularly welcome:\n\nImprovements to economic efficiency: better memory packing, lower-overhead scheduling, faster startup, smaller binaries.\nLocal LLM serving: partitioning a model into compiled stages, streaming weights from storage, direct storage-to-device transfer, and the generation support built on them.\nNew conversion paths from upstream frameworks to StableHLO, especially for model architectures the project does not yet handle well.\nDocumentation and tooling that make the project easier to evaluate and adopt for the target audience.\nBenchmarks against alternative inference servers on realistic workloads.\nBug reports and fixes, especially for edge cases discovered in production deployments.\nPerformance regressions, especially with reproducers. The project takes performance as a feature, not as an afterthought.\n\nContributions are weighed against the mission. Changes that improve efficiency, reduce cognitive load, or make the project more useful to the target audience are likely to land. Changes that move in the opposite direction are unlikely to, regardless of how well-implemented they are.","title":"What the project will consider"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Scheduling-and-Coalescing","page":"Scheduling","text":"The worker's scheduler decides which model runs next whenever the GPU is free, and how many queued requests one execution should serve. It is a deficit-weighted, cost-aware, coalescing dispatch engine: concurrent requests land on per-model queues, a single dispatch loop runs exactly one GPU execution at a time, same-model requests are merged into one batched execution at a compiled size, and GPU time is shared by relative model weight and a learned per-batch-size cost estimate. It holds the model registry, the backend, the device memory pool, the SchedulerConfig, per-model dispatch state, and the optional weight cache. Submit work with ReactantServer.infer and read observability counters with ReactantServer.scheduler_metrics.\n\nBecause it serializes the GPU, the scheduler is the component that turns a burst of concurrent requests into a steady stream of full batches. This page covers that worker-side engine and the coalescing it performs. Routing a model's requests across several workers, including the gateway scheduling modes (round_robin, least_outstanding, lpt_packing), is the gateway's job; see Multi-GPU Gateway. Where model weights live and how on-demand loading works is covered in On-demand Weights.","title":"Scheduling & Coalescing"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#How-a-request-flows-through-the-worker","page":"Scheduling","text":"Each gRPC request arrives on its own task. The request runs its preprocess hook on that task, then is queued on its model's queue (FIFO, so the front is always the oldest request). The dispatch loop wakes when work is available, selects a model, coalesces that model's queued requests into one execution at a compiled batch size, runs it, and hands each caller back its raw output slice; the caller's task runs the postprocess hook on that slice. The loop runs no model.jl code itself, so user hooks never serialize against the GPU.\n\n       gRPC requests (one task each)\n                 |\n   preprocess hook runs on the caller's task\n                 |\n        per-model queues (FIFO, one per model)\n                 |\n       +---------+---------+\n       |   dispatch loop   |   one GPU execution at a time\n       | select -> coalesce -> run -> slice\n       +---------+---------+\n                 |\n        per-request output slices\n                 |\n   postprocess hook runs on the caller's task\n                 |\n          replies to clients\n\nA request becomes visible to the dispatch loop only after its preprocess has returned, so the loop never executes a request whose hook work has not finished. Because the hooks run on the per-request tasks rather than on the loop, the CPU-side pre/post work of many requests proceeds in parallel and overlaps the single, serialized GPU execution: while the loop runs one model on the GPU, other requests are being pre/post-processed on other threads.\n\nA single Threads.Condition guards all mutable scheduler state (the per-model queues and the EMA/cost fields) and signals the dispatch loop when work arrives. The loop holds the lock only to select and dequeue; the actual execution runs outside the lock, so producers keep enqueuing during GPU work, and the lock is re-taken briefly afterwards to record the dispatch. Only one execution runs at a time by design, which keeps memory and concurrency reasoning simple.","title":"How a request flows through the worker"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#The-dispatch-loop-runs-on-its-own-thread","page":"Scheduling","text":"The supervisor starts each worker with --threads=<compute_threads>,1: a default pool sized to the worker's share of the host for the per-request pre/post tasks, plus one interactive thread. The scheduler pins its GPU dispatch loop to the interactive thread when one exists, so the GPU dispatch is scheduled promptly and is never starved by the hook tasks that saturate the default pool. With no interactive thread the default pool is used; correctness is unaffected, only the overlap.","title":"The dispatch loop runs on its own thread"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Decision-order","page":"Scheduling","text":"Each time the GPU frees up, the scheduler decides in this order:\n\nDispatch a committed meta sub-call first, if one is pending. A meta model's orchestration runs off the dispatch loop and re-enters the scheduler for each sub-call (see Meta Models); those continuations are committed and jump ahead of the discipline scan, at most one per in-flight meta, so a meta already in progress is not stalled behind newly arrived work.\nOtherwise select the next model, according to the configured discipline: fair (deficit-weighted and cost-aware, the default), fifo (oldest queued request), or edf (soonest deadline).\nCoalesce the winner. The selected model's queued requests are taken in FIFO order and packed into the largest compiled batch size that fits, padding a partial batch up to the smallest size when needed and always making forward progress on at least the oldest request. The remainder stays queued for the next round.","title":"Decision order"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Disciplines","page":"Scheduling","text":"The discipline selects which model is dispatched next. Coalescing applies underneath all three: whichever model wins, its queue is coalesced in FIFO order.","title":"Disciplines"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#fair-(default)","page":"Scheduling","text":"Each model has a relative weight that defines its share of compute: share = weight / sum(weights). The scheduler tracks a decaying exponential moving average of how much GPU time each model has recently consumed (decayed to \"now\" with a half-life of ema_halflife_seconds before every read or write), and scores every model with a non-empty queue by how far below its share it is, divided by its estimated cost:\n\npriority = clamp(share - recent_compute_ema, -cap, cap) / effective_cost\n\nA model that has consumed less than its share recently scores higher. Dividing by cost stops an expensive model from blocking cheaper ones on a marginal fairness edge. The clamp (recency_penalty_cap, default 0.25) bounds both lockout and domination: a quiet model cannot run away with the worker, and a saturated one cannot be starved entirely. When every model's recent-compute EMA is zero (quiet or newly loaded models), the deficit equals the share, so those models stay schedulable rather than normalizing to zero.","title":"fair (default)"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#fifo","page":"Scheduling","text":"Under fifo, per-model weights are ignored. The model whose oldest queued request is the oldest overall is served first, with ties broken by model name for determinism. This is the discipline to use when the worker sits behind a gateway that is already doing placement; see \"Which discipline to use\" below.","title":"fifo"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#edf","page":"Scheduling","text":"Under edf (earliest-deadline-first), the model whose most-urgent queued request has the soonest deadline is served first. The deadline comes from the request's remaining-budget timeout; a request with no deadline is treated as least urgent. Ties break by the model's oldest queued request and then by name, so when all queued requests share a deadline (the common case) edf is exactly fifo. It diverges only to promote a model carrying a request with less budget left, so a meta's sub-call, which inherits the meta's deadline, is served ahead of fresher regular work. Queues stay arrival-ordered (never reordered), and coalescing still draws FIFO from the front, so a request deep behind the batch size advances over successive dispatches of its model rather than jumping the batch.\n\nedf also sheds work it cannot finish in time. Before GPU work begins, a request whose deadline has already passed is dropped under every discipline; under edf a laxity check is added: a request is also dropped if it cannot finish within this dispatch's learned compute cost, so the GPU is not spent on work that will miss anyway (the classic EDF overload failure mode). Dropped requests receive a DeadlineExceeded error (gRPC DEADLINE_EXCEEDED upstream); only feasible requests run, and when none are feasible the execution is skipped entirely. The cost estimate is learned from prior dispatches and unseeded pairs default small, so the laxity drop is a no-op until a model has run once. Committed meta sub-calls skip the laxity drop (their earlier stages already spent GPU time) but still honor the hard deadline check.\n\nNote that because edf derives urgency solely from the deadline, issuing different per-client deadlines for the same model reorders that model's service and therefore affects fairness across clients; keep deadlines uniform to retain fifo-like fairness.","title":"edf"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Which-discipline-to-use","page":"Scheduling","text":"fair is for workers with no upstream placement authority: a single-GPU worker, or a fleet behind the round-robin gateway, where the worker itself must stop one model from crowding out the rest. Behind a gateway running lpt_packing, the gateway is the fairness and placement authority (see Multi-GPU Gateway), and the worker must run fifo or edf so the two do not fight; fair would double-book GPU time against the gateway's placement decisions. edf is for deadline-sensitive serving, and it degrades gracefully to fifo when clients use uniform deadlines.","title":"Which discipline to use"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Coalescing","page":"Scheduling","text":"Coalescing merges same-model requests into one GPU execution. The selected model's queue is scanned in FIFO order, the requests that fit are concatenated along the batch axis into a single compiled-batch-size dispatch, the execution runs once, and the outputs are sliced back per caller (zeros padding a partial fill are dropped). The remainder stays queued for the next round. The batch sizes themselves come from the bundle's manifest batching spec: a bundle may carry several compiled batch sizes that share one set of weights, and the scheduler picks the executable that matches each coalesced batch without duplicating parameters.","title":"Coalescing"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#When-a-model-can-coalesce","page":"Scheduling","text":"A model coalesces multiple requests only when every executable input and every output carries a batch axis (an all-or-nothing requirement that mirrors the loader's manifest check). Inputs are then concatenated along that axis and outputs split back per request. Otherwise the model serves one request per dispatch. Single unbatched modules (the batch-0 executable) are never coalesced. Inputs without a batch axis are taken from the first request, since they do not vary across the batch.\n\nOnly requests that resolve to the same input-shape variant coalesce together. A model compiled for several input shapes (aspect-ratio variants) keeps one executable set per variant over a single shared set of weights, and different shapes cannot be concatenated along the batch axis. The coalescing window is the leading run of same-variant requests at the front of the queue, which keeps the taken set a contiguous prefix (so it can be removed in one step) and preserves FIFO order. A request whose shape has no compiled executable dispatches alone so that the caller gets the precise \"no compiled program for input shape\" error.","title":"When a model can coalesce"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Sizing-the-batch","page":"Scheduling","text":"The scheduler chooses the largest compiled batch size that the queued rows can fill; a partial fill pads up to the smallest compiled size (the compiled shape may therefore exceed the queued rows, and the dispatch runs padded). Coalescing uses only what is queued now; there is no look-ahead.\n\nA per-model max_batch_size caps how many rows are coalesced into one dispatch. The cap does not change compiled shapes: a partial fill still pads up to the smallest compiled size, and a single request larger than the cap is never split, so it dispatches alone (growing the chosen size to the smallest compiled batch that fits it when necessary). Forward progress is guaranteed: an indivisible front request that exceeds the batch size or the cap still dispatches alone rather than being held back, so the oldest request is always served within a bounded number of rounds. A lone request that already fills the dispatch passes its inputs straight through with no concatenation or padding.","title":"Sizing the batch"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Requests-larger-than-every-compiled-size","page":"Scheduling","text":"A request whose batch exceeds the largest compiled size for its shape variant is split before it is queued. The inputs are cut into pieces of at most the largest compiled size, the pieces are submitted as separate requests so they coalesce with each other and with other traffic exactly as if the client had sent them separately, and the outputs are joined back along each output's batch axis and post-processed once. The whole request's deadline rides along on every piece. This is coalescing run in the other direction: a client that batches 32 against a bundle compiled for [1] gets an answer rather than an error.\n\nSplitting is only sound when the bundle's preprocess hook keeps one client row per executable row (a hook that changes the row count, cropping per image say, breaks the correspondence) and every executable output carries a batch axis to join along. A request that cannot be split runs whole and fails at execution with the shape error, as before. This mechanism is separate from max_batch_size, which caps how many rows coalesce into one dispatch and never splits a request.","title":"Requests larger than every compiled size"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Why-coalescing-is-the-throughput-lever","page":"Scheduling","text":"Packing many requests into one execution amortizes the fixed per-launch overhead and, for a model that had to be loaded on demand, the one-time weight transfer, across every item in the batch, so a full batch costs less per item than the same items run one at a time. Coalescing is therefore the reason a worker can absorb bursts: concurrent requests pile onto a model's queue and the loop converts them into a small number of full dispatches instead of paying launch cost once per request.","title":"Why coalescing is the throughput lever"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Turning-coalescing-off","page":"Scheduling","text":"scheduler.coalescing: false (the default under the regulated profile) makes every dispatch serve exactly one request: the front of the queue, at the smallest compiled size that fits it, padded with zero rows. A request's rows then occupy the same slots of the same program no matter what else is queued, which is what a validated deployment needs; see Reproducibility & Regulated Use. A client's own pre-batched request still runs as one dispatch, and an oversize request is still split into pieces, each of which runs alone.\n\nThe cost is the throughput lever above: concurrent requests to a model are served one dispatch each, and coalescing_discount has no effect. The gateway does not yet know whether its workers coalesce, so under lpt_packing it still concentrates a model's requests on one replica at a time to feed coalescing that no longer happens. A fleet with coalescing off should prefer routing_fill_mode: spread; see Multi-GPU Gateway.","title":"Turning coalescing off"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Cost-learning","page":"Scheduling","text":"The scheduler measures the real GPU time of each execution and refines a per-batch-size cost estimate with an exponential moving average (cost_ema_alpha, default 0.2). The estimate initializes to the first measurement, so it converges immediately rather than drifting up from zero. It feeds the fair discipline (the cost divisor, so an expensive model does not crowd out cheaper ones on a marginal fairness edge) and the edf laxity check.\n\nTwo things keep the estimate honest:\n\nWarmup seeds it. At startup, under the fair discipline, each compiled size is run once on zero-filled inputs and the measured time becomes the estimate. Failures are non-fatal: the default cost covers any unseeded (model, batch size) pair until the first real dispatch measures it. While every candidate is unseeded they all share the default, so the deficit term decides. When the weight cache is enabled with auto-sizing, the startup isolation probe doubles as this warmup.\nWeight load time is excluded. The measured compute_time covers only the model execution, not the on-demand weight load that preceded it, so the estimate reflects steady-state (resident) execution rather than cold-call latency.\n\nCoalescing is also priced fairly: a coalescing_discount (default 0.10) is applied to the effective cost of a coalesced batch, so a larger dispatch is not penalized for being larger when it competes against an unbatched one.","title":"Cost learning"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Per-model-configuration","page":"Scheduling","text":"Most models need no tuning: with the default fair discipline, all-default weights yield uniform shares. When you want to change that, per-model overrides live under scheduler.models in the node file, which builds the ModelSchedConfig entries:\n\nglobal:\n  scheduler:\n    models:\n      resnet50:\n        weight: 2.0           # relative compute share under fair (default 1.0)\n        max_batch_size: 8     # cap on rows coalesced per dispatch (default uncapped)\n\nweight sets the model's fair share. residency is also settable per model (the initial residency floor); see On-demand Weights for what the floors mean. max_batch_size caps coalescing rows per dispatch, as described above; it does not change compiled shapes and never splits a request. The full node-file surface, including the global scheduler: block, is in Node Configuration.\n\nmax_queue_depth caps each model's queue independently: a full model rejects new requests without affecting admission for the others, so one backlogged model cannot starve the rest of the worker.","title":"Per-model configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Live-policy-changes","page":"Scheduling","text":"The control plane can adjust scheduling policy on a running worker. ReactantServer.set_policy! updates a model's live weight (consulted by the fair discipline; it raises on an unknown model). ReactantServer.set_residency! moves a model to a target residency floor; the worker accepts it only under model_control_mode: explicit. Both run on the dispatch thread, the sole mutator of residency, and block until applied; control commands are drained ahead of dispatch selection, so a residency transition takes effect before the dispatch that may depend on it. ReactantServer.compact! frees the resident non-pinned weights so the allocator coalesces its free list, then reloads the listed models, defragmenting the device arena; see On-demand Weights.","title":"Live policy changes"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Observability","page":"Scheduling","text":"ReactantServer.scheduler_metrics snapshots per-model state: dispatch count, requests served, rows served, total compute, the current recent-compute EMA, queue depth, queue-wait P50/P99, the histogram of dispatched batch sizes, the effective max batch, and residency. The counters are cumulative over the worker's lifetime, so deltas between polls give rates; dispatch_count counts coalesced batch executions, requests_served counts individual requests (at least one per dispatch, more under coalescing), and rows_served counts batch-axis rows, the effective batch size actually executed.\n\nThe worker exports these as Prometheus series (worker_dispatch_total, worker_compute_seconds_total, worker_queue_depth, worker_queue_wait_seconds, worker_requests_served_total, worker_rows_served_total, worker_model_max_batch_size, and the residency gauges), tagged per model and per GPU, and the node's metrics endpoint aggregates them. See Monitoring for scraping them.\n\nReactantServer.control_status is the control-plane view a gateway reads: the worker's residency mode and discipline, and for each model its weight, queue depth, cumulative serving counters, effective max batch, and the wire-facing batch axis (the input name and 1-based axis a gateway can count items on without decoding a request). A meta is reported as a single unit whose footprint is the sum of its sub-models, so the gateway packs and routes the group as one. See Meta Models and Multi-GPU Gateway.","title":"Observability"},{"category":"section","location":"scheduling/#Configuration-reference","page":"Scheduling","text":"The scheduler: block in the node file maps to SchedulerConfig, and entries under scheduler.models map to ModelSchedConfig. The discipline enum is SchedulingDiscipline. Defaults and YAML keys:\n\nglobal:\n  scheduler:\n    discipline: fair               # fair | fifo | edf (fair is the default)\n    ema_halflife_seconds: 30.0     # fair: half-life of the recent-compute EMA\n    recency_penalty_cap: 0.25      # fair: clamp on the share-vs-EMA deficit\n    coalescing_discount: 0.10      # fair: cost discount applied to coalesced batches\n    cost_ema_alpha: 0.2            # fair/edf: smoothing for the learned per-batch-size cost\n    max_queue_depth: 1024          # per-model queue cap; a full model rejects new requests\n    dispatch_timeout_seconds: 30.0 # per-request execution timeout\n    compaction_interval: 0         # worker-local memory compaction every N on-demand loads\n    coalescing: true               # merge queued requests per dispatch (false under regulated)\n    models: {}                     # per-model overrides (weight, residency, max_batch_size)\n\nEvery field is also settable with an INFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_* environment variable (for example INFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_DISCIPLINE=fifo). See the API Reference for the full SchedulerConfig documentation.","title":"Configuration reference"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/single_gpu/#Serve-on-One-GPU","page":"Serve on One GPU","text":"This page runs the ReactantServer container image on a GPU host, serves a model from a directory of bundles, and queries it. The image is the supported way to deploy: it carries Julia, every package with its precompiled caches, and the CUDA libraries Reactant needs, so the host needs only an NVIDIA driver and a container runtime.","title":"Serve on One GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/single_gpu/#What-you-need","page":"Serve on One GPU","text":"An NVIDIA GPU with its driver installed.\npodman or Docker, with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit so containers can see the GPU. Podman reads GPUs through CDI: generate the spec once with sudo nvidia-ctk cdi generate --output=/etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml.\nA model repository: a directory whose subdirectories are bundles. Export a Model writes one, models/mlp, in a few lines of Julia on any machine; copy models/ to the GPU host.","title":"What you need"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/single_gpu/#Run-the-container","page":"Serve on One GPU","text":"The image is published as ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver, tagged with each release. Mount the model repository and publish the two ports:\n\npodman run -d --name reactantserver \\\n  --device nvidia.com/gpu=0 \\\n  --ipc=host --pids-limit=-1 \\\n  -p 8001:8001 -p 8002:8002 \\\n  -v $PWD/models:/var/lib/reactantserver/models \\\n  -v reactant-compile-cache:/var/cache/reactant-compile \\\n  --stop-timeout 30 \\\n  ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver:0.1\n\nWith Docker, replace --device nvidia.com/gpu=0 with --gpus device=0; the other flags are the same. In order:\n\n--device nvidia.com/gpu=0 grants one GPU; the node runs one worker per GPU it is granted.\n--ipc=host shares the host's /dev/shm, so a client on the host can use shared memory.\n--pids-limit=-1 lifts podman's thread limit, which a worker can cross while loading models.\nThe model repository is mounted writable, because each bundle caches its compiled programs.\nThe reactant-compile-cache volume keeps XLA's autotuning results across restarts.\n--stop-timeout 30 gives the workers time to drain on stop.\n\nContainer settings in Deployment explains each one and the alternatives when --ipc=host is not an option.","title":"Run the container"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/single_gpu/#Wait-for-it-to-be-ready","page":"Serve on One GPU","text":"On its first start the worker compiles every model before it accepts traffic, which takes from seconds for a small model to much longer for a large set. Follow the logs, and poll /readyz until it answers 200:\n\npodman logs -f reactantserver\ncurl -sf http://127.0.0.1:8002/readyz && echo ready\n\nWith one GPU the node runs a single worker on the public ports and no gateway: KServe V2 gRPC on 8001, and /readyz, /healthz, and Prometheus /metrics on 8002.","title":"Wait for it to be ready"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/single_gpu/#Query-it","page":"Serve on One GPU","text":"Any KServe V2 or Triton gRPC client works. From Julia, with ReactantServerClient installed on any machine that can reach the host:\n\nusing ReactantServerClient\n\nkserve_init()\nmlp = KServeModel(\"grpc://127.0.0.1:8001\", \"mlp\")\nx = reshape(Float32[1, 2, 3, 4], 4, 1)               # (features, batch)\ny = InferOutput(\"output\", infer_sync(mlp, [InferInput(\"input\", x)]), Float32)\nkserve_shutdown()\n\nRun Inference covers batched inference over a dataset and the shared-memory transport.","title":"Query it"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/single_gpu/#Configure-the-node","page":"Serve on One GPU","text":"The image runs /etc/reactantserver/node.yaml, a node file that serves everything in the model repository with one worker per visible GPU. To change it, mount your own file over that path:\n\n# node.yaml\nmodel_repo: /var/lib/reactantserver/models\nbase_port: 8080\nmetrics_base_port: 9100\ngpus: auto\n\nglobal:\n  runtime:\n    backend: cuda\n  endpoints:\n    host: 0.0.0.0\n\npodman run ... -v $PWD/node.yaml:/etc/reactantserver/node.yaml:ro ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver:0.1\n\nAny key can also be overridden from the environment with -e, for example -e INFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_DISCIPLINE=fifo. Node Configuration covers every key and its environment variable.","title":"Configure the node"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/single_gpu/#Stop","page":"Serve on One GPU","text":"podman stop reactantserver\n\nThe supervisor drains its workers on SIGTERM before exiting.","title":"Stop"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/single_gpu/#Next","page":"Serve on One GPU","text":"Scale to Many GPUs: the same image and node file on a multi-GPU host.\nDeployment: the image's tags and provenance, every container setting, health checks, and building the image yourself.\nMonitoring: scraping the node's metrics.\nSecurity: what to know before exposing an endpoint.","title":"Next"},{"category":"section","location":"deployment/#Deployment","page":"Deployment","text":"A node runs as a single supervisor process (ReactantServerNode): it detects every visible GPU, spawns one single-GPU worker subprocess per device, multiplexes their logs onto one stream with [name] line prefixes, and restarts children that die. With two or more workers it also runs an embedded gateway on the public ports; with a single worker it binds that worker to the public ports directly. The external interface is the same either way: KServe V2 gRPC on :8001, health and metrics on :8002 (/readyz, /healthz, /metrics), matching Triton's ports.\n\nThe supported deployment is the container image: pull the published image, or build your own with Bazel, and run it under podman or Docker. The image carries Julia, every package, their precompiled caches, and the CUDA userspace Reactant needs, so a host needs only an NVIDIA driver and a container runtime with the NVIDIA Container Toolkit. The supervisor also runs from any Julia environment that holds ReactantServerNode, ReactantServer, and ReactantServerGateway.\n\nThis page is the reference for how a node behaves and how the image is configured. To run one, follow a tutorial: Serve on One GPU and Scale to Many GPUs for the container, or Run a Node for the supervisor from a Julia environment. Scraping the node's metrics is covered under Monitoring, and what to know before exposing an endpoint under Security.","title":"Deployment"},{"category":"section","location":"deployment/#The-node-supervisor","page":"Deployment","text":"The supervisor's job on startup is to turn \"this host plus this node file\" into a concrete set of child processes. It does so in three steps.\n\n1. Detect the devices. The first of these that yields a non-empty answer wins (ReactantServerNode.detect_gpus):\n\nREACTANT_GPUS environment variable: a count (2) or an explicit list (0,2 or GPU UUIDs); 0 means a CPU node.\nthe node file's gpus: key (auto, a count, or a list).\na CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES already set on the host.\nnvidia-smi enumeration.\n/dev/nvidiaN device nodes.\n\nFor a CUDA node that finds no devices, startup fails with guidance (run with --gpus all, set REACTANT_GPUS, or set backend: cpu).\n\n2. Materialize the workers (ReactantServerCore.materialize_node!). With no workers: list, one worker is synthesized per detected device (worker0..workerN-1); an explicit workers: list wins and assigns each worker a device positionally, or by its gpu: key. Either way each worker is pinned to exactly one device through its own CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES, so inside the worker the device is always ordinal 0 and the single-GPU worker code runs unchanged. The materialized node file is written to /run/reactantserver/node.yaml for inspection.\n\nEach worker's compute-thread pool is sized to its share of the host, min(CPU_THREADS ÷ workers, 16) threads, plus one interactive thread for the GPU dispatch loop. This avoids the oversubscription that --threads=auto would cause: with several workers on one node, each auto worker (and its GC and host library pools) would size itself to the whole machine and the workers would fight for every core under load. The cap keeps a very large box from handing any one worker an unhelpfully huge pool. Set REACTANT_WORKER_THREADS to override the computed value; it is used verbatim, bypassing the split and the cap.\n\n3. Decide on the gateway by worker count, in the default all role:\n\nOne worker, no gateway. A lone worker already serves the full KServe V2 API, so the supervisor binds it directly to the public ports (8001/8002) and starts no gateway. No extra process, no extra hop.\nTwo or more workers, workers plus the embedded gateway. Each worker binds base_port + i (and metrics_base_port + i), and the gateway binds the public 8001/8002. The supervisor synthesizes the gateway's worker list (and worker metrics list) from the node file, so the gateway needs no config of its own.\n\nClients and the public ports therefore stay the same as a node grows from one GPU to many, and because gpus: auto (the default) means one worker per visible device, so does the node file. By default every worker serves every model in the repository, so on a multi-worker node each model is replicated on every GPU unless the gateway's lpt_packing mode places it on fewer (Multi-GPU Gateway).\n\nReactantServerNode.supervise runs the supervisor, and ReactantServerNode.main(), the container entrypoint, runs it on the node file named by REACTANT_NODE_FILE. On a host without GPUs, REACTANT_GPUS=0 with backend: cpu makes a CPU node, and REACTANT_CPU_WORKERS sets its worker count. REACTANT_ROLE selects what the supervisor runs: the default all, or only the workers or only the gateway, for splitting a node across machines.","title":"The node supervisor"},{"category":"section","location":"deployment/#The-container-image","page":"Deployment","text":"The image is published as ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver, tagged with each ReactantServer release (:0.1.0, the moving :0.1, and :latest for the newest). Its entrypoint is the node supervisor, so one container runs the whole node: one worker per GPU it is given, plus the embedded gateway when there are two or more. Serve on One GPU walks through running it; the repository's docker-compose.yml is the same deployment as a compose file (REACTANTSERVER_MODELS=/path/to/bundles docker compose up -d).\n\nEach published image carries signed SLSA build provenance naming the commit and workflow run that built it. Check it before deploying (with the GitHub CLI, logged in):\n\ngh attestation verify oci://ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver:latest --repo EnzymeAD/ReactantServer.jl\n\nPin a deployment to a release tag such as :0.1.0, or by digest (ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver@sha256:...) for an image that can never change, rather than :latest, which moves with every release; deploy/README.md describes how the image is published and attested. Container Releases lists what each release ships (Julia, Reactant, CUDA) and the driver it requires.\n\nEvery model compiles on every worker before the gRPC port accepts traffic, so the first start is slow; later starts load the compiled programs from each bundle's cache (runtime.executable_cache in Node Configuration). Watch readiness with curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:8002/readyz or the container's health status, not the container state. On stop, the supervisor drains its workers on SIGTERM; give it room before the runtime escalates to SIGKILL (--stop-timeout, or stop_grace_period in compose).","title":"The container image"},{"category":"section","location":"deployment/#Container-settings","page":"Deployment","text":"Most of these flags exist because a container's defaults are sized for small services, not for a process that maps model weights into shared memory and runs several thread pools per GPU.\n\nGPU access. The host needs the NVIDIA driver and the NVIDIA Container Toolkit. Podman reads GPUs through CDI (--device nvidia.com/gpu=all, or nvidia.com/gpu=0 for one card; generate the spec with nvidia-ctk cdi generate), Docker through --gpus. The supervisor runs one worker per GPU it can see, so the GPUs you grant the container are the topology. The image sets NVIDIA_DRIVER_CAPABILITIES=compute,utility; keep utility, which injects nvidia-smi and NVML for the entrypoint's GPU-reclaim wait and the out-of-pool memory metric. If CDI is unavailable on an older podman, bind the device nodes and driver libraries instead; deploy/README.md lists them.\n\nShared memory (/dev/shm). Two features place data in POSIX shared memory: clients that send tensors through the shared-memory transport (the client's staging pool is 256 MiB by default), and runtime.shared_host_weights, which keeps one host copy of every model's weights for all of a node's workers (see On-demand Weights). A container's own /dev/shm defaults to 64 MiB, far too small for either. There are two ways to provide it:\n\n--ipc=host (compose: ipc: host) shares the host's IPC namespace and its /dev/shm, which is usually sized at half of RAM. This is also what lets a client running on the host, outside the container, use the shared-memory transport.\nA private namespace with an explicit size, --shm-size 32g (compose: shm_size: 32g), or a podman pod created with --share ipc --shm-size so that client containers in the same pod share it. Use this when the host namespace is not yours to share.\n\nWith shared_host_weights on, size /dev/shm above the combined weights of every model the node serves, plus room for the client regions. A tmpfs is allocated lazily, so a generous limit costs nothing until it is used. --shm-size does not apply together with --ipc=host.\n\nProcess and thread limits. Podman limits a container to 2048 processes by default (--pids-limit), and the limit counts threads. Each worker runs a Julia compute pool, XLA's asynchronous runners, and the host libraries' own pools, so a multi-GPU node can cross 2048 while it loads models; the worker then aborts with pthread_create() failed (errno 11, EAGAIN). Pass --pids-limit=-1 (compose: pids_limit: -1) to defer to the host's limits, or a value large enough for your worker count. Docker sets no limit by default unless its daemon is configured with one.\n\nIf you limit the container's CPUs (for example with --cpus), the worker thread pools are still sized from the CPUs Julia detects (The node supervisor). Check the worker compute threads: ... startup line, and set REACTANT_WORKER_THREADS to the number each worker should run.\n\nModel repository. Mount it at /var/lib/reactantserver/models, writable: the compiled executable cache lives inside each bundle, so a read-only mount recompiles every program on every start. In dynamic mode the server watches the mount and hot-loads changes.\n\nPersistent caches. Mount a volume at /var/cache/reactant-compile to keep XLA's autotune results across container recreation; without it every start re-times every GEMM and convolution. Never mount anything over /opt/julia-depot/compiled, which holds the image's precompiled packages.\n\nNode file. The image runs /etc/reactantserver/node.yaml, a copy of config/node.default.yaml (gpus: auto, one worker per visible GPU). Mount your own file over that path to change it; config/node.yaml is the commented template, and Node Configuration covers every key and its INFERENCE_SERVER_* environment override, which can be passed with -e.\n\nHealth. An OCI image cannot carry a healthcheck, so pass one at run time: /usr/local/bin/healthcheck.node.sh reports healthy once the node's /readyz answers. Set the start period longer than a cold start, which compiles every model before /readyz succeeds.","title":"Container settings"},{"category":"section","location":"deployment/#Building-the-image","page":"Deployment","text":"The image is built with Bazel from committed locks: deploy/Manifest.toml pins every Julia package and artifact, and the CUDA base image is pinned by digest, so the same commit always yields the same image. To build it and load it into podman:\n\nbazel run //deploy:image_load    # loads localhost/reactantserver:bazel\nREACTANTSERVER_IMAGE=localhost/reactantserver:bazel docker compose up -d\n\nSee deploy/README.md for the build itself, the precompiled caches, pushing to a registry, and consuming the image from another Bazel module, and deploy/runtime/README.md for the entrypoint and healthcheck scripts.","title":"Building the image"},{"category":"section","location":"deployment/#Outside-the-image","page":"Deployment","text":"The supervisor runs the same way from a Julia environment, as in Run a Node. Start it with julia --handle-signals=no so its own handler runs and it shuts its workers down on SIGTERM. Unlike the image, such an environment resolves its dependencies itself, so it does not get the image's locked versions or its precompiled caches. On a GPU host, select the Reactant CUDA build before the environment precompiles, with REACTANT_GPU=cuda and REACTANT_GPU_VERSION (12.9 or 13.1), and make sure the host provides the CUDA libraries Reactant loads by name (libnvJitLink.so.13, libnvrtc.so.13, libcupti.so). INFERENCE_SERVER_MODEL_DIRS overrides the node file's model repository (colon-separated). Use the container for anything long-running.","title":"Outside the image"},{"category":"section","location":"deployment/#Where-the-children-load-from","page":"Deployment","text":"The supervisor never loads Reactant itself; each worker is a julia subprocess that loads ReactantServer, and the gateway one that loads ReactantServerGateway. Before spawning anything, the supervisor decides where those come from, first match wins:\n\nA workspace named by REACTANT_WORKSPACE_ROOT. Each child runs from its own member project (<root>/packages/ReactantServer, <root>/packages/ReactantServerGateway). The image sets the variable to /opt/reactantserver.\nThe supervisor's own environment, when it lists ReactantServer (and ReactantServerGateway, if a gateway will run) as dependencies. This is the Pkg workflow: add the three packages to one environment and every child runs in it, with JULIA_LOAD_PATH limited to that environment and the standard library so a different copy in your default environment cannot be picked up instead. ReactantServerNode declares the other two as weak dependencies with compat bounds, so Pkg keeps the three on versions that work together.\nA checkout around the active project: --project=packages/ReactantServerNode or --project=. in a clone of this repository.\n\nWhat is needed depends on the role: all needs both packages, workers only ReactantServer, and gateway only ReactantServerGateway. When nothing provides them, the supervisor stops with a ConfigError naming the packages to add, rather than starting children that cannot load and restarting them indefinitely.","title":"Where the children load from"},{"category":"section","location":"deployment/#Beyond-one-host","page":"Deployment","text":"The project does not ship a multi-node control plane. Each node exposes the interfaces one integrates against, and what answers depends on the port:\n\nEach worker's own port serves the full KServe V2 data plane, including ModelInfer, RepositoryIndex, ServerReady, and ModelMetadata, plus the worker control RPCs (ModelControlStatus, SetModelResidency, SetModelPolicy, CompactMemory). A control plane discovers which models a worker serves with RepositoryIndex.\nA multi-worker node's public port is the gateway, which forwards only ModelInfer and the shared-memory RPCs, answers CompactMemory and its own GatewayControlService, and returns UNIMPLEMENTED for the rest (Multi-GPU Gateway). Discovery and metadata go to the workers.\nThe HTTP admin port serves /healthz, /readyz, and Prometheus /metrics.\n\nThe simplest multi-node setup is a standalone gateway pointed at every node's worker endpoints (Front Workers with a Gateway); a larger deployment replaces it with its own control plane speaking the same interfaces.","title":"Beyond one host"},{"category":"section","location":"meta_models/#Meta-Models","page":"Meta Models","text":"warning: Experimental\nMeta models are an experimental feature and their design is subject to change, including the manifest keys, the register_meta_model hook, the call/call.scratch interface, and the scheduling behavior described below. Do not build production pipelines on them. Most logic that looks data-dependent has a fixed-shape form that exports as a plain bundle: the two-stage object detectors this feature was first built for now compile to a single StableHLO program (see Object Detection), and a variable-length result is a fixed-size buffer plus a count trimmed by a model.jl hook (see Variable-length results).\n\nA meta model is a bundle whose model.jl orchestrates other models with ordinary Julia in between, rather than wrapping a single compiled executable. It exists for the logic torch.export cannot trace: data-dependent control flow, loops whose bounds depend on a tensor's contents, or a pipeline where stage two's inputs are computed from stage one's outputs. The server runs the orchestration as a normal inference request, so a meta model is addressable by clients under its own name exactly like any other model.\n\nIf your model is a single traced graph, use a plain bundle with optional pre/post hooks (see Bundles). Reach for a meta model only when the glue between sub-models is real program logic with no fixed-shape form, and keep in mind that the feature is experimental.","title":"Meta Models"},{"category":"section","location":"meta_models/#Anatomy","page":"Meta Models","text":"A meta bundle is a directory with a manifest.yaml and a model.jl, and no compiled artifact or weights of its own. The manifest declares kind: meta, lists the models the orchestration is allowed to call, and must carry client_inputs/client_outputs (there is no executable to infer the client-facing I/O from):\n\nformat_version: \"2.0\"\nname: two_stage\nkind: meta\nmeta:\n  calls: [two_stage_encode, two_stage_score]\nclient_inputs:\n  - {name: TOKENS, dtype: i64, shape: nb, dims: {b: 32}}\nclient_outputs:\n  - {name: SCORES, dtype: f32, shape: nc, dims: {c: 512}}\n\nThe model.jl calls register_meta_model with the orchestration function:\n\nregister_meta_model(\"two_stage\"; run = function (inputs, call)\n    feats = call(\"two_stage_encode\", inputs)     # stage one: encode\n    rows  = rank_rows(feats)                     # ordinary Julia, data-dependent\n    out   = call(\"two_stage_score\", rows)        # stage two: score\n    return [ReactantServer.NamedTensor(\"SCORES\", out[1].data)]\nend)\n\nrun has the form run(inputs::Vector{NamedTensor}, call) -> Vector{NamedTensor}. The injected call(model_name, inputs) invokes another model and returns its outputs as NamedTensors. The callee must appear in meta.calls; calling an undeclared model is a loud error. The loader also rejects a meta bundle whose model.jl calls register_model instead of register_meta_model.","title":"Anatomy"},{"category":"section","location":"meta_models/#Execution:-local,-on-the-request-task,-one-meta-at-a-time","page":"Meta Models","text":"A meta's run(inputs, call) executes on the gRPC request task, off the GPU dispatch loop, exactly like a model's pre/post hooks. Each call(name, inputs) re-enters the local scheduler in-process: the sub-model is dispatched on the loop and its result handed back to the orchestration. There is no gateway round-trip and no serialization between stages; a tensor handed from one stage to the next is passed by reference. The same model.jl runs unchanged on a single worker or in a fleet; the author never writes routing and never needs to know where anything is placed, because a meta only ever runs where its sub-models already live (see Placement, below).\n\nTwo rules shape how a meta shares the GPU with everything else on the worker:\n\nA bounded number of GPU metas at a time. A per-worker gate admits REACTANT_META_CONCURRENCY meta orchestrations at once, default 2. A meta holds its permit across the whole run, including the CPU glue between stages, but it does not hold the GPU during that glue. While a meta computes between stages, the dispatch loop is free and serves other models, so the GPU is not idle during the glue. The default of 2 lets two heavy pipelines overlap (one's glue hides behind another's GPU stage); at 1 two heavy metas serialize, while one holds the gate the other waits and can miss its deadline. Raising it further favors meta completion via more concurrency, but more metas share the serial GPU and the scratch pool (size it for that many concurrent metas). A compute-only meta (empty meta.calls, below) issues no sub-calls and bypasses the gate entirely, so a heavy pure-Julia meta never holds a permit a GPU meta needs.\nIn-flight sub-calls jump the line. Once a meta holds the gate, each of its GPU stages is dispatched ahead of the queued regular work rather than waiting behind it. The number that can cut the line is tied to the gate: each in-flight meta contributes at most one pending sub-call, so the committed set is bounded by REACTANT_META_CONCURRENCY. At a setting of 1 exactly one meta cuts the line and the behavior is fully deterministic; raising the gate lets that many metas cut the line symmetrically (no contention for a single privileged slot), so the one knob sets both how many metas run and how many are prioritized: a single dial for how strongly to favor meta completion.\n\nClient --infer(two_stage)--> Gateway\n  Gateway --routes the whole group to the one worker that holds it--> Worker request task\n    Worker: run(inputs, call), holds a meta-gate permit\n            (up to REACTANT_META_CONCURRENCY, default 2)\n      call(\"two_stage_encode\", input)   committed, jumps the queue --> dispatch loop / GPU\n      feature maps <---------------------------------------------------------------|\n      Julia glue (the GPU is free; other models' requests are dispatched)\n      call(\"two_stage_score\", feats)    by reference, committed --------------> dispatch loop / GPU\n      scores <--------------------------------------------------------------------------|\n      assembled outputs\n  Worker --outputs--> Gateway --outputs--> Client","title":"Execution: local, on the request task, one meta at a time"},{"category":"section","location":"meta_models/#How-it-interacts-with-batch-scheduling","page":"Meta Models","text":"A meta does not hold the GPU for its whole run. Each stage is an ordinary dispatch on the loop, so the GPU is occupied only while a stage actually computes and is free during the meta's CPU glue, when the loop serves other models. The trade is the gate: while a meta holds its permit, a second meta on the same worker waits (its stages cannot start), though the GPU is not wasted because regular models run in the meantime. A stage runs at batch 1 for the meta unless other clients' calls to the same sub-model happen to coalesce with it on the loop.\n\nA meta carries a deadline like any request. It is dropped before it starts if the deadline has already passed or if it cannot take a gate permit in time, so a meta backlog sheds at admission rather than piling up, and no GPU is spent on a meta that will not run. Once a meta is in flight, its continuation sub-calls are committed: they skip the predictive laxity drop, so a stage is never shed merely because it might run long after earlier stages already consumed GPU. They still honor the base check, so a meta whose deadline genuinely elapses mid-pipeline stops at the next stage boundary (raising DeadlineExceeded) instead of running the rest. The net effect is that a meta either runs to completion or is shed at a clean boundary, never silently discarding a stage it already paid for. Under the edf discipline the committed sub-call inherits the meta's deadline, so it is ordered ahead of fresher work (see the discipline notes in Node Configuration).","title":"How it interacts with batch scheduling"},{"category":"section","location":"meta_models/#Placement:-the-group-travels-together","page":"Meta Models","text":"A meta owns no weights or executable of its own, so it is not placed on a GPU by itself. Instead the meta and the sub-models it calls form a group that the gateway places as a single unit. The worker reports the meta to the gateway with a memory footprint equal to the sum of its sub-models' weights and hides the sub-models from discovery, so the gateway packs and routes the group by the meta's own traffic and never sees the individual stages. When the group is placed on a GPU, the sub-models' weights are kept resident there together, so the meta's in-process sub-calls always find their executables and weights local.\n\nThis is the inverse of routing each sub-call independently. The author still writes the orchestration with no knowledge of placement, but the cost of that abstraction is now paid once, at placement time (the group is co-located), rather than per sub-call at request time (a hop to wherever each stage happened to land). The sub-models are internal to the meta: they are never addressable by clients and never appear in the gateway's routing table.","title":"Placement: the group travels together"},{"category":"section","location":"meta_models/#Reuse-buffers-with-call.scratch","page":"Meta Models","text":"A meta's data-dependent glue often builds a large intermediate, for example a feature tensor of tens of megabytes handed from one stage to the next. Allocating that fresh on every request drives GC pressure. The injected call exposes a reuse-buffer allocator for this case:\n\nfeats = call.scratch((256, 512), Float32)      # from the worker's reuse pool (or a plain array)\nfill_features!(feats, inputs)                  # write directly into it\nout = call(\"two_stage_score\", [ReactantServer.NamedTensor(\"FEATS\", feats)])\n\ncall.scratch returns plain Arrays aliasing the pool's backing (or, with no pool configured, fresh heap arrays), so a buffer can be handed straight to the next stage as a NamedTensor by reference; either way the model.jl is identical. The pool is plain memory, local to the worker, and never shared across processes. It exists purely to keep large intermediates off the per-request allocation path; because the sub-call runs in-process, the buffer is handed to the next stage by reference, with no copy. A meta that never calls scratch just allocates normally. The pool defaults to 1 GiB across 16 slots, configured per worker, and the acquired slots are released when the orchestration returns. Concurrent metas share the pool, so size it for that many in-flight metas; the deadline-bounded acquire degrades gracefully if it is ever starved. The client driver exposes the same scratch interface for staging inference inputs (see Client Usage).\n\nRequest every buffer in one call.scratch call (pass a vector of dims => T pairs to get several at once); calling it more than once per request is rejected, and a scratch buffer must reach the sub-call as a contiguous array (a reshape or contiguous prefix is fine).","title":"Reuse buffers with call.scratch"},{"category":"section","location":"meta_models/#Constraints","page":"Meta Models","text":"A meta model may not call another meta model. This is validated at load: a group is exactly one level deep.\nCompute-only metas are allowed. A meta may declare an empty meta.calls and do all its work in Julia, calling no sub-models at all. This is useful for logic that is awkward to express as a traced graph but needs no separate executable. A compute-only meta issues no GPU sub-calls, so it bypasses the meta gate and runs on its request task without taking a permit.\nSub-models are internal. A model named in some meta's meta.calls is reachable only through that meta, never addressed by clients directly, and is hidden from the gateway's discovery and placement; the meta's group carries it.","title":"Constraints"},{"category":"section","location":"meta_models/#See-also","page":"Meta Models","text":"Object Detection for how a two-stage detector, the original motivating case for meta models, exports as one plain bundle instead\nBundles for the plain (non-meta) bundle path and pre/post hooks\nMulti-GPU Gateway for how the gateway routes and places models\nNode Configuration for the scheduling disciplines, including edf\nClient Usage for the client-side scratch interface\nregister_meta_model in the API reference","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#Multi-GPU-Gateway","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"The gateway is a gRPC reverse proxy that puts several workers behind one KServe V2 endpoint. It lives in ReactantServerGateway and does not load Reactant. A node with two or more workers runs one in front of them on the public ports, configured from the node file (Deployment); it can also run on its own over workers you start yourself (Front Workers with a Gateway).\n\nThe gateway reads each request's model name and forwards the request bytes unchanged to a worker that serves the model. On a multi-worker node it also serves the node's merged metrics (Monitoring).","title":"Multi-GPU Gateway"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#What-the-gateway-does","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"Single endpoint: clients reach all workers through one gRPC listener.\nModel-name routing by autodiscovery: the gateway is given a flat list of worker endpoints: in its own gateway.yml and queries each worker's RepositoryIndex RPC (every 10s) to learn which models it currently serves. The discovered model-to-workers routing table is rebuilt and swapped in atomically on each probe, so a control-plane pin/unpin or a worker restart flips routing on the next probe.\nReplica scheduling: a model served by more than one worker is load-balanced across those workers, either uniformly (round_robin, the default) or by packing each model onto a fixed, operator-configured number of GPUs with coalescing-aware routing (lpt_packing); see Scheduling modes below. A third mode, least_outstanding, spreads by live occupancy. Either way, a request fails over to the remaining replicas when a worker returns NotFound or Unavailable.\nReadiness probe: a background loop calls each worker's KServe ServerReady RPC; /readyz is ready when at least one worker reports ready.\nRaw passthrough: the ModelInfer hot path never decodes or re-marshals the protobuf body. The request and response types are Vector{UInt8} end to end (gRPCServer.jl and gRPCClient.jl support raw byte messages natively). To route, the gateway decodes a partial schema declaring only model_name (field 1) and id (field 3). Under lpt_packing a second partial decode reads one named tensor's shape to size the request in items (see the fill quantum below). ProtoBuf seeks past everything else, including every tensor payload; shapes precede the data on the wire, so neither decode touches it. Requests from this repo's client make that property stronger: their inputs ship as raw_input_contents, so every InferInputTensor descriptor stays contiguous in field 5 and the whole payload moves to field 7, letting a prefix scan read every input's name, datatype, and shape. That is a property of these requests, not a protocol guarantee: a Triton client may still inline typed contents, and the gateway keeps handling that.\nSHM broadcast: SystemSharedMemoryRegister / Unregister are fanned out to every worker. POSIX SHM regions are host-local; every worker attaches via shm_open independently. Register succeeds only if all workers succeed (it rolls back partial success); unregister succeeds if any worker does.\nSHM namespace probe: IsSameIPCNamespace is fanned out to every worker and the gateway returns true only if all of them can see the client's region (any worker may service a later ModelInfer). A worker that errors or does not implement the RPC counts as false.\nObservability: structured logs, Prometheus metrics, /healthz, and /readyz on a separate admin HTTP port.","title":"What the gateway does"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#Scheduling-modes","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"The gateway routes each model's requests across its replicas according to scheduling.mode in gateway.yml. round_robin is the predictable default and needs no measurements; least_outstanding spreads by live occupancy; lpt_packing concentrates each model's traffic on few GPUs for deep coalescing batches and is the mode whose placement the gateway manages. Every setting in the block below except mode can also be changed while the gateway runs, through its own gRPC control plane; see Runtime scheduling control.\n\nscheduling:\n  mode: lpt_packing             # round_robin (default) | least_outstanding | lpt_packing\n  work_basis: compute           # compute (default) | items | requests  (what \"least busy\" measures,\n                                #   for least_outstanding and for fill_least; see below)\n  rebalance_compute_seconds: 300 # fleet GPU-seconds consumed that triggers a repack\n  first_rebalance_compute_seconds: 60 # smaller budget for the first repack (0 = use rebalance_compute_seconds)\n  ema_halflife_compute_seconds: 0 # demand-EMA halflife in fleet compute-seconds (0 = track rebalance_compute_seconds)\n  hysteresis: 0.0               # extra improvement required before a model moves workers (0 = move on any gain)\n  default_replicas: 1           # GPUs per model unless overridden below (a number, or \"all\")\n  routing_fill_factor: 1.0      # fill quantum as a multiple of max batch size (lpt_packing only)\n  routing_policy: fill_least    # fill_least (default) | fill_rr  (lpt_packing only)\n  routing_fill_mode: run        # run (default) | spread | inflight  (what the quantum counts;\n                                #   `inflight` can park a model on one GPU, see the warning below)\n  forbid_memory_oversubscription: true # never strand a model on-demand when it could fit resident (default on)\n  compaction_mode: off          # off (default) | lazy | preload  (see On-demand Weights)\n  compaction_interval: 1        # repacks between compactions; 0 disables  (see On-demand Weights)\n  models:\n    big-model:\n      replicas: 2               # this model is placed on 2 distinct GPUs (a number, or \"all\")\n      fill_mode: spread         # optional per-model override of routing_fill_mode\n      fill_factor: 0.5          # optional per-model override of routing_fill_factor","title":"Scheduling modes"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#round_robin","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"round_robin (the default) spreads each model's requests uniformly across its replicas. It is fully predictable from the config file and needs no measurements, at the cost of thin per-worker queues: when every model is on every worker, each worker sees a slice of every model's traffic, so coalesced batches rarely fill.","title":"round_robin"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#least*outstanding-and-work*basis","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"least_outstanding sends each request to the replica carrying the least in-flight work, spreading by live occupancy rather than blindly. Like round_robin it needs no preconditions and does not concentrate traffic, so it favors even spreading over batch coalescing; prefer it over round_robin when a model's replicas have uneven or unpredictable per-request latency, so a slow replica stops attracting new work instead of accumulating a backlog.\n\nscheduling.work_basis decides what \"work\" means. It is fleet-wide: the same setting denominates least_outstanding's per-replica counter and lpt_packing's fill_least per-worker load, so there is one answer to \"what does this fleet call busy\".\n\ncompute (the default) counts in-flight GPU-seconds: each request charges its item count times the model's worker-measured cost per item. This is the only basis that is comparable across models, where 32 items of a cheap model are not 32 items of an expensive one.\nitems counts in-flight items, so a client that pre-batches is weighed by how much it sent. Correct without any cost measurement when a fleet's models cost about the same per item. Weakest under fill_least, which compares one worker's load across all models, so dropping the cost weighting makes a cheap model's items look as expensive as a costly model's.\nrequests counts requests: a raw count for least_outstanding, and the model's measured cost per request for fill_least (which keeps its cross-model cost weighting). Blind to batch size either way, so a pre-batching client defeats the balancing.\n\ncompute and items need to know where each model's batch axis is and what an item costs, which the gateway learns from a ModelControlStatus poll on each health-probe round. Under least_outstanding that is one extra RPC per worker per tick (requests polls nothing); lpt_packing already polls.\n\nUnder lpt_packing the basis is also runtime-mutable, like every other knob in the block: change it with tools/gateway_ctl.jl set-policy work_basis=items and read it back from GetSchedulingStatus, so a fleet can be switched and rolled back without a restart. Under least_outstanding it is fixed at startup, and the startup log line states which basis is in force.","title":"leastoutstanding and workbasis"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#Models-with-no-batch-axis,-and-metas","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"Both work bases degrade to one unit per request rather than misreporting, so no model ever routes on zeros:\n\nA model that declares no batch axis (wire_batch_spec finds none: an unbatched bundle, a model whose inputs are all fixed-shape) counts as one item per request. Its leading dimension names an axis, not a count, so there is nothing to read; under compute it still charges its own measured cost, which for such a model is a cost per request.\nA model the gateway has not polled yet counts as one item until the first successful poll, and under compute borrows the fleet-mean cost per item, so a cold model weighs like an average one instead of looking free and attracting every request.\nA meta (kind: meta) reports no batch axis and counts one row per request, even when its own client_inputs declare a batch axis, so a work-routing gateway charges each request the meta's whole measured cost. That cost is the GPU time of its sub-calls, which are hidden from the gateway, so it is counted once. Under items a meta charges one unit however expensive it is, so fleets that serve metas should use compute.","title":"Models with no batch axis, and metas"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#lpt_packing","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"lpt_packing places each model on a fixed number of distinct GPUs and routes its requests to preserve batch fill. A model's replica count is operator-controlled: default_replicas (default 1, the single-GPU case that coalesces best), overridable per model under scheduling.models.<name>.replicas. Both accept a positive integer or all, which places the model on every ready worker (so default_replicas: all replicates the whole model set across all GPUs without listing each model, and tracks the fleet as workers come and go). The count never grows automatically under load, so a hot model relies on its worker's queue and coalescing rather than fanning out on its own; an operator can promote one at any time with SetModelPlacement, effective at the next repack.\n\nwarning: Replication is the operator's responsibility\nThe gateway does not check that a replica count is feasible for your hardware. Replicating a model charges its full weight footprint to every GPU it lands on, so replicas: 2 (or default_replicas: all) only makes sense when those weights actually fit on each card alongside everything else placed there. If the assigned footprint exceeds a worker's on-demand weight budget, the weights cannot all stay resident and the worker thrashes, loading and evicting weights on nearly every request, which destroys throughput. Size replica counts against your GPU memory. The gateway logs a weight footprint exceeds the worker's on-demand budget warning at each repack when a placement is oversubscribed, so watch for it.\n\nThe packer chooses which GPUs host each model's replicas by balancing two live measurements: compute demand (the gateway-measured arrival rate times the true per-request compute cost the workers report over the control plane) and resident weight footprint against each worker's weight-memory budget, placing models heaviest-first onto the least pressured workers, where pressure is whichever of compute or memory is closer to full. Packing by memory keeps each GPU's resident weight set bounded so evictions stay rare. With forbid_memory_oversubscription enabled (the default), this becomes a hard guarantee: a model is placed only on a worker where its weights still fit whenever some worker can hold it, so a feasible fully-resident placement is never passed over for one that strands a model on-demand (the worker LRU evicting a placed model). The packer falls back to the unconstrained choice, and logs the oversubscription warning above, only when the weights genuinely exceed every worker's budget.\n\nPlacement is kept stable mainly by smoothing the demand signal rather than by a large dead band: the arrival rate and per-request cost are each tracked with an exponential moving average whose halflife is measured in fleet compute-seconds (ema_halflife_compute_seconds, defaulting to one rebalance_compute_seconds interval, so the signal decays about half per repack and ages with how busy the fleet is rather than with wall-clock). hysteresis adds an optional dead band on top: a single-replica model moves only when the move improves its resulting pressure by more than hysteresis (default 0.0, i.e. move on any improvement), because batching depends on traffic staying where the queues are. (max_worker_share is accepted but does not affect a model's GPU count.)\n\nRepacks are driven by accumulated compute, not wall-clock: the gateway polls the workers every probe round and recomputes the placement once the fleet has consumed rebalance_compute_seconds GPU-seconds since the last repack. This compute is cumulative across every GPU: the gateway sums each worker's consumed GPU-seconds, so a fleet of N busy GPUs accrues the budget about N times as fast as one (the accrual rate is the sum of the per-GPU utilizations). The budget is therefore in GPU-work, not real time, and the wall-clock gap between repacks shrinks as the fleet gets busier or larger and stretches out when it is idle (rebalance_compute_seconds divided by the sum of utilizations). The same cumulative clock drives ema_halflife_compute_seconds, so the demand signal also ages in GPU-work rather than wall-clock. The first traffic-driven repack can use a smaller first_rebalance_compute_seconds budget so an early rebalance corrects the cold placement quickly, then later repacks use the larger steady-state budget to limit memory churn (0 means the first repack uses rebalance_compute_seconds like the rest). An idle fleet does not repack until traffic resumes. Gateway-driven compaction of worker memory follows placement changes; see On-demand Weights for compaction_mode and compaction_interval.","title":"lpt_packing"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#The-fill-quantum","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"For a model with more than one replica, the gateway concentrates its requests on one replica at a time so the workers receive deep same-model groupings to coalesce (the coalescing itself stays at the worker; see Scheduling). The fill quantum Q is the model's worker-reported max batch size scaled by routing_fill_factor, and it is denominated in items, not requests. One request carrying a batch of 32 and 32 requests carrying one item each are the same amount of work, and both spend a quantum of 32.\n\nThe gateway does not guess where a request's items are counted. Each worker reports, per model, the name of the wire-facing input carrying the batch axis and that tensor's 1-based axis, taken from the bundle manifest's reserved n/b shape marker. The gateway matches that name against the request's own name-addressed inputs and reads that one dimension, without decoding the payload. This has to come from the manifest: the axis is per tensor and varies between bundles (an image model's whcn puts it last, a tokenized nc puts it first), the first input need not carry one at all (a cross-encoder's query is unbatched while its keys are batched), and it has moved between exports of the same model. A model that declares no batch axis counts one item per request.\n\nThat matters most for a client that batches on its own behalf. If your client already fills requests to the model's max batch size, a request-denominated quantum would hold one replica for 32 batches and your bursts would land on a single GPU; the item denomination makes one such request close the run. Models the worker does not batch (a meta, or a model compiled unbatched, both reporting a max batch of 0 or 1) charge one unit per request, since their leading dimension names an axis rather than a count.\n\nrouting_fill_mode decides what Q counts against:\n\nrun (default) counts items routed to the current replica. A run spends Q items on one replica, then the next run opens elsewhere, so a model's GPUs serve it in turn: batches stay deep and every replica gets an even share (to within one run) at any concurrency. A run also ends when the model has nothing in flight, so a low-rate model rotates per request instead of committing Q in a row, and when a replica falls a whole quantum behind the least-backed-up one, so a slow replica loses its turn rather than accumulating a queue.\nspread equalizes in-flight items across the replica set, so all k GPUs serve the model at once. This is the right mode when your client already batches to the max batch size, because then the worker has nothing left to coalesce and concentration only idles GPUs. It is also what promotion means for a latency-bound model whose client concurrency sits well below its max batch; the cost is coalescing depth, since each GPU now batches roughly 1/k as many requests. Prefer it per model rather than fleet-wide. The exception is a fleet whose workers run with scheduler.coalescing: false (the regulated profile): no worker coalesces, so concentration buys nothing, and spread is the right fleet-wide choice. The gateway does not detect this on its own; set it explicitly.\ninflight: Q counts requests in flight, so a replica keeps receiving the model's traffic until it holds a full quantum at once.\n\nwarning: `inflight` can park a model on one GPU indefinitely\nBecause inflight compares the quantum against in-flight requests, a replica keeps winning until it holds Q of them simultaneously. A model whose in-flight concurrency stays between 2 and Q and never drains to zero is therefore served by one replica for the life of the process, and its other replicas receive no traffic at all. This is not a warm-up transient: with max_batch = 32 and a client holding 4 requests in flight, the second GPU receives zero requests, forever. Closed-loop clients (a fixed-size worker pool, a benchmark harness) hit this reliably, so promoting such a model to more GPUs under inflight buys nothing.Diagnose it with gateway_replica_routed_total: a flat series on one replica of a multi-replica model is the signature (gateway_replica_outstanding cannot show it, because a starved replica reads 0 either way). Use run instead.\n\nrouting_fill_factor is the direct trade between the two concerns: a model's share is even to within one quantum, so a smaller factor balances more finely at the cost of splitting more batches, and a larger one commits longer to each replica for deeper batches.\n\nrouting_policy (lpt_packing only) decides which replica each run opens on:\n\nfill_least (default) opens each run on the replica whose GPU currently carries the least in-flight work, measured across all models and denominated by work_basis (in-flight GPU-seconds by default). This is what you want whenever replicas share GPUs with other models: a model's runs open on whichever of its GPUs is least busy rather than always the same one.\nfill_rr rotates the opening replica across the model's set, so successive runs of the same model spread evenly over its GPUs. Deliberately load-blind, which is right only when every model on the fleet costs about the same and no GPU is shared with anything else.\n\nBoth policies are consulted at every run boundary, and exact ties rotate rather than falling back to the worker name, so an idle fleet warms every replica instead of pinning to the lowest-named one. Spreading every request without concentrating it at all is the separate least_outstanding scheduling mode above, not a fill mode. A single-replica model is the degenerate case: all its requests go to its one GPU (and still count toward that GPU's load for the fill_least decisions of models that share it).","title":"The fill quantum"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#Preconditions-and-startup","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"lpt_packing has two preconditions, verified at gateway startup: every worker must run the fifo or edf scheduler discipline, not fair (placement decisions move to the gateway, so workers should not re-order against it; see scheduler.discipline in Node Configuration), and every worker must serve the identical model set. Because a worker compiles and warms up every model before its control plane answers, the workers are usually not up when the gateway starts, so the gateway waits for all of them before serving rather than failing, logging which workers are still pending. Under the node supervisor (the embedded gateway) this wait is enabled automatically; a standalone gateway waits 60 seconds by default, which covers a worker's first control-plane call after it reports ready (that call compiles the worker's gRPC request path). Set REACTANT_GATEWAY_STARTUP_WAIT_SECONDS to a number of seconds, inf to wait indefinitely, or 0 to check once and fail fast. Each poll is watchdog-bounded, and if the gRPC client stack wedges during the long warmup (a known libcurl failure mode) the gateway exits so the supervisor restarts it with a fresh stack; this self-heals and you may see one such restart before it serves. Once all workers are up, a wrong discipline or differing model set is a hard error.\n\nRuntime drift after startup degrades gracefully: a model temporarily missing from some workers is routed uniformly over its actual replicas with a warning until the fleet converges, and a worker that drops out is excluded from placement, its traffic failing over to the remaining replicas.","title":"Preconditions and startup"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#Observability","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"The placement is observable: gateway_model_replicas reports each model's replica count, gateway_placement_weight reports its per-worker weight, gateway_replica_outstanding reports the in-flight items per replica (summed batch sizes, matching the quantum's denomination; equal to a request count when every request carries one item), gateway_replica_routed_total counts the requests routed to each replica since start (the series that shows whether every replica is actually being used), gateway_model_fill_quantum reports each model's effective quantum, gateway_repacks_total counts repacks by what triggered them, and gateway_model_utilization reports each model's estimated demand in GPU-seconds per second.\n\ngateway_worker_inflight_work reports the per-worker load the router actually compares when it picks a replica, so a routing decision can be explained after the fact. It is exported by both work-routing mechanisms (fill_least under lpt_packing, and the least_outstanding mode) and carries a basis label, because the unit follows work_basis: GPU-seconds under compute, items under items, and a per-request cost under requests. Read it live at scrape time from the scheduler's counters, so it never lags a prober tick, and a worker that no longer hosts anything stops being emitted rather than freezing at its last value. round_robin exports nothing here, since it tracks no load.\n\nThe bundled Grafana dashboards use it: Scheduling & Placement plots the per-worker load, the spread across workers (max - min, which is what fill_least exists to shrink), and the basis in force as the running gateway reports it, and Fleet Overview carries the per-worker load alongside readiness and device memory. For whether the routing actually produced an even result, as opposed to what the router intended, the GPU Work Balance dashboard is the one to read. It is built on rate(worker_compute_seconds_total) per worker, the device-seconds each GPU really consumed, and leads with the spread of that rate across devices (0% is even). Its planned-vs-actual panel is the diagnostic that separates a skewed plan (a repack or a replica count is the lever) from routing not following an even plan. A persistently high spread usually means a model's replicas cannot absorb the imbalance (too few replicas, or a single-replica model pinning one GPU) rather than a misbehaving policy, so read it next to the placement table.","title":"Observability"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#Runtime-scheduling-control","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"The gateway answers its own gRPC service, reactant_control.GatewayControlService, on the same port it serves inference. It exposes the live scheduling state and lets an operator retune it without a restart:\n\nRPC Purpose\nGetSchedulingStatus The mode, every runtime knob, repack bookkeeping, and one row per model and per worker (placement, in-flight and routed counts, resolved fill mode and quantum, measured demand and cost, assigned weights against each worker's budget). Answers in every scheduling mode.\nSetSchedulingPolicy Change any knob in the scheduling: block except mode. update_mask names the fields to apply; one invalid value rejects the whole request and nothing changes.\nSetModelPlacement Set, change, or clear one model's replicas, fill_mode, and fill_factor. The three are independent, so one call can promote a model and change how it uses its GPUs. Effective at the next repack.\nRepack Repack now, bypassing the accumulated-compute budget, optionally waiting a bounded time for it to land.\n\ntools/gateway_ctl.jl is the operator front end, run against the gateway's gRPC port:\n\njulia --project=packages/ReactantServerGateway tools/gateway_ctl.jl --gateway HOST:8001 status\n... set-replicas big-model 2 --fill-mode spread\n... repack --wait 30\n... set-policy hysteresis=0.15 routing_policy=fill_least compaction_interval=4\n\ngrpcurl -plaintext -proto proto_src/reactant_control_v1.proto ... works too, with no Julia installed.\n\nwarning: Runtime changes are not persisted, and there is no authentication\nEvery override lives in memory only: a gateway restart reverts it to gateway.yml plus the environment, and restart is not hypothetical (the gateway deliberately exits on a wedged client stack, expecting its supervisor to bring it back). Treat these RPCs as a way to try a setting or respond to an incident, and write anything you want to keep into gateway.yml. SchedulingPolicy.generation reads 0 while every knob still matches the config file and is bumped by each accepted change, so you can tell a tuned gateway from a fresh one, and each accepted change is logged with its before and after values, which is the only durable record of it. Like the rest of the control plane these RPCs are unauthenticated (see Security), so the gateway's gRPC port must not be exposed outside the trusted network.","title":"Runtime scheduling control"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#What-the-gateway-does-not-do","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"Streaming RPCs.\nThe repository / model-config / statistics / trace / log RPCs in the Triton spec, plus ServerLive, ServerReady, ModelMetadata, and RepositoryIndex for clients. Of the KServe data plane only ModelInfer, the two SHM register/unregister RPCs, and IsSameIPCNamespace are proxied; everything else returns UNIMPLEMENTED. On the control plane the gateway answers ControlService/CompactMemory (fanned out to every worker) and all four GatewayControlService RPCs itself (see Runtime scheduling control); the other ControlService RPCs are worker-only.\nTLS: parsed but not yet enforced; the listener and the worker back-hop are cleartext h2c.\nCUDA shared memory.\nDynamic worker membership: the worker endpoint list is fixed at startup (from gateway.yml or REACTANT_GATEWAY_WORKERS). Which models each worker serves is rediscovered continuously, but adding or removing workers requires a gateway restart.","title":"What the gateway does not do"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#Configuration","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"On a node, the supervisor passes the embedded gateway its worker endpoints and metrics endpoints as REACTANT_GATEWAY_WORKERS and REACTANT_GATEWAY_WORKER_METRICS. Which models each worker serves is never configured; the gateway discovers it.\n\nTo tune the gateway, provide a gateway.yml and point REACTANT_GATEWAY_FILE at it (or leave it at the conventional /etc/reactantserver/gateway.yml, which the supervisor picks up automatically; or, for the embedded gateway, set the REACTANT_GATEWAY_* environment below); it carries the gateway's own settings (listen addresses, message limits, logging, and the scheduling: block above). Settings can also be overridden by environment with the prefix REACTANT_GATEWAY_ and the dotted path uppercased with underscores, for example REACTANT_GATEWAY_LOGGING_LEVEL=debug or REACTANT_GATEWAY_SCHEDULING_MODE=lpt_packing.\n\nnote: A mounted `gateway.yml` owns the endpoint list\nPer-model settings (scheduling.models.<name>) are the one part of the config with no environment equivalent, since the overrides only reach scalar keys, so promoting a single model requires a file. When the supervisor finds one it stops synthesizing the worker lists, because the file is now the authority: the file must therefore carry endpoints: itself, plus metrics_endpoints: and worker_names: if you want the aggregated /metrics and the worker0..N labels the Grafana dashboards join on. The startup wait is not suppressed (it describes the supervisor's own co-launched workers, not gateway configuration), so an lpt_packing node still waits for its workers to finish compiling rather than crash-looping.\n\nFor a standalone deployment, the package also ships probe_worker_ready, the worker container's healthcheck: it resolves a worker's port from the node file and calls its KServe ServerReady on localhost, returning whether it reported ready.","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"gateway/#Operational-notes","page":"Multi-GPU Gateway","text":"The gateway is a single point of failure. Each Julia worker stays reachable on its own KServe V2 gRPC endpoint during a gateway outage, so a client can fall back to addressing a worker directly.\nThe routing table is rebuilt every 10s from each worker's RepositoryIndex and swapped in atomically. If a worker dies, its routes persist until the next successful probe (up to ~10s); in the gap, requests to its models fail over to the remaining replicas (on NotFound or Unavailable), and a model with no live replica returns NotFound. The worker-side readiness probe (ServerReady, same 10s loop) drives /readyz and the gateway_worker_ready metric.\nUnder lpt_packing, the gateway polls the workers on every 10s probe round to refresh routing metadata and accumulate consumed compute, but recomputes the placement only once the fleet has consumed scheduling.rebalance_compute_seconds GPU-seconds (the first repack uses scheduling.first_rebalance_compute_seconds when set). Each repack logs a lpt_packing: repack line with the number of models placed, how many moved workers, how many were held_by_hysteresis, the largest available max_improvement against the hysteresis threshold, and the compute_seconds/wall_seconds since the last repack, useful for watching placement churn and the trigger cadence.\nIf a probe to a worker hangs (times out, rather than failing fast), the gateway drops and recreates that worker's gRPC connection before the next attempt. This recovers from a half-open connection (for example one caught during a worker's brief silent-accept window at startup) that would otherwise be reused and stall every later request to that worker, the per-worker equivalent of a restart, without dropping HTTP/2 multiplexing for healthy workers.\nSuccessful ModelInfer requests are not logged (to keep the hot path quiet); worker errors and a model with no live replica are logged, and per-request latency and gRPC status are exported as Prometheus metrics. Logs contain no tensor data.","title":"Operational notes"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/#Tutorials","page":"Overview","text":"The tutorials follow a model from export to inference: export it into a bundle, serve the bundle from a Julia session or with the container image, then send it requests from a client. Each page stands on its own, so you can start with any of them.","title":"Tutorials"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/#Export","page":"Overview","text":"Export a Model: turn a Lux model into a bundle, the directory of compiled program, weights, and manifest that a server loads.","title":"Export"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/#Serve-from-Julia","page":"Overview","text":"Add the packages to a Julia environment and run the server from a Julia session, on any machine, with or without a GPU. This is the natural way to develop against the server. These pages run on the CPU; a GPU needs one line of configuration changed.\n\nServe a Model: one worker serving a model repository.\nRun a Node: the node supervisor, running one worker per device behind an embedded gateway.\nFront Workers with a Gateway: a standalone gateway over workers you start yourself, placing models with lpt_packing.","title":"Serve from Julia"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/#Serve-with-the-Container","page":"Overview","text":"Run the container image on a GPU host. It is the supported deployment: the host needs only an NVIDIA driver and a container runtime, and the image carries Julia, every package precompiled, and the CUDA libraries.\n\nServe on One GPU: run the image over a model repository and query it.\nScale to Many GPUs: one worker per GPU behind the embedded gateway, with models replicated on every GPU or distributed across them.","title":"Serve with the Container"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/#Inference","page":"Overview","text":"Run Inference: send a dataset through ReactantServerClient, with concurrent batched requests and shared memory when the client shares a host with the server. The client works the same against either way of serving.","title":"Inference"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/#End-to-End-Examples","page":"Overview","text":"Real models exported from PyTorch, served, and queried, using the scripts in the repository's examples/ directory. Each runs on the CPU, and serves on a GPU by dropping one flag.\n\nServe an Object Detector: a pretrained Faster R-CNN that finds and labels the objects in a photo.\nServe Transformer Text Models: four HuggingFace BERT-family models, queried with raw text, for sparse and dense embeddings, reranking, and sentiment.\n\nExport, Run Inference, and the Julia serving pages run in CI on Linux and macOS exactly as written. The end-to-end examples run in CI on request, since they download PyTorch and pretrained weights, and the container pages need a GPU.","title":"End-to-End Examples"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/inference/#Run-Inference","page":"Run Inference","text":"ReactantServerClient is a KServe V2 gRPC client with no Reactant or XLA dependency, so it installs quickly on any machine that needs to talk to a server. This page runs inference over a whole dataset: the client stages inputs through a buffer pool, sends many requests concurrently, and uses shared memory instead of the network when it shares a host with the server.","title":"Run Inference"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/inference/#Install","page":"Run Inference","text":"A client machine needs only the client:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add(\"ReactantServerClient\")\n\nThis page also starts a server to query, so it adds the server, the export tooling, and Lux too:\n\nPkg.add([\"ReactantServer\", \"ReactantServerExport\", \"Lux\"])","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/inference/#Setup:-a-server","page":"Run Inference","text":"The client talks to any server, whichever way it was started. To keep this page self-contained, it exports the MLP from Export a Model to a Bundle and serves it from Julia as in Serve a Model. It takes 4 features and returns 3, and is compiled for batches of 1 and 8. If you already run the container from Serve on One GPU with this bundle, skip this step: it listens on the same address.\n\nusing Lux, Random, ReactantServerExport, ReactantServer\n\nmodel = Lux.Chain(Lux.Dense(4 => 8, tanh), Lux.Dense(8 => 3))\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.Xoshiro(0), model)\nexport_bundle(\n    :lux, model, ps, st, randn(Float32, 4, 1);\n    dir = joinpath(\"models\", \"mlp\"), name = \"mlp\", batch_sizes = [1, 8]\n)\nwrite(\n    \"node.yaml\", \"\"\"\n    model_repo: $(abspath(\"models\"))\n    base_port: 8001\n    metrics_base_port: 8002\n    global:\n      runtime: { backend: cpu }\n      endpoints: { host: 127.0.0.1 }\n    workers:\n      - { name: worker0 }\n    \"\"\"\n)\nserver = serve(\"node.yaml\"; blocking = false)","title":"Setup: a server"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/inference/#Describe-your-data-as-an-inference-IO","page":"Run Inference","text":"Batched inference is driven by an AbstractInferenceIO: a type that knows how many items there are, how to write a range of them into request buffers, and what to do with the response. The driver splits the dataset into chunks of at most max_batch_size items and calls back into the IO for each one.\n\nusing ReactantServerClient\n\nstruct FeatureIO <: AbstractInferenceIO\n    inputs::Matrix{Float32}     # (4, items)\n    outputs::Matrix{Float32}    # (3, items), filled in as responses arrive\nend\n\nBase.length(io::FeatureIO) = size(io.inputs, 2)\n\nThe bytes one item adds to a request, summed over every input:\n\nReactantServerClient.item_input_bytes(::FeatureIO) = 4 * sizeof(Float32)\n\ninfer_encode_chunk! writes items r into a buffer from the chunk's staging slot. scratch allocates the named input with the batch axis last, and pool_view is the array to fill:\n\nfunction ReactantServerClient.infer_encode_chunk!(io::FeatureIO, r::UnitRange, slot::PoolSlot)\n    input = scratch(slot, \"input\", (4, length(r)), Float32)\n    pool_view(input) .= view(io.inputs, :, r)\n    return input\nend\n\ninfer_decode_chunk! reads the response for the same items:\n\nfunction ReactantServerClient.infer_decode_chunk!(io::FeatureIO, r::UnitRange, response)\n    io.outputs[:, r] .= InferOutput(\"output\", response, Float32)\n    return nothing\nend","title":"Describe your data as an inference IO"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/inference/#Run-it","page":"Run Inference","text":"kserve_init creates the staging pool. max_batch_size matches the bundle's largest compiled batch, so each request fills one execution:\n\nkserve_init()\nmlp = KServeModel(\"grpc://127.0.0.1:8001\", \"mlp\"; max_batch_size = 8)\n\ndata = randn(Random.Xoshiro(1), Float32, 4, 100)\nio = FeatureIO(data, zeros(Float32, 3, 100))\ninfer_async(mlp, io)\n\ninfer_async sends the chunks concurrently and returns once every response is decoded; infer_sync(mlp, io) sends them one at a time. The results match the model run locally:\n\nmaximum(abs, io.outputs .- first(model(data, ps, st)))      # ≈ 0","title":"Run it"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/inference/#Shared-memory","page":"Run Inference","text":"On its first request to a server, the client asks whether the server can see its shared memory. When it can, as here with both in one process or a server on the same host, inputs travel through POSIX shared memory and only small descriptors cross the network. Otherwise they are sent inline. The shared_memory keyword of KServeModel picks: :auto (the default) probes, :on requires shared memory and fails loudly without it, and :off always sends inline. Shared memory is not supported on macOS yet, so there :auto sends inline and :on is refused. Both paths give the same results:\n\ninline = KServeModel(\"grpc://127.0.0.1:8001\", \"mlp\"; max_batch_size = 8, shared_memory = :off)\nio_inline = FeatureIO(data, zeros(Float32, 3, 100))\ninfer_async(inline, io_inline)\nio_inline.outputs == io.outputs                              # true","title":"Shared memory"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/inference/#Check-an-IO-against-the-model","page":"Run Inference","text":"An IO hard-codes tensor names, dtypes, and shapes, so it can drift from the model it targets. validate_io runs its encode and decode methods once over synthetic data shaped by the model's real spec and reports any mismatch, without sending an inference request. It reads the spec from a running server, or offline from a bundle's manifest:\n\nvalidate_io(mlp, FeatureIO(zeros(Float32, 4, 8), zeros(Float32, 3, 8)))\nvalidate_io(\n    manifest_io_spec(joinpath(\"models\", \"mlp\", \"manifest.yaml\")),\n    FeatureIO(zeros(Float32, 4, 8), zeros(Float32, 3, 8))\n)","title":"Check an IO against the model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/inference/#Stop","page":"Run Inference","text":"Close the client's connections, and stop the server if this page started it:\n\nkserve_shutdown()\nstop!(server)\n\nClient Usage is the reference for the rest: output tensors read back through shared memory, retries when a server sheds load, bounds checks under validate_io, and the lower-level buffer interface.","title":"Stop"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#Object-Detection-(GeneralizedRCNN)","page":"Object Detection","text":"A two-stage object detector (an FPN backbone + RPN + RoIHeads GeneralizedRCNN, such as a torchvision fasterrcnn_resnet50_fpn) is mostly dense tensor math with a data-dependent middle: selecting RPN proposals, running NMS, pooling ROI features with ROIAlign, decoding boxes, and the final per-class NMS. torch.export cannot capture that middle, because the host implementations use findall, variable-length lists, and loops whose trip count depends on the data.\n\nFor inference none of that needs to be variable-shape. The number of proposals handed to the box head is a fixed K (1000 in torchvision), every other intermediate is bounded by a configuration value, and only the values depend on the image. ReactantServerExport therefore traces the whole detector (stage1, the glue, stage2, and the final NMS) into one static StableHLO program and serves it as a plain bundle. The glue lives in ReactantServerExport.Detection; the high-level entry point is export_two_stage_detector.","title":"Object Detection (GeneralizedRCNN)"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#Why-the-glue-has-a-fixed-shape-form","page":"Object Detection","text":"Each data-dependent step is rewritten over fixed-size buffers with a validity mask:\n\nProposal selection. Per FPN level, the top pre_nms_topk anchors by objectness come from a stable sort; a level with fewer anchors is padded to pre_nms_topk with invalid entries. Clipping and the empty-box filter update the mask instead of dropping rows.\nNMS. Greedy NMS in descending-score order is the unique solution of the recurrence keep[i] = valid[i] && no kept j before i overlaps i above the threshold. That recurrence is a DAG in sort order, so iterating it from keep = valid reaches exactly the greedy result. Each iteration is one batched (M x M) mat-vec, one column per NMS group (an FPN level or a class), inside a traced while loop that typically settles in a handful of iterations.\nTop-k with ties. Sorts are stable with an index operand (Ops.sort(...; is_stable=true)), so equal scores keep their input order, as the host implementation's stable sortperm does. Base.sortperm on a traced array is not stable.\nROIAlign. Each box picks its FPN level from its area, and all levels are concatenated so a single gather serves every box. With adaptive sampling (sampling_ratio = 0) the per-box sample grid is ceil(roi / pooled) per axis, which is data-dependent; the program loops over the sample index up to the largest grid in the batch and masks out samples a box does not have.\nAnchors. The anchor grid is built in the program from the cell anchors and two iotas, so a multi-shape bundle carries no per-shape anchor constants.\n\nThe program returns a fixed-size, zero-padded DETECTIONS buffer (detections_per_img rows) plus NUM_DETECTIONS, and the bundle's model.jl trims the buffer to the detections found (see Variable-length results).\n\nWeights stay program arguments: each stage's weights are written to weights.safetensors prefixed stage1. and stage2., and the export refuses a program whose entry arity does not equal inputs plus weights. The programs are small next to their weights (the served detectors' programs are 104 KB to 9 MB against 159 to 402 MB of weights), which is also how a constant-folding regression would show up.","title":"Why the glue has a fixed-shape form"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#Running-the-converter","page":"Object Detection","text":"The converter is tools/convert_to_stablehlo.jl, driven by a YAML config. The torchvision detector is a handler (a special-case builder), shipped at tools/handlers/torchvision_frcnn_detector.jl. Reference it from the handlers: block of your config, keyed by a model name:\n\noutput_root: /docker/reactantserver/models\n\nhandlers:\n  - file: handlers/torchvision_frcnn_detector.jl\n    models: [my_detector]\n    options:\n      weights: DEFAULT      # DEFAULT = pretrained COCO; \"none\" = random; or a .pth path\n      num_classes: 91       # classes INCLUDING background; set when loading a custom head\n      image_size: 640       # canonical square input edge, divisible by 64\n      input_dtype: u8       # client image dtype (u8 | f32)\n      output_cols: 6        # 5 = [box4, score]; 6 = [box4, score, class]\n\nRelative paths (including file: and any option key ending in _dir/_path) resolve against the config file's directory. With weights: DEFAULT the converter builds the pretrained COCO model, so no source artifact is needed; to convert your own trained detector, point weights at a saved state_dict and set num_classes to match its head.\n\nRun it from the repository root, in an environment that has torch, torchvision, torchax, and ReactantServerExport:\n\njulia tools/convert_to_stablehlo.jl <config>.yaml --only my_detector\n\nUse --dry-run to validate the config and handler load without paying torch startup, and --force to rebuild a bundle that already exists. The run emits one bundle, my_detector.","title":"Running the converter"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#What-the-handler-does","page":"Object Detection","text":"The handler builds the torchvision model and wraps backbone + rpn.head (stage1) and box_head + box_predictor (stage2) as two small nn.Modules that torch.export traces directly. It exports each into a scratch directory, reads the programs and weights back with read_stage_bundle, and fuses them:\n\nusing ReactantServerExport\n\ncfg = DetectorConfig(;\n    strides = [4, 8, 16, 32, 64], scales = [0.25, 0.125, 0.0625, 0.03125],\n    cell_anchors = cells,                     # per level, [A, 4] xyxy, read off the live model\n    rpn_weights, roi_weights,                 # box-coder weights\n    pre_nms_topk = 1000, post_nms_topk = 1000, rpn_nms_thresh = 0.7,\n    score_thresh = 0.05, nms_thresh = 0.5, detections_per_img = 100,\n    # torchvision conventions (the defaults are detectron2's)\n    aligned = false, sampling_ratio = 2, bg_first = true, min_size = 1e-2,\n)\nS1 = read_stage_bundle(stage1_dir); S2 = read_stage_bundle(stage2_dir)\nexport_two_stage_detector(\"models/my_detector\"; name = \"my_detector\",\n    input = IOSpec(\"INPUT__0\", UInt8, [640, 640, 3, 1]; letters = ['w', 'h', 'c', 'a']),\n    stage1 = S1.texts, stage1_weights = S1.weights,\n    stage2 = S2.texts[Int[]], stage2_weights = S2.weights, cfg,\n    output_columns = 6)\n\nStage1 takes the image and returns 14 dense tensors: the four ROI-pooling feature maps, the five per-level objectness maps, and the five per-level box deltas. Stage2 takes ROI-pooled features for K proposals ([K, 256, 7, 7] in torch) and returns cls_logits and bbox_deltas. The DetectorConfig fields that differ between frameworks:\n\nField detectron2 (default) torchvision\naligned true (half-pixel ROIAlign offset) false (and malformed ROIs clamp to 1 px)\nsampling_ratio 0 (adaptive) 2\nbg_first false (background is the last class column) true (background is column 0)\nmin_size 0.0 1e-2 (drop sub-pixel final boxes)","title":"What the handler does"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#Options-and-assumptions","page":"Object Detection","text":"Option Default Meaning\nweights DEFAULT DEFAULT = pretrained COCO; none = random; or a state_dict .pth.\nnum_classes 91 Classes including background; set this when loading a custom head.\nimage_size 640 Canonical square input edge, divisible by 64 (FPN p6/pool stride).\ninput_dtype u8 Client image dtype: u8 (/255 then normalized) or f32 (assumed in [0,1]).\noutput_cols 6 Per-detection width: 5 = [box4, score], 6 = [box4, score, class].\ninput_shapes none Optional [W, H] pairs for extra aspect ratios, one weight set.\n\nEach input_shapes edge must be divisible by 64. Every variant is its own program in the same bundle, sharing one weight set, and the server routes each request to the variant matching its input shape. Boxes are clipped to the input's own width and height, which also covers letterboxed inputs.\n\nTwo assumptions are worth calling out:\n\nInput is one RGB image. The client sends an NCHW image ([1, 3, H, W], Julia (W, H, 3, 1)) at a compiled size; stage1 bakes the ImageNet normalization (and the /255 for u8), so the client sends a raw image, resized or letterboxed to a compiled size. The model is not resized at run time.\nClass ids follow torchvision. With output_cols: 6 the emitted class is the torchvision label (1..num_classes-1); background (class 0) is dropped.","title":"Options and assumptions"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#Validating-a-conversion","page":"Object Detection","text":"Compare against a reference as detection sets, not as ordered lists. When two overlapping detections score within about 1e-6 of each other (a saturated softmax, for example 0.9999964 vs 0.9999958), a last-digit difference in the ROI features decides which one NMS keeps. The traced ROIAlign accumulates in Float32, so its features can differ from a Float64 host implementation by a few 1e-6, which is far smaller than the difference between a GPU run (TF32) and any CPU run. Inlining stage1 into the larger program also lets XLA compile it slightly differently, which can reorder near-tied proposals without changing the final detections. A good acceptance check: every reference detection has a served one with the same class, a box within half a pixel, and a score within 1e-4, and the counts match; report any unmatched detection together with its score gap to its nearest neighbour.\n\nThe ReactantServerExport test suite checks the traced glue this way against a host implementation, for both convention sets, on synthetic stage outputs.","title":"Validating a conversion"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#Runnable-example","page":"Object Detection","text":"examples/object_detection/ in the repository exports a torchvision Faster R-CNN pretrained on COCO into one bundle this way, serves it, and draws its detections on an image. Serve an Object Detector walks through running it.","title":"Runnable example"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#Reactant-tracing-pitfalls","page":"Object Detection","text":"These apply to any hand-written traced export:\n\nA returned reshape wrapper is emitted with its parent's shape. reshape(x, 2, 4, 6, 1) as a function result produces an (8, 6) program output, and the server trusts the executable's shape, not the manifest. Materialize outputs with Reactant.ReactantCore.materialize_traced_array.\n@trace while bodies. Loop-carried values must be distinct arrays (copy before the loop), and captured values must be materialized traced arrays, not reshape wrappers. Referencing a type parameter such as T(0.5) inside the body breaks the macro; hoist constants out of the loop.\nmap(1:n) do ... end inside a trace fails; plain for loops work.\nMatrix products. A traced A * B can fall back to generic LinearAlgebra; use Ops.dot_general. Number constructors on traced scalars (Float32(x), Int64(x), sign(x)) are not traceable; use Ops.convert(TracedRArray{Int64, N}, x) and ifelse.\nSorting. Base.sortperm on traced arrays is not stable; use Ops.sort(...; is_stable=true) with an index operand where tie order matters.\nGathers. A[:, idx] needs idx to be a materialized traced Int64 vector.\nPer-shape constants add up. A constant is baked into every shape variant's program; build large ones, such as anchor grids, inside the program from iotas instead.","title":"Reactant tracing pitfalls"},{"category":"section","location":"object_detection/#See-also","page":"Object Detection","text":"Bundles & model.jl for the plain bundle path, the manifest encoding, and the fixed-size buffer + trim pattern\nServe an Object Detector to run the example end to end\nClient Usage for the client library the demo uses\nexport_two_stage_detector, DetectorConfig, and read_stage_bundle in the API reference","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"use_cases/#Use-Cases","page":"Use Cases","text":"Most deployments match one of the three cases below. Find the closest one and set only the settings it names; everything else can stay at its default. The defaults assume nothing about your models:\n\neach GPU worker loads every model it can fit onto its GPU at startup, and serves the rest on demand\nno copy of a model's weights is kept in host RAM (runtime.host_weight_budget: 0), so host memory use stays small, but a model that has to be reloaded onto the GPU reads its weights file from disk\n\nThe settings are described in full on Node Configuration and On-demand Weights.","title":"Use Cases"},{"category":"section","location":"use_cases/#Static:-every-model-fits-on-the-GPU","page":"Use Cases","text":"Every model stays on the GPU from startup onwards. Nothing is swapped, nothing is kept in host RAM, and no weights file is read after startup.\n\nmodel_repo: /var/lib/reactantserver/models\nglobal:\n  runtime:\n    backend: cuda\n\nThat is the whole configuration: the defaults already do this. With several GPUs, every worker serves every model, and the gateway spreads requests across them. If the models fit on the node only when split between GPUs, give each worker its share with models_include.\n\nCheck it: the worker logs no model weights reloaded from disk warnings, and worker_weight_evicts_total stays at 0. If either appears, the models do not all fit: use the hot swapping case.","title":"Static: every model fits on the GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"use_cases/#Hot-swapping:-the-models-do-not-all-fit","page":"Use Cases","text":"The models move on and off the GPU as requests arrive. A reload is fast when the model's weights are in host RAM and much slower when they come from disk, so give host RAM a budget:\n\nmodel_repo: /var/lib/reactantserver/models\nglobal:\n  runtime:\n    backend: cuda\n    host_weight_budget: 64g          # host RAM for weight copies; size it to the swapped models\n    shared_host_weights: true        # with several GPUs: one copy per model for the whole node\n    shared_host_weights_mode: \"660\"\n\nhost_weight_budget is the setting that matters. Size it to hold the weights of the models that get swapped (roughly the sum of their weights.safetensors files), within the RAM the machine can spare. When it is too small, the least recently loaded copies are dropped and those models are read from disk on their next reload.\nshared_host_weights: true makes the budget one pool for the whole node, holding each model once however many GPUs load it. Without it, each worker keeps its own copies and gets an equal share of the budget.\nhost_weight_budget: unlimited keeps every model in host RAM with no cap. Use it only when RAM comfortably holds every model.\n\nCheck it: after warm-up worker_host_weight_hits_total keeps growing and worker_host_weight_misses_total barely moves. A model weights reloaded from disk warning means that model was not in host RAM when it was needed.\n\nCommon mistakes:\n\nLeaving host_weight_budget at 0: every swap reads the disk, and the worker warns about it.\nA budget larger than the free RAM, or than a container's memory limit (shared copies live in /dev/shm, which counts against it): the machine runs out of memory instead of evicting.","title":"Hot swapping: the models do not all fit"},{"category":"section","location":"use_cases/#Mixed:-hot-swapping-with-some-models-pinned","page":"Use Cases","text":"Some models must always answer at full speed, and the rest share what is left of the GPU. Pin the latency-critical models to GPU memory and configure the rest as in the hot swapping case:\n\nmodel_repo: /var/lib/reactantserver/models\nglobal:\n  runtime:\n    backend: cuda\n    host_weight_budget: 64g\n    shared_host_weights: true\n    shared_host_weights_mode: \"660\"\nmodels:                              # pinned to GPU memory on the listed workers\n  detector: [worker0, worker1]\n  classifier: [worker0]\n\nA model in the models: map is loaded at startup and never leaves the GPU on the listed workers. Every other model is swapped as in the hot swapping case.\nresidency: system (under scheduler.models) is the middle ground: the model stays in host RAM permanently, so its reloads are always fast, but it is swapped on the GPU like any other. It counts against host_weight_budget.\n\nCommon mistakes:\n\nPinning so much that no GPU memory is left for swapping. The worker warns at startup (no room for the on-demand cache), and every unpinned model then reloads on every request.\nresidency: system models that add up to more than host_weight_budget. They still load, the worker warns that the budget is overcommitted, and no other model's copy stays in RAM.","title":"Mixed: hot swapping with some models pinned"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/node/#Run-a-Node","page":"Run a Node","text":"A node is one machine running ReactantServer. Serve a Model runs one worker of a node file inside a Julia session; the node supervisor, ReactantServerNode, runs the whole node file instead: it starts one worker process per device, restarts workers that die, and when there are two or more workers it puts an embedded gateway in front of them, so clients always talk to a single endpoint. The container image runs exactly this supervisor.\n\nThis page runs a node with two CPU workers, which takes the same shape as a two-GPU machine: two workers behind the embedded gateway.","title":"Run a Node"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/node/#Install","page":"Run a Node","text":"Add the supervisor together with the worker and gateway packages, plus the client and (for the setup step) the export tooling and Lux:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add([\"ReactantServerNode\", \"ReactantServer\", \"ReactantServerGateway\",\n         \"ReactantServerClient\", \"ReactantServerExport\", \"Lux\"])\n\nThe supervisor itself never loads Reactant. Each worker is a separate julia process that loads ReactantServer, and the gateway one that loads ReactantServerGateway; both run in the supervisor's own environment, which is why all three packages go in the same one. Pkg keeps their versions compatible with each other.","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/node/#Setup:-a-bundle","page":"Run a Node","text":"Export the MLP from Export a Model to a Bundle into models/mlp:\n\nusing Lux, Random, ReactantServerExport\n\nmodel = Lux.Chain(Lux.Dense(4 => 8, tanh), Lux.Dense(8 => 3))\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.Xoshiro(0), model)\nexport_bundle(\n    :lux, model, ps, st, randn(Float32, 4, 1);\n    dir = joinpath(\"models\", \"mlp\"), name = \"mlp\", batch_sizes = [1, 8]\n)","title":"Setup: a bundle"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/node/#Describe-the-node","page":"Run a Node","text":"Without a workers: list, the supervisor creates one worker per detected GPU. Worker i serves gRPC on base_port + i and metrics on metrics_base_port + i; the gateway takes the public ports, 8001 for gRPC and 8002 for health and metrics.\n\nwrite(\n    \"node.yaml\", \"\"\"\n    model_repo: $(abspath(\"models\"))\n    base_port: 8080\n    metrics_base_port: 9100\n    global:\n      runtime:\n        backend: cpu           # cuda on a GPU host\n      endpoints:\n        host: 127.0.0.1\n    \"\"\"\n)\n\nOn a GPU host, that file already scales: the supervisor runs one worker per visible GPU. Without GPUs, two environment variables ask for a CPU node with two workers: REACTANT_GPUS=0 (a CPU node) and REACTANT_CPU_WORKERS=2.","title":"Describe the node"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/node/#Start-the-node","page":"Run a Node","text":"The supervisor is a long-running process of its own, like the container it runs in. From a shell:\n\nREACTANT_GPUS=0 REACTANT_CPU_WORKERS=2 \\\n  julia --project --handle-signals=no -e 'using ReactantServerNode; exit(supervise(\"node.yaml\"))'\n\n--handle-signals=no lets the supervisor handle SIGTERM and Ctrl-C itself, so it stops its workers before exiting. To keep this page one runnable script, it starts the same command from Julia, in this environment, with its output shown in this session (run without wait would otherwise discard it):\n\nsupervise_cmd = `$(Base.julia_cmd()) --project=$(Base.active_project()) --handle-signals=no\n    -e 'using ReactantServerNode; exit(supervise(\"node.yaml\"))'`\nsupervise_cmd = addenv(supervise_cmd, \"REACTANT_GPUS\" => \"0\", \"REACTANT_CPU_WORKERS\" => \"2\")\nnode = run(pipeline(supervise_cmd; stdout, stderr); wait = false)\n\nEvery line the workers and the gateway print comes out on the supervisor's output with a [worker0], [worker1], or [gateway] prefix. Each worker compiles its models before it reports ready, so wait for /readyz to answer 200 on the gateway and on each worker:\n\nusing Downloads\n\nfunction wait_ready(url; timeout = 1800)\n    deadline = time() + timeout\n    while time() < deadline\n        process_exited(node) && error(\"the node exited before $url was ready\")\n        r = Downloads.request(url; throw = false)\n        r isa Downloads.Response && r.status == 200 && return true\n        sleep(2)\n    end\n    return error(\"$url was not ready after $timeout seconds\")\nend\n\nwait_ready(\"http://127.0.0.1:9100/readyz\")      # worker0\nwait_ready(\"http://127.0.0.1:9101/readyz\")      # worker1\nwait_ready(\"http://127.0.0.1:8002/readyz\")      # the gateway","title":"Start the node"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/node/#Query-it","page":"Run a Node","text":"Clients use the gateway's port, 8001, whether the node has one worker or eight. Both workers serve mlp, so the gateway spreads its requests across them. The first request the gateway and a worker handle also compiles their request paths, which can take longer than the client's default 10 second deadline, so this allows a minute:\n\nusing ReactantServerClient\n\nkserve_init()\nmlp = KServeModel(\"grpc://127.0.0.1:8001\", \"mlp\"; max_batch_size = 8, deadline = 60)\nx = randn(Random.Xoshiro(1), Float32, 4, 8)\ny = InferOutput(\"output\", infer_sync(mlp, [InferInput(\"input\", x)]), Float32)\nmaximum(abs, y .- first(model(x, ps, st)))      # ≈ 0\n\nOne scrape of the gateway's /metrics covers the whole node: the gateway's own series plus every worker's, each tagged with a worker label.\n\nmetrics = String(take!(Downloads.download(\"http://127.0.0.1:8002/metrics\", IOBuffer())))\noccursin(\"worker=\\\"worker1\\\"\", metrics)          # true","title":"Query it"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/node/#Stop","page":"Run a Node","text":"SIGTERM, which is what kill sends and what a container runtime sends on stop, makes the supervisor drain its workers and the gateway, then exit with status 0:\n\nkserve_shutdown()\nkill(node)\nwait(node)\nnode.exitcode                                    # 0\n\nDeployment describes how the supervisor detects devices, sizes each worker's thread pool, and where its children load from; Node Configuration covers every key of the node file; and Multi-GPU Gateway covers the gateway's scheduling modes.","title":"Stop"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/export/#Export-a-Model-to-a-Bundle","page":"Export a Model","text":"ReactantServer serves bundles: directories that hold a compiled StableHLO program, its weights, and a manifest describing the tensors. This page builds a small Lux model and exports it into a bundle with ReactantServerExport. Export runs on the CPU and needs no server; the server never loads the export tooling or the model framework, only the bundle it writes.","title":"Export a Model to a Bundle"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/export/#Install","page":"Export a Model","text":"Add the export package and Lux to an environment:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add([\"ReactantServerExport\", \"Lux\"])","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/export/#Build-a-model","page":"Export a Model","text":"Any Reactant-traceable model(x, ps, st) works; Lux is just the convenient way to build one. This MLP maps 4 features to 3. Lux puts the batch on the last axis, so a batch of inputs is a (4, batch) matrix.\n\nusing Lux, Random, ReactantServerExport\n\nmodel = Lux.Chain(Lux.Dense(4 => 8, tanh), Lux.Dense(8 => 3))\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.Xoshiro(0), model)\nexample = randn(Float32, 4, 1)                     # (features, batch)","title":"Build a model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/export/#Export-it","page":"Export a Model","text":"export_bundle(:lux, ...) traces the model once per requested batch size and writes the bundle into dir. The bundle's directory name is the model's name on the server.\n\nexport_bundle(\n    :lux, model, ps, st, example;\n    dir = joinpath(\"models\", \"mlp\"), name = \"mlp\", batch_sizes = [1, 8]\n)\n\nThe bundle is a directory with a manifest.yaml, one compiled program per batch size (model.b1.mlir and model.b8.mlir), and the shared weights.safetensors. The manifest names the input input and the output output (override with input_name and output_name), records their dtypes and shapes, and lists the compiled batch sizes.\n\nA server answers requests of any size up to the largest compiled batch by padding a request to the next compiled size, and splits larger ones. Compiling more sizes trades startup compile time for less padding.","title":"Export it"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/export/#Check-the-bundle","page":"Export a Model","text":"A program must take exactly as many arguments as the manifest declares inputs plus the weights file holds tensors. export_bundle already refuses to write a bundle that disagrees, and bundle_arity_report reads the numbers back from any bundle, including one written elsewhere:\n\nreport = bundle_arity_report(joinpath(\"models\", \"mlp\"))\nreport.servable                                    # true\n\nmodels/ is now a model repository: every subdirectory with a manifest.yaml is a servable model. Either serving path takes it as is: Serve a Model from a Julia session, or Serve on One GPU with the container image. Bundles documents the manifest, custom pre- and postprocessing through a bundle's model.jl, and the multi-input and PyTorch frontends.","title":"Check the bundle"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API Reference","text":"Every exported name that carries a docstring across the five documented packages appears below, collected automatically. ReactantServer re-exports the whole ReactantServerCore substrate, so the shared API (dtypes, protobuf messages, boundary types, manifest, config, codec, shared memory, the staging BufferPool) is documented under its original bindings once. The guide pages give the worked context: Tutorials, Bundles, Node Configuration, Scheduling, On-demand Weights, Multi-GPU Gateway, Client Usage, Meta Models, Object Detection, Transformer Text Models, and Deployment.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Operational-internals","page":"API Reference","text":"A few names the guides and docstrings reach for are deliberately unexported; they are documented here so their docstrings render and the qualified references in the guides are backed by the manual. Reach them from the REPL fully qualified, for example ReactantServer.scheduler_metrics(). They are implementation details and may change without a breaking release.","title":"Operational internals"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServer.register_heartbeat!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"register_heartbeat!(f) -> f\n\nRegister f(event::Symbol, detail) to receive this process's serving heartbeat:\n\n:up, detail::ServerConfig: a server finished bring-up and is accepting traffic\n:request, detail::String: a ModelInfer request for that model completed or failed\n:down, detail::ServerConfig: stop! tore the server down (a blocking serve fires it when run returns)\n:load / :unload, detail::String: the directory watcher loaded (or reloaded) or unloaded that bundle\n\n:request fires on failure too: a failing client is still a client using this server. The callback is fired synchronously on the request task and must not block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.register_heartbeat!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServer.register_meta_model-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"register_meta_model(name; run)\n\nCalled from a meta bundle's model.jl to register the orchestration function. run has the form run(inputs::Vector{NamedTensor}, call) -> Vector{NamedTensor}, where call(model_name, inputs) invokes another model in-process (no gateway hop). The orchestration runs on the request task under the meta gate (a compute-only meta, with no sub-calls, skips it), and each call re-enters the scheduler as a committed request.\n\nMeta models are experimental and their design is subject to change; do not build production pipelines on them. See the Meta Models guide.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.register_meta_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServer.register_model-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"register_model(name; preprocess=identity, postprocess=identity)\n\nCalled from a bundle's model.jl to register custom pre/post-processing. Both hooks receive and return a Vector{NamedTensor}. Omitted hooks default to identity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.register_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServer.register_serve_guard!-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"register_serve_guard!(f) -> f\n\nRegister f(cfg::ServerConfig) to be called before every serve brings a server up. A guard refuses by throwing; the error propagates to the caller of serve unchanged and nothing has been allocated yet. Guards run in registration order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.register_serve_guard!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServer.serve-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"serve(node_path; worker=nothing, backend=ReactantBackend(), blocking=true) -> nothing | RunningServer\n\nRun one worker from the node file at node_path, in this process: load the file, resolve the worker entry, bring up the runtime and its models, and start the gRPC server (the KServe V2 inference API and the worker control service). worker selects which worker entry to serve; it may be omitted when the node file has exactly one worker. When blocking is false the server runs in the background and a RunningServer is returned (stop it with stop!).\n\nTo run every worker in a node file, one process per device, use ReactantServerNode.supervise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.serve"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServer.serve_worker-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"serve_worker(node_path, worker; backend=ReactantBackend(), blocking=true)\n\nConvenience alias for serve that names the worker positionally.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.serve_worker"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServer.stop!-Tuple{ReactantServer.RunningServer}","page":"API Reference","text":"stop!(s::RunningServer)\n\nShut down a server started with serve(...; blocking=false). Stops the model-directory watcher (if running), closes the metrics endpoint (if running) and the gRPC server, halts the scheduler's dispatch loop, and tears down any registered shared-memory regions. Returns nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.stop!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.AbstractInferenceIO","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractInferenceIO\n\nInterface for streaming a dataset through batched inference with infer_async / infer_sync. A concrete subtype implements length(io), item_input_bytes(io), infer_encode_chunk!(io, range, slot) (stage a chunk's inputs into the pool slot and return the input descriptors), and infer_decode_chunk!(io, range, response) (consume the response). The pool owns the staging bytes; the IO must not retain slot references past infer_encode_chunk!.\n\nA subtype may optionally implement output_specs to read its outputs back through shared memory. The default is empty, which keeps every output inline (the response carries raw_output_contents as before). Declaring outputs is transparent to infer_decode_chunk!: the driver reads the shared-memory results back into the response before handing it over, so InferOutput works the same on either transport.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.AbstractInferenceIO"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.KServeModel","page":"API Reference","text":"KServeModel(host, port, model_name; secure=false, max_batch_size=1, deadline=10.0, ...)\nKServeModel(url, model_name; max_batch_size=1, deadline=10.0, ...)\n\nA handle to one model served by a KServe V2 gRPC endpoint (a ReactantServer worker or the gateway). The second form parses a url of the form grpc://host:port (or host:port); grpcs/https selects a secure channel. max_batch_size caps how many items the batched infer_async / infer_sync drivers coalesce per request; deadline is the per-request timeout in seconds. max_send_message_length / max_receive_message_length bound a single gRPC message; each defaults to 512 MiB (matching the worker and gateway), or the REACTANT_CLIENT_GRPC_MAX_SEND_MSG_BYTES / REACTANT_CLIENT_GRPC_MAX_RECV_MSG_BYTES environment variable when set and the kwarg is omitted.\n\nshared_memory controls system shared-memory transport for staged inputs/outputs:\n\n:auto (default): probe the server with IsSameIPCNamespace; use shared memory only if the server confirms it shares this client's IPC namespace. If the server returns false, or does not implement the RPC, fall back to inline transport. There is no silent runtime fallback.\n:on: force shared memory. The server confirming a different namespace is a hard error (fail loudly, no fallback). If the server does not implement IsSameIPCNamespace (e.g. stock Triton), shared memory is still attempted via SystemSharedMemoryRegister; making that work is then the caller's responsibility.\n:off: never use shared memory; always send inline. The probe is not sent.\n\nOn macOS system shared memory is not supported yet: :auto sends inline without probing (and warns once per process), and :on throws an ArgumentError when the model is constructed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.KServeModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.OutputSpec","page":"API Reference","text":"OutputSpec(name, dtype, per_item_dims)\n\nDeclares an expected network output so the driver can read it back through shared memory. name is the output tensor name, dtype its element type, and per_item_dims its Julia column-major shape for a single item, excluding the leading batch dimension. For each chunk the driver reserves sizeof(dtype) * prod(per_item_dims) * items_in_chunk bytes in the staging slot and asks the server to write that output there. See output_specs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.OutputSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.InferInput-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{String, AbstractArray{T}}} where T<:Union{Bool, Float16, Float32, Float64, Int16, Int32, Int64, Int8, UInt16, UInt32, UInt64, UInt8}","page":"API Reference","text":"InferInput(name, array) -> ModelInferRequest.InferInputTensor\n\nBuild a wire input tensor named name from a Julia array, shipping the bytes inline as typed contents, encoded element by element (the one-shot path warns once about this cost; see infer_sync). You pass the array in its natural Julia column-major shape (W, H, …, N); the client reverses it to the network's row-major (N, …, H, W) internally (the bytes are unchanged). Pass a vector of these to the one-shot infer_sync(model, inputs). Variants taking an explicit Julia column-major shape and a typed contents vector are also provided.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.InferInput"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.InferOutput-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{String, ReactantServerClient.inference.ModelInferResponse, Type{T}}} where T<:Union{Bool, Float16, Float32, Float64, Int16, Int32, Int64, Int8, UInt16, UInt32, UInt64, UInt8}","page":"API Reference","text":"InferOutput(name, response, dtype) -> Array\nInferOutput(name, response) -> Array\n\nExtract the output tensor named name from a ModelInferResponse as a Julia array. The wire row-major shape (N, …, H, W) is reshaped (no copy) to Julia column-major (W, …, N). Pass the element type as dtype for a type-stable result; the two-argument form reads the dtype from the response metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.InferOutput"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.infer_async-Tuple{AbstractInferenceModel, AbstractInferenceIO}","page":"API Reference","text":"infer_async(model, io::AbstractInferenceIO)\n\nRun inference over every item in io, staging inputs through the shared-memory [BufferPool] and dispatching chunks concurrently (bounded by the pool's slot count). Each chunk acquires a disjoint slot, so this is safe to call from multiple threads against one model. Results are delivered through io's infer_decode_chunk!. Use infer_sync for serial dispatch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.infer_async"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.infer_sync-Tuple{AbstractInferenceModel, AbstractInferenceIO}","page":"API Reference","text":"infer_sync(model, io::AbstractInferenceIO)\ninfer_sync(model, network_inputs) -> ModelInferResponse\n\nSynchronous inference. The AbstractInferenceIO form drives io one chunk at a time (no concurrency). The second form is a one-shot call: network_inputs is a vector of wire tensors built with InferInput, sent inline in a single ModelInferRequest, and the decoded ModelInferResponse is returned for reading with InferOutput. The one-shot form encodes tensor data element by element as typed contents, so it emits a one-time performance warning (once per session) steering large or repeated workloads to the AbstractInferenceIO drivers, which stage through the buffer pool and ship raw bytes on every transport.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.infer_sync"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.kserve_init-Tuple{}","page":"API Reference","text":"kserve_init(; pool_bytes=DEFAULT_POOL_BYTES, n_slots=DEFAULT_POOL_SLOTS, shm_reprobe_interval=60.0)\n\nInitialize the gRPC subsystem and (re)set the staging-pool parameters. n_slots is the number of fixed-size slots each pool is divided into, which bounds how many chunks can be in flight concurrently against a single pool. shm_reprobe_interval (seconds) is how often the background task re-probes endpoints that fell back to inline transport and restores shared memory if the server has recovered; set it to 0 (or negative) to disable that background recovery.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.kserve_init"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.kserve_shutdown-Tuple{}","page":"API Reference","text":"kserve_shutdown()\n\nTear down the client: unregister and unlink the shared-memory pool from every server it was registered with, drop the cached pools and per-URL routes, and shut the gRPC subsystem down. Pair with kserve_init.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.kserve_shutdown"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.manifest_io_spec-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"manifest_io_spec(path) -> ModelIOSpec\n\nLoad a model's input/output spec from a manifest YAML at path, with no running server. Reuses the server's own wire encoding (encode_model_metadata), so the result matches model_io_spec for the same model. Suitable for an offline precompile or build-time check. The reported shapes are Julia column-major (batch axis last), so they read in the same axis order as the manifest's einsum letters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.manifest_io_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.model_io_spec-Tuple{KServeModel}","page":"API Reference","text":"model_io_spec(model::KServeModel) -> ModelIOSpec\n\nFetch a model's input/output spec from a running server over the ModelMetadata RPC. Throws if the server is unreachable or does not implement ModelMetadata; the call is explicit, so failing loudly is intended. Pair with validate_io or use it to introspect a model's I/O directly. The reported shapes are Julia column-major (batch axis last), matching the order you build arrays in; the row-major KServe wire shape is reversed away by the client.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.model_io_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.output_specs-Tuple{AbstractInferenceIO}","page":"API Reference","text":"output_specs(io) -> Vector{OutputSpec}\n\nOutputs an AbstractInferenceIO declares for shared-memory read-back. Defaults to empty, which keeps every output inline (raw_output_contents) exactly as before: no output declaration means no shared memory, the safe fallback. Returning a non-empty vector opts the IO into shared-memory outputs and explicit-output mode: the request asks the server for exactly these outputs in this order, so every output the IO consumes must be declared. Outputs with data-dependent (dynamic) shapes cannot be sized ahead of time and must stay inline (leave them out, which means returning empty here).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.output_specs"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.validate_io-Tuple{ModelIOSpec, AbstractInferenceIO}","page":"API Reference","text":"validate_io(spec::ModelIOSpec, io::AbstractInferenceIO; items=1)\nvalidate_io(model::KServeModel, io::AbstractInferenceIO; items=1)\n\nDry-run io against a model's true I/O spec without sending an inference request. Runs the user's infer_encode_chunk! and infer_decode_chunk! (see AbstractInferenceIO) once for the first items items against a synthetic, spec-shaped request and response, checking input/output names, dtypes, and shapes and surfacing indexing or shape errors in the user's own code as exceptions.\n\nspec comes from manifest_io_spec (offline) or model_io_spec (online); the KServeModel form fetches it online. The harness runs the user's real methods, so it has side effects (it may write into the io's buffers at positions 1:items); call it on a representative or dummy io. Zeroed synthetic data does not exercise data-dependent branches.\n\nThe dry run runs the user methods inside with_bounds_checks, so indexing written with @infer_inbounds is bounds-checked here even though it elides in normal use. Bare @inbounds is not affected by that context; to catch out-of-bounds in bare-@inbounds code, start Julia with --check-bounds=yes (which Pkg.test does).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.validate_io"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.with_bounds_checks-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"with_bounds_checks(f)\n\nRun f with forced bounds checking enabled for any @infer_inbounds blocks reached during the call (task-local, restored on exit). validate_io uses this so the dry run honors bounds checks even where the IO would normally elide them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.with_bounds_checks"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.scratch-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{PoolSlot, AbstractString, Any, Type{T}}} where T","page":"API Reference","text":"scratch(slot, name, dims, T) -> PoolInferInput\nscratch(slot, [\"name1\" => (dims1, T1), \"name2\" => (dims2, T2), ...]) -> Vector{PoolInferInput}\n\nCarve one input buffer per named spec from the chunk's slot, advancing its cursor so the buffers occupy disjoint, contiguous byte ranges, and return the wire descriptors ready to hand back from infer_encode_chunk!. dims is the Julia column-major shape (per-item dims then the batch axis), or a bare integer for a vector. Get the writable views with pool_view: one descriptor returns one view, and splatting the vector returns all of them to destructure at once.\n\nThe scalar form returns one PoolInferInput; the vector form returns a Vector{PoolInferInput}. infer_encode_chunk! accepts either as its return value, so a single-input IO can return the scalar form directly without wrapping it in a vector.\n\nfunction infer_encode_chunk!(io, r, slot)\n    n = length(r)\n    inputs = scratch(slot, [\"INPUT__0\" => ((4, n), Float32), \"MASK\" => ((n,), Int32)])\n    feats, mask = pool_view(inputs...)\n    for (j, i) in enumerate(r); feats[:, j] .= io.feats[i]; mask[j] = io.labels[i]; end\n    return inputs\nend\n\nThe returned vector is homogeneous (Vector{PoolInferInput}), so it never triggers element promotion the way a literal of differently-typed arrays would. item_input_bytes(io) must equal the per-item sum over these buffers, since the driver sizes the slot from it before infer_encode_chunk! runs. The lower-level path (carve a subslot, pool_view it, build InferInput by hand) is still supported and may be mixed into the returned vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.scratch"},{"category":"macro","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.@infer_inbounds-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"@infer_inbounds expr\n\nLike @inbounds, but the elision is conditional: outside a with_bounds_checks context the wrapped expr runs with bounds checks elided (as @inbounds); inside one it runs with bounds checks. Use it instead of @inbounds in infer_encode_chunk! / infer_decode_chunk! so a validate_io dry run still catches out-of-bounds indexing. Wrap a whole loop or block so the runtime branch is taken once, not per element.\n\nfunction ReactantServerClient.infer_decode_chunk!(io::MyIO, r, response)\n    out = InferOutput(\"OUTPUT__0\", response, Float32)\n    @infer_inbounds for (j, i) in enumerate(r)\n        io.results[i] = collect(out[:, j])\n    end\n    return nothing\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.@infer_inbounds"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.ReactantServerExport","page":"API Reference","text":"ReactantServerExport\n\nOffline tooling that assembles ReactantServer model bundles (manifest.yaml, model[.b{N}].mlir, weights.safetensors) from StableHLO modules and weights, and the Reactant tracing frontend that turns a model + parameters into a bundle. This package is the bundle-format authority for producers; it is not depended on by the server runtime. The format is kept in sync with the server by the round-trip tests.\n\nwrite_bundle/IOSpec are the low-level writer. export_bundle traces a Reactant model (the former LuxExport; it needs only Reactant, not Lux). PyTorch support (export_bundle(:pytorch, ...) and export_bundle(:torchscript, ...)) lives in a package extension that loads when PythonCall is present — using ReactantServerExport, PythonCall enables it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.ReactantServerExport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.IOSpec","page":"API Reference","text":"IOSpec(name, dtype, shape; batch_axis=nothing, letters=nothing)\n\nA tensor's spec. shape is the Julia (column-major) shape, the reverse of the row-major shape a KServe client sends, with a concrete value at the batch axis; batch_axis is the 0-based Julia axis that carries the batch dimension, or nothing. By convention the batch axis is the last one: a client's [batch, max_bytes] wire tensor is IOSpec(name, UInt8, [-1, 1]; batch_axis = 1). The server reads the batch count of each request at this axis, so a spec written in row-major order makes it count the wrong axis and breaks coalescing and request splitting.\n\nThe manifest serialization encodes the shape as an einsum-style letter string (\"chwn\") plus a dims map of letter→size; the batch axis is emitted as n. Non-batch letters are auto-allocated from _AXIS_LETTERS and carry no semantic meaning across tensors.\n\nletters optionally overrides that auto-allocation with explicit non-batch axis letters, one per non-batch axis in shape order (the batch axis is still emitted as n). Pass it to give axes meaningful names, e.g. ['w','h'] for an image input so its manifest reads whn. The reserved markers n/b are rejected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.IOSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.assert_bundle_arity-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"assert_bundle_arity(dir) -> NamedTuple\n\nbundle_arity_report as a refusal: raise unless every compiled module in dir takes exactly declared inputs + serialized weights arguments. Returns the report when it passes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.assert_bundle_arity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.bundle_arity_report-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"bundle_arity_report(dir) -> NamedTuple\n\nRead a written bundle's three numbers and say whether they agree: the inputs its manifest.yaml declares, the tensors its weights.safetensors holds, and the arity of each compiled module.\n\nReturns (; name, n_inputs, n_weights, expected, modules, servable), where modules is one (; module_file, entry_args, servable) per *.mlir. Nothing is thrown for a mismatch, so this can be run across a directory of bundles to triage them; assert_bundle_arity is the same check as a refusal.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.bundle_arity_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.bundle_entry_arity-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"bundle_entry_arity(mlir_path) -> Int\n\nThe number of arguments the compiled program in mlir_path takes, read out of the StableHLO portable artifact.\n\nmodel*.mlir is a serialized vhlo artifact, so parse(MLIR.IR.Module, ...) fails with \"dialect 'vhlo' does not implement the bytecode interface\": it has to be deserialized against a Reactant context first. That is the whole reason this is a function rather than a regex at a call site.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.bundle_entry_arity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.collect_provenance","page":"API Reference","text":"collect_provenance(repo_dir=pwd(); extra=Dict()) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nBest-effort reproducibility provenance for a bundle. Captures the state of the model repo at repo_dir (repo_remote, git_commit, git_tree_sha1, git_branch, git_dirty) plus the export environment (julia_version, reactantserverexport_version) and an exported_at UTC timestamp. Merge the result into export_bundle's provenance; the frontends separately stamp their framework versions (reactant_version, or torch_version/torchax_version).\n\nWhen the work tree is dirty, the uncommitted changes are captured as git_diff (git diff --binary HEAD), so git_commit plus the patch reconstructs the exact exported code (untracked files are not captured). write_bundle extracts git_diff into a working_tree.patch file in the bundle dir rather than embedding it in the manifest.\n\nEvery git field is best-effort: when git is missing, repo_dir is not inside a work tree, or a repo has no origin remote, the affected fields are omitted (with a warning for the non-repo case) and the function never throws. extra entries are merged last and override collected fields.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.collect_provenance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.export_bundle-Tuple{Symbol, Vararg{Any}}","page":"API Reference","text":"export_bundle(frontend::Symbol, args...; kwargs...) -> dir\n\nExport a model to a bundle, naming the frontend explicitly so the call site is unambiguous:\n\nexport_bundle(:lux, model, ps, st, example_input; ...) — a Lux-style model with separate parameters/state (any Reactant-traceable model(x, ps, st); Lux itself is not required).\nexport_bundle(:reactant, f, inputs::Tuple, weights; ...) — any Reactant-traceable function f(inputs..., weights...) with explicit name => array weight pairs.\nexport_bundle(:pytorch, model, example_inputs::Tuple; ...) — a torch.nn.Module; provided by the package extension, so it requires using PythonCall.\n\nEvery frontend takes layout (default :xla), passed to write_bundle: :legacy writes weights that servers older than ReactantServer 0.2 can load.\n\nThe Reactant frontends (:lux, :reactant, and export_two_stage_detector) also take compile_options: the keywords Reactant.@compile accepts, as a NamedTuple (for example compile_options = (; raise = true) to raise kernels such as KernelAbstractions or CUDA.jl code to StableHLO, or (; optimize = :all, no_nan = true)), or a Reactant.CompileOptions. They apply when the model is traced to StableHLO; the exporter sets client and drop_unsupported_attributes itself. Non-default options are recorded under compile_options in the manifest's provenance. The PyTorch frontends lower through JAX and do not take them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.export_bundle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.export_bundle-Tuple{Val{:lux}, Any, Any, Any, AbstractArray}","page":"API Reference","text":"export_bundle(:lux, model, ps, st, example_input; dir, name, input_name=\"input\",\n              output_name=\"output\", batch_sizes=[1], provenance=Dict(), layout=:xla,\n              compile_options=(;)) -> dir\n\nTrace model(x, ps, st) (taking the first return as the output) at each batch size and write a bundle. The batch dimension is the last Julia axis (Lux convention) and the leading network axis. Works for any Reactant-traceable model; Lux itself is not required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.export_bundle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.export_bundle-Tuple{Val{:lux}, Any, Any, Any, Tuple}","page":"API Reference","text":"export_bundle(:lux, model, ps, st, example_inputs::Tuple; dir, name,\n              input_names=nothing, output_names=nothing,\n              output_select = y -> (y isa Tuple ? y : (y,)),\n              input_batch_axes=nothing, output_batch_axes=nothing,\n              client_inputs=nothing, client_outputs=nothing,\n              batch_sizes=[1], provenance=Dict(), layout=:xla, compile_options=(;)) -> dir\n\nMulti-input / multi-output Lux export. client_inputs/client_outputs (each nothing or a Vector{IOSpec}) declare the wire-facing spec when a shipped model.jl postprocess transforms the executable tensors into different client tensors; they are passed through to write_bundle. Traces model(x_tuple, ps, st) where x_tuple is the tuple of array inputs the model's forward expects as its single positional argument. output_select(first(model(...))) maps the raw model output to the ordered tuple of arrays to export; non-array returns (e.g. an Int step count) are dropped by the selector and must not appear in its result. Weights are extracted from ps automatically (same as the single-array method). Per-tensor batch axes default to each array's last Julia axis (natural Julia batch-last, the Lux convention) so every exported input and output is batch-last without the caller hand-specifying it. Override with input_batch_axes/output_batch_axes (1-based Julia axes) when the batch axis is not last (e.g. a (D, N, K+1) output whose batch axis N is the middle one). An entry of nothing in either vector opts that one tensor out of batching (a genuinely unbatched tensor), so single-dispatch or partially-unbatched models can still be exported; note the server requires all executable inputs to be batched or none, so a per-tensor opt-out that leaves a mix of batched and unbatched inputs is rejected at load time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.export_bundle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.export_bundle-Tuple{Val{:reactant}, Any, Tuple, AbstractVector{<:Pair}}","page":"API Reference","text":"export_bundle(:reactant, f, inputs::Tuple, weights; dir, name, input_names=nothing,\n              output_name=\"output\", provenance=Dict(), layout=:xla, compile_options=(;)) -> dir\n\nGeneric single-size export for any Reactant-traceable f(inputs..., weights...). weights is an ordered collection of name => array pairs. Produces one unbatched model.mlir.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.export_bundle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.export_two_stage_detector-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"export_two_stage_detector(dir; name, input, stage1, stage1_weights, stage2, stage2_weights, cfg,\n                          input_shapes=nothing, output_columns=6, output_layout=:cn,\n                          glue_precision=Float64, provenance=Dict(), layout=:xla,\n                          compile_options=(;)) -> dir\n\nExport a two-stage detector as one plain bundle.\n\nStage1, the traced ReactantServerExport.Detection glue, stage2, and the per-class NMS compile to a single StableHLO program per input shape, and a model.jl postprocess hook trims the program's fixed-size detection buffer.\n\ninput: the client image IOSpec, for example IOSpec(\"INPUT__0\", UInt8, [512, 512, 1]; letters = ['w', 'h', 'c']). Its first two Julia axes are the image width and height (the glue clips boxes to them); the reversed array is what stage1 takes. With input_shapes, the variable axes are -1. Batched inputs are not supported (one program handles one image).\nstage1: Dict from variant key to stage1 StableHLO text: Int[] for a single-shape detector, otherwise one entry per input_shapes element. Stage1 returns (feats..., objectness..., deltas...) in torch layout, see ReactantServerExport.Detection.\nstage2: stage2 StableHLO text, (K, C, pooled, pooled) ROI features to (cls_logits, bbox_deltas), with K == cfg.post_nms_topk.\nstage1_weights, stage2_weights: name => array pairs in each program's argument order (read_stage_bundle returns both halves from an existing stage bundle). They are written to weights.safetensors prefixed stage1. and stage2. and stay program ARGUMENTS; the entry arity is checked against them before anything is written.\noutput_columns: 6 for rows [x1, y1, x2, y2, score, class], 5 for [x1, y1, x2, y2, score].\noutput_layout: :cn returns OUTPUT__0 as (columns, N); :cn1 as (columns, N, 1).\nlayout: how weights.safetensors stores the weights, see write_bundle.\ncompile_options: Reactant compile options for tracing the fused program, as for export_bundle.\n\nThe executable returns DETECTIONS ((columns, detections_per_img) in the chosen layout, zero past the count) and NUM_DETECTIONS (Int64, length 1); client_outputs declares the trimmed OUTPUT__0 with a variable detection axis. dir should not already hold a bundle: files from an earlier export with more variants would be left behind.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.export_two_stage_detector"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.read_bundle_weights-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"read_bundle_weights(dir) -> Vector{Pair{String, Array}}\n\nA bundle's weights as name => array in argument_order, each array in the axis order of the program's StableHLO signature (for a torch export, torch order), whichever layout the weights file uses. Reads the file the manifest's weights_file names (default weights.safetensors).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.read_bundle_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.read_stage_bundle-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"read_stage_bundle(dir) -> (; texts, weights, input_shapes)\n\nRead a stage bundle's programs and weights back for fusing.\n\nReturns the pieces export_two_stage_detector takes for one stage: texts maps each input-shape variant key (Int[] for a single-shape bundle, otherwise the manifest input_shapes entry in (input, axis) order of the variable axes) to the StableHLO text of its program, weights is the name => array list in argument_order with each array in the axis order of the program's StableHLO signature (for a torch export, torch order), which is what Ops.hlo_call takes, and input_shapes lists the variant keys in manifest order. Each variant must carry exactly one module (one batch size).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.read_stage_bundle"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.stablehlo_text-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"stablehlo_text(path_or_bytes) -> String\n\nThe textual StableHLO of a serialized portable artifact (a bundle's model*.mlir, given as a file path or its bytes), for Ops.hlo_call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.stablehlo_text"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerExport.write_bundle-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"write_bundle(dir; name, executable_inputs, executable_outputs, modules, weights,\n             client_inputs=nothing, client_outputs=nothing, input_shapes=nothing,\n             provenance=Dict(), layout=:xla) -> dir\n\nWrite a bundle. With input_shapes === nothing, modules is a Dict keyed by batch size of StableHLO modules (or text or bytes); a single entry under key 0 writes model.mlir, otherwise each writes model.b{N}.mlir. weights is an ordered collection of name => array pairs whose order becomes the safetensors argument_order. The per-input batch axis is recorded in each input's IOSpec.batch_axis; the manifest derives batching.batch_dim from there.\n\ninput_shapes (a Vector{Vector{Int}} of compiled input-shape variants) turns on the multi-shape layout: the variable executable-input axes are marked -1 in executable_inputs, and each variant gives the concrete sizes of those axes in (input, axis) order. modules is then keyed by variant (each key a Vector{Int} equal to one input_shapes entry) and maps to that variant's batch-size module dict; the files are written as model.v{i}.*.mlir (i indexing input_shapes), all sharing the single weights.safetensors. The variants must share one set of batch sizes.\n\nclient_inputs/client_outputs (each nothing or a Vector{IOSpec}) declare the wire-facing spec when it differs from the executable spec, for bundles that ship a model.jl whose preprocess/postprocess transform between the two. They are emitted only when given. A variable (non-batch) axis is encoded by passing -1 for that axis size (e.g. the variable detection count of a postprocessed detector). The server requires these only when a model.jl is present, so the caller is responsible for also shipping model.jl into the bundle dir (see the converter handlers).\n\nlayout chooses how weights.safetensors stores each array. :xla (the default) writes the array's memory unchanged under the program's shape (the reversed Julia shape) and marks the file axis_order: xla, so the server reads each tensor with no permute; the manifest's format_version is \"2.1\", which ReactantServer 0.2 and later load and older servers refuse. :legacy writes the Julia shape with the data permuted to row-major and format_version: \"2.0\", for servers older than 0.2 (ReactantServer 0.2 migrates such bundles with runtime.weights_migration).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerExport.write_bundle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServerGateway.probe_worker_ready","page":"API Reference","text":"probe_worker_ready(node_path, worker=nothing) -> Bool\n\nResolve a worker's port from the node file and call its KServe ServerReady on localhost, returning whether it reported ready. Used as the worker container's healthcheck (a Julia replacement for the former Go reactant-healthprobe). worker may be omitted when the node has a single worker.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerGateway.probe_worker_ready"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServerGateway.serve_gateway","page":"API Reference","text":"serve_gateway(gateway_path=nothing; blocking=true) -> nothing | RunningGateway\n\nLoad gateway.yml (listen addresses and the worker endpoint list), build the worker client pool, start the admin HTTP server and the readiness/discovery prober (which probes each endpoint's ServerReady and RepositoryIndex and swaps in the discovered routing table), and serve the KServe gRPC proxy. When blocking is false the server runs in the background and a RunningGateway is returned (stop it with stop!).\n\ngateway_path may be omitted (or nothing) to configure the gateway from defaults and REACTANT_GATEWAY_* environment variables alone; the endpoint list then comes from REACTANT_GATEWAY_WORKERS. The node supervisor uses this to run an embedded gateway without a gateway.yml.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerGateway.serve_gateway"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerNode.supervise-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"supervise(node_path; role=nothing, gateway_path=nothing, sink=stdout, env=ENV,\n          install_signal_handlers=true, kwargs...) -> Int\n\nRun the node: spawn one worker subprocess per visible GPU (and the embedded gateway in the default all-in-one role), multiplex their output onto sink with [name] line prefixes, restart children that die, and block until SIGTERM/SIGINT (or a crash-loop budget breach). Returns the process exit code.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerNode.supervise"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.NO_WEIGHTS_FILE","page":"API Reference","text":"NO_WEIGHTS_FILE\n\nThe WeightsFileId of an entry with no weights file; its token is 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.NO_WEIGHTS_FILE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.TIMEOUT_NS_PARAM","page":"API Reference","text":"TIMEOUT_NS_PARAM\n\nKey of the request-level KV parameter carrying the caller's REMAINING budget in nanoseconds (relative, not an absolute timestamp). Like the shared-memory region parameters, this is an extension to KServe V2 passed through ModelInferRequest.parameters. It is relative so each hop converts it to its own local absolute deadline (time_ns() + budget), which makes it robust to cross-process monotonic-clock differences and lets it ride unchanged through the gateway's raw-byte request forwarding. See deadline_params.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.TIMEOUT_NS_PARAM"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.BatchSizeMode","page":"API Reference","text":"BatchSizeMode\n\nWhich of a bundle's compiled batch sizes the worker loads (the runtime.batch_sizes knob). BATCH_SIZES_ALL (the default) loads every model.b{N}.mlir the manifest declares, so the scheduler can pick the largest size a dispatch can fill. BATCH_SIZES_LARGEST loads only the largest declared size (per input-shape variant) and never reads the others, so every dispatch runs the same compiled program, padded with zero rows when fewer requests are queued. That makes a row's result independent of how many other requests happened to be coalesced with it, at the cost of paying the full batch's compute on every dispatch. Unbatched bundles (model.mlir) are unaffected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.BatchSizeMode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.BatchingSpec","page":"API Reference","text":"BatchingSpec\n\nThe set of batch sizes a bundle was compiled for (compiled_batch_sizes). At inference the request's size along the batch axis must equal one of these; the scheduler coalesces requests up to a compiled size and selects the matching executable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.BatchingSpec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.BufferPool-Tuple{Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"BufferPool(n_bytes; n_slots=8, use_shm=true, name=\"reactant_server_pool\")\n\nAllocate a staging pool of n_bytes divided into n_slots equal slots. slot_bytes is fixed at construction (n_bytes ÷ n_slots), not recomputed per request, so the allocator can hand disjoint slots to concurrent callers. A SHM-backed pool can be registered with a server; an inline pool (use_shm=false) is the fallback transport.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.BufferPool"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.DType","page":"API Reference","text":"DType\n\nCanonical element-type enumeration shared across the server, the single source of truth for dtype translation. The companion maps convert between three representations: the manifest token form (e.g. \"f32\", \"bf16\"), the Julia element type (e.g. Float32, BFloat16), and the KServe V2 wire datatype string (e.g. \"FP32\", \"BF16\").\n\nThe DType to XLA primitive-type mapping deliberately lives in the Reactant backend, not here, so this layer carries no Reactant dependency.\n\nFP8 (F8E5M2, F8E4M3) has no standard KServe wire datatype, so those two variants are intentionally absent from the wire mapping and may appear only on executable-internal tensors, never on client-facing inputs or outputs. Conversions are performed by dtype_from_token, dtype_token, julia_type, dtype_of, dtype_size, kserve_string, and dtype_from_kserve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.DType"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.DeadlineExceeded","page":"API Reference","text":"DeadlineExceeded(model_name)\n\nRaised when a request's deadline has already passed at dispatch admission: the scheduler refuses to begin GPU work that is already expired, and a meta orchestration refuses to issue a further sub-call once its budget is gone. It never interrupts a running PJRT/GPU call; it only declines to start new work. The gRPC layer maps it to DEADLINE_EXCEEDED.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.DeadlineExceeded"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.Dim","page":"API Reference","text":"Dim\n\nA single axis of a tensor shape. kind is one of FIXED, BATCH, or VARIABLE; size is meaningful only when kind == FIXED (it is 0 otherwise). A FIXED dim has a concrete size, a BATCH dim is the batch axis (from the reserved n/b shape letters), and a VARIABLE dim (a -1 in the manifest dims map) is a dynamic non-batch axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.Dim"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.EndpointsConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"EndpointsConfig\n\nThe listen addresses (the endpoints: config block): host, the gRPC port, the optional metrics_port for the Prometheus exposition endpoint (0 = disabled), and max_concurrent_requests, the cap on simultaneously-handled RPCs (0 = uncapped). For a worker fronted by the gateway, bind host to all interfaces (0.0.0.0) so the gateway and Prometheus can reach it; the gRPC port is usually derived from the node file's base_port and the metrics port from metrics_base_port.\n\nmax_concurrent_requests is a worker-level overload backstop: past the cap, new requests are shed immediately with RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED rather than queued. Keep it strictly above the gateway's per-worker outbound stream limit so it never sheds traffic the gateway has already admitted (and so it never rejects a meta-model's loopback sub-call); in single-worker mode (no gateway, clients hit the worker directly) it is the only inbound admission control.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.EndpointsConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.GrpcConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"GrpcConfig\n\ngRPC transport limits for a worker's server. max_recv_msg_bytes / max_send_msg_bytes bound a single gRPC message in each direction (decode/encode caps, not allocations). Configured under the grpc: block of a worker (or node global:) config, with INFERENCE_SERVER_GRPC_MAX_RECV_MSG_BYTES / INFERENCE_SERVER_GRPC_MAX_SEND_MSG_BYTES environment overrides. Default DEFAULT_GRPC_MSG_BYTES (512 MiB).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.GrpcConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.InferRequest","page":"API Reference","text":"InferRequest\n\nA decoded inference request, the scheduler's unit of work. It names the target model (model_name), the requested_outputs the caller wants returned, and the input tensors (inputs, a Vector{NamedTensor}). deadline_ns is an absolute local time_ns() deadline (0 means none): a remaining-budget timeout carried over the wire is converted to this local absolute form at decode, so cross-process monotonic-clock differences never matter. The codec produces it from a wire ModelInferRequest; the scheduler and runtime consume only this transport-agnostic form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.InferRequest"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.Manifest","page":"API Reference","text":"Manifest\n\nThe parsed and validated manifest.yaml of a model bundle. It records the format_version, the bundle name and description, the executable input/output specs (executable_inputs/executable_outputs), the optional client-facing specs (client_inputs/client_outputs, present only when a model.jl transforms the I/O), the BatchingSpec, provenance metadata, and the derived 0-based input_batch_dim. Tensor specs are TensorSpec values; see TensorSpec and Dim for the einsum-style shape encoding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.Manifest"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.ModelControlMode","page":"API Reference","text":"ModelControlMode\n\nHow a worker manages the set of loaded models over its lifetime (mirrors NVIDIA Triton's model-control-mode). STATIC loads and compiles every bundle once at startup and never changes the set. DYNAMIC (the default) additionally runs a filesystem watcher that polls the model repository every model_poll_seconds and hot-swaps bundles as they are added, changed, or removed on disk. EXPLICIT cedes authority to an upstream control plane (the externally-managed residency behavior): no autonomous watcher, and non-resident models are not served until the control plane pins them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.ModelControlMode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.ModelSchedConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"ModelSchedConfig\n\nPer-model scheduler overrides (an entry under scheduler.models). weight is the model's relative compute share (default 1.0, so all-default weights yield uniform shares; consulted only by the fair discipline). residency is the model's initial residency floor (see ResidencyState); nothing means unspecified, which the server resolves at startup to UNPINNED (swapped on demand, with host RAM copies within runtime.host_weight_budget), or to PINNED_SYSTEM when the on-demand weight cache is enabled and the budget is unlimited. max_batch_size caps how many rows the scheduler coalesces into one dispatch of this model; nothing means uncapped. The cap does not change compiled shapes: a partial fill still pads up to the smallest compiled batch size, and a single request larger than the cap is still served (requests are never split).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.ModelSchedConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.NamedTensor","page":"API Reference","text":"NamedTensor(name, dtype, shape, data)\nNamedTensor(name, data)\n\nA named host tensor carried across the transport boundary as both an input and an output. It pairs a tensor name with its DType, its shape (Julia column-major dimensions), and the backing data array. The two-argument form derives dtype and shape from a typed host Array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.NamedTensor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.NumericsMode","page":"API Reference","text":"NumericsMode\n\nHow f32 matmul/convolution precision is resolved at model compile time (the runtime.numerics knob). NUMERICS_AUTO (the default) is hardware-adaptive: TF32 is used where the GPU supports it (compute capability >= 8.0) and explicit TF32 algorithms are stripped where it does not, so the same bundle compiles everywhere but its numerics follow the hardware. NUMERICS_F32 pins full f32 everywhere: TF32 DotAlgorithms are rewritten to f32 and every algorithm-free f32 dot_general/convolution gets precision_config = HIGHEST, so numerics are identical across GPU generations (the mode for validated deployments; costs tensor-core throughput on TF32-capable GPUs). NUMERICS_TF32 compiles exactly like auto (TF32 permitted; kernel choice stays with XLA/cuBLAS, and StableHLO cannot force TF32 for convolutions at all) but makes the hardware requirement a guarantee: the worker fails startup on hardware that cannot run TF32, rather than silently degrading per worker in a mixed fleet.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.NumericsMode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.PoolAcquireTimeout","page":"API Reference","text":"PoolAcquireTimeout(span, waited_ns)\n\nRaised by acquire_slot! when a deadline_ns was supplied and passed before span contiguous slots became free. The waiter is dequeued before this is thrown, so it never stalls the line. Callers that carry a request deadline (e.g. a meta model's fan-out) translate this into their own deadline-exceeded error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.PoolAcquireTimeout"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.PrivateWeightStore","page":"API Reference","text":"PrivateWeightStore()\n\nThe default store: each worker materializes its own host copy of a model's weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.PrivateWeightStore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.ResidencyMode","page":"API Reference","text":"ResidencyMode\n\nWho owns device residency on a worker, fixed at startup. SELF_MANAGED lets the worker autonomously transfer and evict weights above each model's floor within the device budget; EXTERNALLY_MANAGED makes a control plane authoritative (no autonomous eviction, non-resident models are not served until pinned). This is no longer configured directly: it is derived from ModelControlMode (explicit ⇒ EXTERNALLY_MANAGED, otherwise SELF_MANAGED).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.ResidencyMode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.ResidencyState","page":"API Reference","text":"ResidencyState\n\nThe residency floor an operator (self-managed) or control plane (externally-managed) sets for a model's weights. UNPINNED keeps no guaranteed residency (loaded from the mmap on demand); PINNED_SYSTEM guarantees the weights stay resident in host RAM (and must be transferred to the device before execution); PINNED_DEVICE guarantees them resident on the GPU for the server's lifetime (exempt from eviction).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.ResidencyState"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.RuntimeConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"RuntimeConfig\n\nRuntime and device settings (the runtime: config block). backend selects CPU or CUDA execution; device_ordinal picks the GPU among several visible ones; device_memory_fraction is the fraction of device memory claimed for the pool; preallocate claims that pool up front; allow_cpu_fallback permits falling back to CPU when the device is unavailable; device_weight_fraction is the share of the BFC arena (device_memory_fraction * device memory) that holds resident model weights, device-pinned and on-demand together, in (0, 1] (1.0, the default, uses the whole arena minus measured scratch and headroom; resolved to a byte budget at startup, GPU only; models that do not fit in it are swapped on and off the device on demand); device_headroom_fraction is the fraction of the arena kept free as anti-fragmentation slack and feeds the startup auto-sizing (the worker probes each model's peak device usage and sizes the cache so pinned weights + on-demand + scratch + this slack fit); residency_mode selects self-managed or externally-managed residency; shared_host_weights opts the node into the shared-memory host-weight store so same-node workers share one host copy of each system-pinned model; and shared_host_weights_mode sets the permission bits (an octal string, default \"666\") for those shared regions and their lock files. The \"666\" default keeps cross-UID container setups working but is world-writable; \"660\" is recommended for production and multi-user systems. autotune (default true) enables XLA's GPU compile autotuner; set it false to compile with xla_gpu_autotune_level=0 (default gemm/conv algorithm selection, no timing trials), which removes autotuning's run-to-run non-determinism and its device scratch that otherwise inflates the startup memory probe (_probe_max_scratch!) on the first, un-cached start. autotune_cache (default nothing, meaning inherit Reactant's LocalPreferences.toml) toggles the persistent per-fusion autotune cache, and autotune_cache_dir (default \"\", inherit) sets its directory; both are applied to Reactant's compile cache at worker startup, so a container can drive them by env. numerics (default auto) sets the f32 matmul/convolution precision policy; see NumericsMode. executable_cache (default true) stores each compiled program under the bundle's .cache/ directory and loads it on later starts instead of recompiling, keyed by the Reactantjll build, the device, and the MLIR content (a changed model*.mlir invalidates its programs; a weights-only update does not); it needs a Reactant that exposes executable serialization and is otherwise a no-op. profile (default default) applies a named set of defaults; see RuntimeProfile. `batchsizes(defaultall, or the profile's) selects which compiled batch sizes are loaded; see [BatchSizeMode](@ref).xlaflags(default empty, plus the profile's) is a mapping of XLADebugOptionsfield names to values, passed to every compile, for examplexlagpuexcludenondeterministicops: true; names and value types are checked against the linked XLA at startup, and the flags are part of the executable cache key.weightsmigration(defaultoff) converts legacy-order weights files at load; see [WeightsMigration](@ref).hostweightbudgetcaps the host RAM held by weight copies kept between GPU loads, in bytes (see [parsebytesize](@ref) for the accepted spellings). The default0keeps no copies;nothing(unlimitedin YAML) keeps every model's copy with no cap. Withsharedhostweightsthe budget is one node-wide pool shared by every worker; without it each worker getsbudget ÷ hostweightbudget_workers` (default 1, which the node supervisor sets to its worker count).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.RuntimeConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.RuntimeProfile","page":"API Reference","text":"RuntimeProfile\n\nA named set of runtime defaults (the runtime.profile knob). PROFILE_DEFAULT changes nothing. PROFILE_REGULATED is for validated deployments that need reproducible results: it defaults batch_sizes to largest, adds xla_gpu_exclude_nondeterministic_ops: true to xla_flags, and turns scheduler.coalescing off. Anything set explicitly in the config wins over the profile, so each piece can be turned back off. The profile does not change numerics; instead the worker logs a prominent warning at startup naming the f32 matmul/convolution precision actually in effect unless numerics is f32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.RuntimeProfile"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.SchedulerConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"SchedulerConfig\n\nGlobal scheduler settings (the scheduler: config block). ema_halflife_seconds is the half-life of the recent-compute moving average that drives fairness; recency_penalty_cap bounds the recency adjustment; coalescing_discount is the cost discount applied to coalesced batches; cost_ema_alpha is the smoothing factor for the learned per-batch-size cost; max_queue_depth caps each model's queue independently (a full model rejects new requests without affecting admission for the others); dispatch_timeout_seconds is the per-request execution timeout; discipline selects the inter-model ordering (see SchedulingDiscipline); compaction_interval runs worker-local memory compaction every N on-demand weight-cache loads (0 disables), the standalone (no-gateway) trigger, off by default so a gateway-fronted worker never self-compacts; and models holds the per-model ModelSchedConfig overrides. coalescing (default true, false under the regulated profile) lets the scheduler merge several queued requests to one model into a single dispatch; when it is false every dispatch serves exactly one request, padded to its compiled batch size, so a request's rows always occupy the same batch slots whatever else is queued.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.SchedulerConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.SchedulingDiscipline","page":"API Reference","text":"SchedulingDiscipline\n\nThe inter-model dispatch ordering. FAIR is the deficit-weighted, cost-aware policy with per-model weights and the coalescing discount; FIFO serves in global arrival order; EDF (earliest-deadline-first) serves the model whose most-urgent queued request has the soonest deadline. Coalescing runs underneath all three.\n\nGuidance: FAIR is for deployments where models share this worker with no upstream placement authority, a single-GPU worker or a multi-GPU fleet behind the round-robin gateway, where the worker itself must stop one model from crowding out the rest. Under the gateway's lpt_packing scheduling the gateway is the fairness authority (placement concentration plus the per-worker share cap), and workers must run FIFO or EDF so the two do not fight; lpt_packing supersedes the role FAIR played on manually-assigned multi-GPU fleets.\n\nEDF is for deadline-sensitive deployments where requests carry a remaining-budget timeout (see the request-level timeout parameter). It degrades to FIFO whenever queued requests share the same deadline, so its only divergence from FIFO is to promote requests with less budget left, in practice the in-flight meta-model sub-calls that have already spent part of their budget on an earlier stage. It also sheds work that cannot finish within its learned compute cost (laxity), so it trades some throughput (batch fragmentation, and no per-model fairness) for hitting more deadlines under load. NOTE: EDF derives urgency purely from the deadline, so issuing different per-client deadlines for the same model reorders that model's service and therefore affects fairness across clients; uniform deadlines keep it behaving like FIFO.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.SchedulingDiscipline"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.ServerConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"ServerConfig\n\nThe fully resolved configuration for a single worker process, frozen for the process lifetime. It is produced from a node file (see node.jl) with environment-variable overrides applied, then checked by validate_config. Fields: model_dirs (bundle search paths), cache_dir, the RuntimeConfig, SchedulerConfig, and EndpointsConfig sub-configs, models_include (an allowlist of model names to load; empty means load all), model_poll_seconds (the dynamic-mode interval at which the worker re-scans its model_dirs for added, changed, or removed bundles and hot-swaps them), model_control_mode (see ModelControlMode: static, dynamic, or explicit), and the GrpcConfig grpc sub-config (gRPC message-size limits). ReactantServer.serve also accepts a ServerConfig directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.ServerConfig"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.SharedStoreFull","page":"API Reference","text":"SharedStoreFull\n\nRaised by materialize_host_weights! on a SharedWeightStore when the node cannot back a new region: /dev/shm is full or the memory limit refuses its pages. Nothing is left behind (the partial region is unlinked); the caller can fall back to a private copy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.SharedStoreFull"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.SharedWeightStore","page":"API Reference","text":"SharedWeightStore(; mode=0o666, budget=nothing)\n\nOpt-in store backing each model's host weights with a node-level POSIX shared-memory region so same-node workers share one copy. See the file header for the flock-coordinated protocol. mode sets the permission bits for the regions and their lock files. The default 0o666 lets workers running as unrelated UIDs (for example different containers) share the regions, but is world-writable; 0o660 is recommended for production and multi-user systems.\n\nbudget (bytes) caps the regions in /dev/shm across every worker on the node; see \"Node budget\" in the file header. nothing (the default) leaves them unbudgeted. prefix names the regions and lock files (/dev/shm/<prefix>-...); stores with different prefixes share neither regions nor a budget. Every worker on a node must use the same one, which the server does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.SharedWeightStore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.TensorSpec","page":"API Reference","text":"TensorSpec\n\nOne tensor in a Manifest: its name, DType, shape (a Vector{Dim}), and batch_axis (the 1-based index of the batch dim, or nothing when the tensor is not batched).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.TensorSpec"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.WeightStore","page":"API Reference","text":"WeightStore\n\nHost-RAM residency backend for a model's materialized weights. PrivateWeightStore (the default) gives each worker its own copy; SharedWeightStore shares one copy per model across same-node workers through a POSIX shared-memory region. See the file header for the coordination protocol.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.WeightStore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.WeightsFileId","page":"API Reference","text":"WeightsFileId\n\nWhich on-disk version of a weights file a process read: its device, inode, size and mtime. Take it from the open file (weights_file_id(stat(io))) at the moment the file is mapped, so it names the bytes actually mapped even if the path is replaced afterwards. NO_WEIGHTS_FILE stands for no file (hand-built entries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.WeightsFileId"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.WeightsMigration","page":"API Reference","text":"WeightsMigration\n\nWhat the worker does with a bundle whose weights.safetensors is in the legacy axis order (the runtime.weights_migration knob). MIGRATE_OFF (the default) loads it as it is, permuting every tensor on each load. MIGRATE_COPY writes a verified copy in the program's axis order, weights.T.safetensors, next to it and points the manifest's weights_file at the copy; the original is untouched and servers that predate the copy keep loading it. MIGRATE_REPLACE does the same, then, after proving the copy again, renames it over weights.safetensors and marks the manifest format_version: \"2.1\". Use replace only once every server reading the directory understands that version. Migration never fails a model: on any error the bundle loads as it was.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.WeightsMigration"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.acquire_slot!","page":"API Reference","text":"acquire_slot!(pool, span=1; deadline_ns=0) -> PoolSlot\n\nBlock until span physically contiguous slots are free, then return them as one slot whose [offset, offset + span*slot_bytes) range no other in-flight slot overlaps. Throws an ArgumentError immediately if span exceeds the pool's total slot count, since such a request could never be satisfied and would otherwise deadlock. Waiters are served in FIFO order. Pair with release_slot!, which frees the whole run.\n\ndeadline_ns is an absolute time_ns() deadline (0 = wait indefinitely, the default and prior behavior). When set, a waiter that has not acquired by the deadline throws [PoolAcquireTimeout] instead of parking past it. A one-shot timer wakes the waiter at the deadline even if no slot is released in the meantime, so a starved waiter fails fast rather than burning a request's whole budget in the park.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.acquire_slot!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.deadline_params-Tuple{Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"deadline_params(budget_ns) -> Dict{String,InferParameter}\ndeadline_params(PB::Module, budget_ns) -> Dict{String,InferParameter}\n\nBuild the request-level parameters map carrying a remaining-budget timeout of budget_ns nanoseconds (see TIMEOUT_NS_PARAM). A non-positive budget_ns yields an empty map (no deadline). Merge the result into a ModelInferRequest's parameters. The PB-first form builds the map from pb module PB (consumer packages pass their own generated module; the single-argument form uses this package's inference module).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.deadline_params"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.decode_infer_request","page":"API Reference","text":"decode_infer_request(msg, registry=nothing) -> DecodedRequest\ndecode_infer_request(PB::Module, msg, registry=nothing) -> DecodedRequest\n\nTranslate a decoded ModelInferRequest message into the boundary InferRequest. The transport (gRPC) hands us the already-decoded protobuf message, so the codec never touches wire bytes. Input tensor data is read from a registered shared-memory region (preferred when the tensor declares one), otherwise from rawinputcontents, otherwise from the typed contents field. The PB-first form accepts a message from pb module PB (consumer packages pass their own generated module; the shorter form uses this package's inference module).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.decode_infer_request"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.decode_infer_response-Tuple{Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"decode_infer_response(msg) -> Vector{NamedTensor}\ndecode_infer_response(PB::Module, msg) -> Vector{NamedTensor}\n\nTranslate a ModelInferResponse into boundary NamedTensor outputs. Data is read from rawoutputcontents when present, otherwise from the typed contents field. Shared-memory-backed outputs are not supported on this path (the caller never requests them). The PB-first form accepts a message from pb module PB (consumer packages pass their own generated module).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.decode_infer_response"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.encode_infer_request-Tuple{AbstractString, Vector{NamedTensor}}","page":"API Reference","text":"encode_infer_request(model_name, inputs; requested_outputs=String[], id=\"\") -> ModelInferRequest\nencode_infer_request(PB::Module, model_name, inputs; requested_outputs=String[], id=\"\") -> ModelInferRequest\n\nBuild a ModelInferRequest from boundary NamedTensor inputs, with tensor data inline in rawinputcontents. requested_outputs, when non-empty, names the outputs to return. The PB-first form builds the message from pb module PB (consumer packages pass their own generated module).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.encode_infer_request"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.encode_infer_request_shm-Tuple{AbstractString, Vector{NamedTensor}, AbstractString, Vector{<:Integer}}","page":"API Reference","text":"encode_infer_request_shm(model_name, inputs, region, offsets; requested_outputs, id)\nencode_infer_request_shm(PB::Module, model_name, inputs, region, offsets; requested_outputs, id)\n\nEncode a request whose inputs are ALL staged in shared-memory region at the given byte offsets (parallel to inputs); no raw_input_contents (the receiver reads each tensor via shm_read). The receiver must have region registered. This is all-or-nothing per request: the decode path treats raw_input_contents as parallel-to-inputs, so a request never mixes raw and SHM inputs. The PB-first form builds the message from pb module PB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.encode_infer_request_shm"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.encode_infer_response-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString, Vector{NamedTensor}}","page":"API Reference","text":"encode_infer_response(model_name, id, outputs) -> ModelInferResponse\nencode_infer_response(PB::Module, model_name, id, outputs) -> ModelInferResponse\n\nBuild the response message with outputs entirely inline (rawoutputcontents). The transport serializes the returned message. The PB-first form builds the message from pb module PB (consumer packages pass their own generated module; the shorter form uses this package's inference module).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.encode_infer_response"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.encode_infer_response-Tuple{AbstractString, DecodedRequest, Vector{NamedTensor}, Union{Nothing, SharedMemoryRegistry}}","page":"API Reference","text":"encode_infer_response(model_name, decoded, outputs, registry) -> ModelInferResponse\nencode_infer_response(PB::Module, model_name, decoded, outputs, registry) -> ModelInferResponse\n\nBuild the response message, writing any output whose requested entry named a shared-memory region into that region (and referencing it in the response) instead of inline. rawoutputcontents holds the inline outputs in order. The PB-first form builds the message from pb module PB (consumer packages pass their own generated module).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.encode_infer_response"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.encode_model_metadata-Tuple{AbstractString, Manifest, AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"encode_model_metadata(name, manifest, platform) -> ModelMetadataResponse\nencode_model_metadata(PB::Module, name, manifest, platform) -> ModelMetadataResponse\n\nBuild a ModelMetadataResponse message from the manifest's client-facing I/O spec. The PB-first form builds the message from pb module PB (consumer packages pass their own generated module).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.encode_model_metadata"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.encode_repository_index-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}}","page":"API Reference","text":"encode_repository_index(names) -> RepositoryIndexResponse\nencode_repository_index(entries::AbstractVector{<:Pair}) -> RepositoryIndexResponse\nencode_repository_index(PB::Module, names) -> RepositoryIndexResponse\nencode_repository_index(PB::Module, entries::AbstractVector{<:Pair}) -> RepositoryIndexResponse\n\nBuild a RepositoryIndexResponse. The first form lists every model as READY (direct-client introspection). The second takes name => ready::Bool pairs and reports READY or UNAVAILABLE per model, so the gateway can discover which replicas actually serve a model (readiness reflects residency on the worker). The PB-first forms build the message from pb module PB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.encode_repository_index"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.host_cache_bytes-Tuple{WeightStore}","page":"API Reference","text":"host_cache_bytes(store) -> Int\n\nBytes held by every weight region on the node (the quantity the node budget bounds). 0 for the private store.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.host_cache_bytes"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.is_meta-Tuple{Manifest}","page":"API Reference","text":"is_meta(m::Manifest) -> Bool\n\nTrue when the bundle is a meta model (a Julia orchestration over other models) rather than a regular StableHLO executable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.is_meta"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.load_manifest-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"load_manifest(path) -> Manifest\n\nParse a manifest YAML file at path into a Manifest. This runs the structural parsing and validation of parse_manifest but not the bundle-directory checks in validate_manifest, so it is usable wherever only the manifest's contents are needed, for example a client deriving a model's I/O spec offline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.load_manifest"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.load_node-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"load_node(path) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nRead and structurally validate a node config file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.load_node"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.load_node_raw-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"load_node_raw(path) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nRead a node config file without structural validation. Used by the supervisor, which may need to synthesize the workers: list (see materialize_node!) before validating.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.load_node_raw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.materialize_host_weights!-Tuple{PrivateWeightStore, Vararg{Any, 4}}","page":"API Reference","text":"materialize_host_weights!(store, key, digest, specs, fill!) -> Vector{Any}\n\nReturn a model's host weight Arrays (in weight order), populating them via fill!(arrays) when this worker is the one that must materialize them. specs is a vector of (eltype, dims) per tensor. For PrivateWeightStore the arrays are freshly allocated; for SharedWeightStore they alias a node-shared region.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.materialize_host_weights!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.materialize_node!-Tuple{AbstractDict, AbstractVector{<:AbstractString}}","page":"API Reference","text":"materialize_node!(node, devices; cpu_workers=1) -> Vector{Union{String,Nothing}}\n\nPrepare a raw node config for supervised single-container deployment: assign one visible device per worker and return the per-worker device selectors (in worker declaration order; nothing means the worker gets no CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES of its own, the CPU case).\n\nWith no workers: list, one is synthesized: worker0..workerN-1, one per device (or cpu_workers workers when devices is empty). An explicit workers: list wins: each worker is assigned devices[i] positionally, or devices[gpu+1] when the entry carries a gpu: key. The gpu: key is consumed here (deleted after assignment) so each child process, seeing a single device through CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES, resolves device ordinal 0. Assigning one device to two workers, or having more workers than devices, is a ConfigError. Call validate_node on the result before use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.materialize_node!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.node_gpus-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API Reference","text":"node_gpus(node) -> :auto | Int | Vector{String}\n\nParse the optional top-level gpus: key: auto (the default when absent) asks the supervisor to enumerate visible devices; an integer is a device count (expanded to ordinals 0..n-1); a list gives explicit device selectors (ordinals or GPU UUIDs, passed to CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES verbatim).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.node_gpus"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.node_server_config-Tuple{AbstractDict, Union{Nothing, AbstractString}}","page":"API Reference","text":"node_server_config(node, worker) -> (ServerConfig, applied_overrides, worker_name)\n\nResolve the ServerConfig for one worker of a node. worker may be nothing when the node has exactly one worker (it defaults to that sole entry); otherwise it must name a defined worker. Environment overrides (INFERENCE_SERVER_*) are applied on top, as for any server config. Does not validate; call validate_config on the result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.node_server_config"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.parse_byte_size-Tuple{Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"parse_byte_size(s) -> Int\n\nParse a byte count such as 0, 0b, 512m, 5g or 1.5TiB: a non-negative number with an optional unit, case insensitive. Every unit is binary (5g and 5GB are both 5 GiB, as in Docker), so no two spellings disagree. An integer is taken as bytes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.parse_byte_size"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.release_host_weights!-Tuple{PrivateWeightStore, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"release_host_weights!(store, key) -> nothing\n\nRelease a model's host weights. For SharedWeightStore this detaches the region and, if this was the last holder on the node (a non-blocking upgrade to an exclusive flock succeeds), unlinks the region and its lock file. A no-op for the private store. The caller must drop all references to the arrays first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.release_host_weights!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.release_slot!-Tuple{PoolSlot}","page":"API Reference","text":"release_slot!(slot)\n\nReturn a slot acquired with acquire_slot! to the pool's free set, freeing every physical slot in its span. Releasing a derived subslot (index == 0) is a no-op. Throws if any slot in the span is already free (double release).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.release_slot!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.rename_host_weights!-Tuple{PrivateWeightStore, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"rename_host_weights!(store, old, new) -> nothing\n\nRekey a model's attached host-weight region from old to new (a model rename; the weights are unchanged). The region itself keeps its original content-addressed SHM name; only this worker's bookkeeping key moves, so a later release_host_weights!(store, new) detaches the same region. A no-op for the private store and when nothing is attached under old.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.rename_host_weights!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.same_ipc_namespace-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"same_ipc_namespace(name) -> Bool\n\nReturn whether the POSIX shared-memory object name is visible in this process's IPC namespace. The client passes the name of an object it created; we answer true only if we can open that same object ourselves, which is what determines whether system shared-memory transport can work between the two processes. The open is read-only and detached immediately; nothing is registered or kept mapped. Any failure (object absent because we are in a different namespace, permission error, malformed name) is reported as false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.same_ipc_namespace"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.scratch-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{PoolSlot, Any, Type{T}}} where T","page":"API Reference","text":"scratch(slot, dims, T) -> Array{T}\nscratch(slot, [dims1 => T1, dims2 => T2, ...]) -> Vector{Array}\n\nCarve one typed buffer per dims => T spec from slot in a single call, advancing the slot's cursor so the buffers occupy disjoint, contiguous byte ranges. Each buffer is an Array{T} aliasing the pool's backing (via pool_view; zero-copy, and uniform across SHM- and Memory-backed pools since it is just pool_base + offset); write into the returned arrays directly. dims is a shape tuple (or a bare integer for a vector).\n\nThis is the buffer-request interface shared by the meta-model call.scratch and the client driver: ask for ALL buffers up front in one call. The carved buffers' lifetime is bounded by slot (and by the pool, which the caller keeps alive); they become invalid once it is released.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.scratch"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.shm_read-Tuple{SharedMemoryRegistry, AbstractString, Integer, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"shm_read(registry, name, offset, byte_size) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nCopy [offset, offset+byte_size) of the named region into a fresh byte vector. The copy is done while the region's lock is held, so a concurrent shm_unregister! of the same region cannot detach the mapping underneath the read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.shm_read"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.shm_register!-Tuple{SharedMemoryRegistry, AbstractString, AbstractString, Integer, Integer}","page":"API Reference","text":"shm_register!(registry, name, key, offset, byte_size)\n\nAttach the existing POSIX shared-memory object key read-write and register it under name. Re-registering a name replaces (and detaches) the previous mapping.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.shm_register!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.shm_unregister!-Tuple{SharedMemoryRegistry, AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"shm_unregister!(registry, name)\n\nUnregister and detach a region, or all regions when name is empty. Idempotent: unregistering a name that is not registered is a successful no-op (it matches KServe semantics and lets the gateway fan-out and the client's pre-emptive cleanup unregister succeed without a spurious error). Registration, by contrast, fails loudly (see shm_register!) so a bad region surfaces at register time rather than during inference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.shm_unregister!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.shm_write!-Tuple{SharedMemoryRegistry, AbstractString, Integer, Vector{UInt8}}","page":"API Reference","text":"shm_write!(registry, name, offset, bytes)\n\nCopy bytes into [offset, offset+length(bytes)) of the named region. The copy is done while the region's lock is held, so a concurrent shm_unregister! of the same region cannot detach the mapping underneath the write.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.shm_write!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.trim_host_cache!-Tuple{WeightStore}","page":"API Reference","text":"trim_host_cache!(store) -> Int\n\nEvict holder-free regions until the node fits the store's budget (a lowered budget, or regions an earlier run left behind). Returns the number evicted; a no-op without a budget.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.trim_host_cache!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.validate_node-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API Reference","text":"validate_node(node)\n\nStructural validation of a parsed node config. Raises ConfigError on a malformed file: missing model_repo, duplicate worker names, colliding ports, or a models: entry that targets an undefined worker. The models: map is optional for any node: when omitted, every worker loads every bundle in the repo; when present, it is a per-model override that pins the named models to device memory on the listed workers (see worker_raw_config).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.validate_node"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.weights_digest-Tuple{AbstractString, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"weights_digest(key, specs; content=0) -> UInt64\n\nDigest over a model's identity, weight layout (name, per-tensor dtype/size/shape, a format version), and a content token identifying the weight file's on-disk version (see weights_file_token). Two workers computing this for the same model and the same file agree on the same region key. Without the content token a weights-only update (same name, same tensor layout) would collide with the previous version's region and silently serve stale weights, including across server restarts (regions in /dev/shm outlive the process by design).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.weights_digest"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.weights_file_id-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"weights_file_id(path) -> WeightsFileId\n\nThe WeightsFileId of whatever the path names now; NO_WEIGHTS_FILE for an empty or missing path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.weights_file_id"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.weights_file_token-Tuple{WeightsFileId}","page":"API Reference","text":"weights_file_token(id_or_path) -> UInt64\n\nIdentity token for one on-disk version of a weights file, folded into weights_digest via its content keyword. Derived from the device, inode, size and mtime: cheap (no content read, so the shared store's zero-copy attach path stays zero-read) and identical across same-node workers that mapped the same file. The inode makes an atomic replacement (write a new file, rename it over the old one) a new version even when size and mtime match. Pass the WeightsFileId taken when the file was mapped; a path is stat'ed now, which can name a newer file than the one mapped. Returns 0 for no file (hand-built test entries), which reproduces the layout-only digest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.weights_file_token"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.wire_batch_spec-Tuple{Manifest}","page":"API Reference","text":"wire_batch_spec(m::Manifest) -> Union{Nothing,Tuple{String,Int}}\n\nThe name and 1-based batch axis of the first WIRE-FACING input that declares one, or nothing when the model declares no batch axis at all (every request is then one item).\n\n\"Wire-facing\" is client_inputs when the bundle has them and executable_inputs otherwise: client_inputs is present exactly when a model.jl intercepts the request (see validate_manifest), and without one the client sends the executable inputs directly.\n\nThis exists so a gateway can count the items in a request it never fully decodes. It cannot infer the axis: the position is per tensor and genuinely varies across bundles (whcn puts it last, nc first), the first input need not carry one at all (a cross-encoder's query is unbatched while its keys are batched), and it has moved between exports of the same model. Picking the first input that declares an axis mirrors _derive_input_batch_dim, and the name lets the reader match it against the request's own name-addressed tensors rather than trusting their order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.wire_batch_spec"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.with_cached_host_weights-Tuple{Any, SharedWeightStore, Any, UInt64, Any, Any}","page":"API Reference","text":"with_cached_host_weights(f, store, key, digest, specs, fill!) -> Union{Tuple{Bool, Any}, Nothing}\n\nRun f(arrays) on a model's host weights from the node cache. The store must have a budget. The region is held only while f runs (an upload) and then detached without unlinking, so it stays cached for the next load by any worker. A missing region is admitted (evicting least recently loaded ones) and filled via fill!(arrays) first. Returns (hit, f(arrays)), where hit says the region already existed, or nothing when the region cannot fit the budget; the caller then loads without the cache.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.with_cached_host_weights"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.worker_names-Tuple{AbstractDict}","page":"API Reference","text":"worker_names(node) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe worker names in declaration order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.worker_names"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#ReactantServerCore.worker_raw_config-Tuple{AbstractDict, AbstractString}","page":"API Reference","text":"worker_raw_config(node, name) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nResolve a single worker's raw config dict from a (validated) node config: deep-merge global with the named worker's override blocks, then set model_dirs to the shared repo, the endpoint port from base_port, the runtime device ordinal from the worker's GPU, and the node-level shared_host_weights flag. Every worker loads every bundle in the repo; the top-level models: map is an optional per-model override that pins the named models to device memory on the listed workers (translated here into scheduler.models.<name>.residency: device). The result has the shape build_config consumes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerCore.worker_raw_config"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServer.Scheduler","page":"API Reference","text":"Scheduler\n\nThe deficit-weighted, cost-aware, coalescing dispatch engine. Concurrent requests land on per-model queues; a single dispatch loop runs one GPU execution at a time, coalescing same-model requests into one batched execution at a compiled size and sharing GPU time by relative model weight and a learned per-batch-size cost estimate. It holds the model registry, the backend, the device memory pool, the SchedulerConfig, per-model dispatch state, and an optional on-demand WeightCache. Submit work with infer and read observability counters with scheduler_metrics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.Scheduler"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.infer","page":"API Reference","text":"infer(scheduler, request) -> Vector{NamedTensor}\n\nSubmit a request and block until the scheduler returns the result. Re-raises any error captured during dispatch.\n\nRuns the model's preprocess/postprocess hooks here, on the caller's task, rather than on the dispatch loop: preprocess before the request is queued, postprocess on the raw device outputs the loop hands back. Because each gRPC request runs on its own task, many requests' hook work proceeds in parallel and overlaps the single, serialized GPU execution. The dispatch loop coalesces and runs qr.prepared and never executes a request whose preprocess has not finished, since a request is only enqueued (made visible to the loop) after preprocess returns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.infer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.scheduler_metrics","page":"API Reference","text":"scheduler_metrics(scheduler) -> Dict{String,NamedTuple}\n\nSnapshot per-model observability: dispatch count, total compute consumed, current recent-compute EMA, queue-wait P50/P99, the histogram of dispatch batch sizes, and residency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.scheduler_metrics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.set_policy!","page":"API Reference","text":"set_policy!(scheduler, name; weight=nothing) -> nothing\n\nUpdate a model's live scheduler policy. weight is consulted only by the fair discipline. Available in both residency modes. Raises on an unknown model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.set_policy!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.set_residency!","page":"API Reference","text":"set_residency!(scheduler, name, target::ResidencyState) -> ResidencyState\n\nMove a model to the target residency floor. Only meaningful in externally-managed mode; the worker rejects it otherwise. Runs on the dispatch thread (the sole residency mutator) and blocks until applied. Raises on an unknown model or in self-managed mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.set_residency!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.control_status","page":"API Reference","text":"control_status(scheduler) -> NamedTuple\n\nA control-plane snapshot of the worker: its residency mode and scheduling discipline, plus a per-model view. A meta is reported as a single model whose footprint is the sum of its sub-models' weights; the internal sub-models are folded into it and not reported on their own, so the gateway packs and routes the group as one unit and never sees the stages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.control_status"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.compact!","page":"API Reference","text":"compact!(cache, registry; reload) -> Int\n\nDefragment the device arena. Frees every resident non-pinned device weight buffer at once so the BFC allocator coalesces its free list, then reloads each non-pinned model named in reload. Models not in reload are left freed; in self-managed mode they reload lazily through acquire! on their next dispatch. Host floors (host_weights) are never touched, so the reload is a pure host->device copy where a floor exists.\n\nDevice-pinned models are deliberately left in place: they are loaded once at startup, before any on-demand traffic, so they sit at the base of the arena, and compaction neither frees them nor pays to re-read them from disk. Only the on-demand churn region above them is freed and coalesced.\n\nRuns on the dispatch-loop thread (the sole residency mutator), so no execution reads a buffer while it is being freed. In externally-managed mode acquire! will not autonomously load, so the reload list is ignored there (the control plane re-pins what it needs). Returns the number of models reloaded.\n\n\n\n\n\ncompact!(scheduler; reload_models=String[]) -> Int\n\nFree the resident non-pinned device weights so the allocator coalesces its free list, then reload each model named in reload_models. An empty list frees only; non-pinned models not listed reload lazily on their next dispatch (self-managed mode). Device-pinned models are left in place (loaded once at startup, never reloaded from disk). A no-op when the on-demand weight cache is disabled. Runs on the dispatch thread (the sole residency mutator) and blocks until applied. Returns the number of models reloaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.compact!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.acquire!","page":"API Reference","text":"acquire!(cache, entry) -> nothing\n\nGuarantee entry.executable.weights is resident before the model runs. Device-pinned and already-resident models return immediately (the latter is bumped to most-recently-used and its eviction priority raised). In self-managed mode an evicted model is admitted autonomously by admit_weights!: it evicts the cheapest set of resident models that makes the weights and the execution scratch fit, compacting only as a fallback. Without device memory statistics (CPU, mocks) admission falls back to the byte budget alone, and a model larger than the whole budget is loaded anyway after evicting everything, with a warning, since it cannot run otherwise. In externally-managed mode the worker does not autonomously load: an evicted model raises NotResidentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.acquire!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.admit_weights!","page":"API Reference","text":"admit_weights!(cache, entry) -> nothing\n\nMake the evicted model entry resident, evicting what it must (see the file header for the steps). Throws AdmissionError when the model cannot be placed. Runs on the dispatch thread, the sole residency mutator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.admit_weights!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServer.AdmissionError","page":"API Reference","text":"AdmissionError\n\nRaised when a model cannot be placed on the device: either it cannot fit even with every evictable model gone, or eviction and compaction together could not make room. Carries the reason and a memory report for the log.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.AdmissionError"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.migrate_bundle_weights!","page":"API Reference","text":"migrate_bundle_weights!(dir, mode::WeightsMigration) -> Symbol\n\nMigrate one bundle's weights file to the program's axis order (see WeightsMigration). Returns :off, :skipped (nothing to migrate: a meta bundle, or a custom weights_file), :current (already in the program's axis order, or already copied under copy), :copied, :replaced, or :failed (logged; the bundle is left loadable as it was).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.migrate_bundle_weights!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.verify_transposed","page":"API Reference","text":"verify_transposed(old, new; reference = nothing) -> nothing\n\nProve that new is an exact copy of the legacy file old in the program's axis order.\n\nnew must be marked axis_order: xla and hold the same tensor names, dtypes and byte counts, each header shape reversed, the same metadata apart from the migration keys, and every tensor's data equal byte for byte (so NaN payloads and signed zeros count) to the array the legacy path reads. With reference (the path of old), each legacy array is also read by SafeTensors.jl and must match ours. Throws WeightsMigrationError naming the first difference.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.verify_transposed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.resolve_weights_path","page":"API Reference","text":"resolve_weights_path(dir, m::Manifest) -> String\n\nThe weights file to load for a bundle. Normally the manifest's weights_file; but a migrated copy whose source weights.safetensors has changed since (a redeploy of the original) is stale, and the original is loaded instead, so a deploy is never ignored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.resolve_weights_path"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServer.NotResidentError","page":"API Reference","text":"NotResidentError\n\nRaised by acquire! in externally-managed mode when a request targets a model whose weights are not currently resident. A control plane is authoritative for residency in that mode, so the worker does not autonomously load; the model must be pinned first.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.NotResidentError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServer.RunningServer","page":"API Reference","text":"RunningServer\n\nHandle to a server started with serve(...; blocking=false). It holds the resolved ServerConfig, the model registry, the running Scheduler, the device memory pool, the shared-memory registry, the underlying gRPC server, and the listen port. Pass it to stop! to shut the server down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.RunningServer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.weight_budget","page":"API Reference","text":"weight_budget(; arena, fraction, wiggle, max_scratch, pinned_bytes) -> (; on_demand_budget, weight_pool, scratch_ceiling, pinned_over_commit)\n\nSolve for the on-demand weight-cache byte budget from the unified memory model. The arena holds, at the worst instant, pinned weights + on-demand weights + one model's execution max_scratch + a wiggle fraction of free slack:\n\nscratch_ceiling  = (1 - wiggle) * arena\nweight_pool      = min(fraction * arena, scratch_ceiling - max_scratch)   # for pinned + on-demand\non_demand_budget = clamp(weight_pool - pinned_bytes, 0, weight_pool)\n\nfraction caps the share of the arena devoted to weights (1.0 = all of it, minus scratch + wiggle). Pinned models reserve their footprint off the top, so the operator never subtracts it by hand. pinned_over_commit (pinned exceed the pool) flags a genuine over-commit no sizing can fix. Pure arithmetic, no device access, so it is unit testable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.weight_budget"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.weight_cache_stats","page":"API Reference","text":"weight_cache_stats(cache) -> NamedTuple\n\nSnapshot the cache counters under its lock for observability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.weight_cache_stats"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.free_weights!","page":"API Reference","text":"free_weights!(backend, bufs) -> nothing\n\nRelease every device buffer in bufs. Used to evict an unpinned model's resident weights.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.free_weights!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#ReactantServer.transfer_to_device","page":"API Reference","text":"transfer_to_device(backend, pool, hosts) -> Vector{Any}\n\nTransfer already-materialized host weight Arrays to device buffers, in order. This is the only cost paid on an on-demand load when weights are pinned in host RAM.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServer.transfer_to_device"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerClient.RetryPolicy","page":"API Reference","text":"RetryPolicy(; enabled=true, initial_backoff=1.0, factor=2.0, max_backoff=Inf,\n              min_budget=0.05, jitter=true, retry_unavailable=true)\n\nClient-side retry policy for requests the server sheds under overload. When a ModelInfer call is rejected with RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED (a worker or gateway at its concurrency cap, see endpoints.max_concurrent_requests), the client waits initial_backoff seconds and retries, growing the interval by factor each attempt (1s, 2s, 4s, ...) up to max_backoff, until the model's deadline budget is spent. The per-attempt deadline sent to the server shrinks to the remaining budget, so the retries never push total wall time past the original deadline.\n\njitter picks each wait uniformly in [0, backoff] (full jitter) so that concurrent chunk requests do not synchronize into a retry storm against the same worker. min_budget (seconds) is the smallest remaining budget worth another attempt; below it the client stops and surfaces the shed error. retry_unavailable also retries UNAVAILABLE (no replica reachable). Deadline, NOTFOUND, and INVALIDARGUMENT errors always fail fast. Set enabled=false to restore the old fail-fast behavior.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerClient.RetryPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#ReactantServerGateway.RunningGateway","page":"API Reference","text":"RunningGateway\n\nHandle to a gateway started with serve_gateway(...; blocking=false). Pass it to stop! to shut the gRPC server, the readiness prober, and the admin HTTP server down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"ReactantServerGateway.RunningGateway"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#Reproducibility-and-Regulated-Use","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"A validated deployment needs more than correct outputs: it needs the same outputs for the same input, today and after a restart, a redeploy, or a scale-out. This page lists what can change a model's output on ReactantServer, what controls each source, and how the regulated runtime profile and the release images fit together into a deployment you can validate once and rely on.\n\nThe short version: the same bundle, served from the same image digest, on the same GPU class, with the same node file, gives the same outputs. Each section below covers one thing that can break that, and how to hold it fixed.","title":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#Numerics","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"runtime.numerics sets the precision of f32 matrix multiplications and convolutions, and it is the largest source of difference between deployments.\n\ntf32: TF32 tensor cores, which keep 10 mantissa bits of each operand instead of f32's 23. Faster on NVIDIA Ampere and newer, with results that differ from full f32 in the low digits. Startup fails unless TF32 is confirmed active, so a worker cannot silently serve in f32.\nf32: full f32 everywhere, on every GPU generation, attested bit-exact by the startup probe. The choice when a hardware refresh must not change outputs, at the cost of tensor-core throughput.\nauto (the default): TF32 where the GPU supports it, full f32 where it does not. One bundle runs everywhere, but its numerics follow the hardware, so a fleet that mixes GPU generations serves different results for the same request. Not suitable for a validated deployment.\n\nPin the mode the deployment was validated with. Even within one mode, XLA and cuBLAS choose kernels per GPU model, so results match a validation only on the same GPU class; validate again when the class changes. Node Configuration describes how each mode is enforced.","title":"Numerics"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#Batch-size","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"A bundle may carry several compiled batch sizes (model.b1.mlir, model.b4.mlir, ...). Each is a separate program, and XLA is free to choose different kernels, tilings, and reduction orders for each. A request served by the batch-1 program can therefore differ in the last bits from the same request served by the batch-8 program.\n\nUnder runtime.batch_sizes: all (the default) the scheduler picks the program from how many requests are queued at the moment of dispatch, so a request's output can depend on unrelated traffic: the same input returns slightly different values at night and at peak load.\n\nruntime.batch_sizes: largest removes that dependence: every dispatch runs the largest compiled program, padded to a full batch. The cost is the full batch's compute on every dispatch, including a lone single-row request.\n\nOne program removes the batch-size dependence, but whether a row's output is also independent of its position in the batch, and of the other rows beside it, depends on the model. Models whose rows are computed independently behave this way in practice; an operation that mixes values across the batch axis does not. Turning coalescing off, described next, removes that dependence on other traffic.","title":"Batch size"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#Coalescing","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"By default the scheduler coalesces: requests to the same model that are queued together are packed into one dispatch, so where a request's rows land in the batch, and which other requests' rows sit beside them, depends on traffic. Even with one compiled program, a kernel is not guaranteed to give bit-identical results for a row in every slot.\n\nscheduler.coalescing: false serves exactly one request per dispatch. With batch_sizes: largest as well, a request always runs the same program with its rows in the same slots, followed by the same zero padding, so its result no longer depends on anything another client sends. The cost is throughput: each request pays for a full padded batch on its own.\n\nWhat remains is set by the request itself. A client's pre-batched request runs as one dispatch, so whether one of its rows is affected by the other rows the client put beside it still depends on the model; the same request always gives the same result. A request larger than the largest compiled size is split into the same pieces every time, and each piece runs alone.\n\nThe gateway does not yet know whether its workers coalesce: under lpt_packing its default routing_fill_mode: run still concentrates a model's requests on one replica at a time. That changes no outputs, but it can queue requests on one GPU while another sits idle; prefer routing_fill_mode: spread for a fleet with coalescing off.","title":"Coalescing"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#Nondeterministic-kernels","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"Some GPU kernels are not deterministic from run to run: a reduction or scatter that accumulates with atomic additions sums in whatever order the threads finish, so the same program on the same input can return different low bits. The XLA option xla_gpu_exclude_nondeterministic_ops asks XLA to leave such implementations out of the compiled program. Set it through runtime.xla_flags; the regulated profile sets it for you.","title":"Nondeterministic kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#Compilation-and-autotuning","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"When XLA compiles a program for a GPU it autotunes by default (runtime.autotune: true): it times several candidate kernels for each matrix multiplication and convolution and keeps the fastest. Autotuning improves performance greatly, but it is also a source of non-determinism. Timing is noisy, so even with everything else held constant (the same image, bundle, GPU, driver, and settings), two compiles of the same model can choose different algorithms, and different algorithms can give different low bits.\n\nA choice, once made, is held by two caches:\n\nThe executable cache (runtime.executable_cache, on by default) stores each compiled program, kernel choices included, in the bundle's .cache/ directory and loads it on later starts, so a restart does not autotune again. A program is compiled fresh whenever its entry is missing: on the first start, for a new bundle directory (each model version has its own), for a bundle copied without its .cache/, on a new host or volume, and after an image upgrade or a change to the compile settings.\nThe autotune cache (runtime.autotune_cache, runtime.autotune_cache_dir; /var/cache/reactant-compile in the image) stores the timing decisions themselves, so a fresh compile reuses earlier choices instead of measuring again.\n\nWith autotuning on, make both caches persistent and back them up. Neither failure is visible: a lost cache does not stop a worker, it only autotunes again and may choose differently, which is a change to revalidate. In practice:\n\nMount the autotune cache directory on a persistent volume, and keep each bundle directory writable and persistent (see Container settings for the mounts).\nBack up the autotune cache and the bundles' .cache/ directories as part of the validation record, and restore them, rather than letting a new or rebuilt host autotune from scratch.\nAfter a restart, check that every program loaded from the executable cache: the worker logs executable cache: hit for each, and worker_executable_cache_misses_total stays at zero.\n\nruntime.autotune: false removes the dependence on timing altogether: XLA uses its default kernels, the same on every compile, at some cost in speed.","title":"Compilation and autotuning"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#The-software-stack","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"Every component between the bundle and the GPU can change numerics: Reactant and its XLA build, the CUDA libraries, and the driver. The release image pins all of them except the host's driver:\n\nEach image is built from a tagged release and its locked deploy/Manifest.toml, which fixes Reactant and Reactant_jll by version and content hash, together with the CUDA userspace.\nDeploy by digest (ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver@sha256:...) or by release tag (:0.1.0), never by :latest, and verify the image's build attestation before deploying it. See The container image.\nKeep the host's NVIDIA driver fixed across the validated fleet, and treat a driver upgrade as a change to validate.\nBefore moving to a new release, read its page under Container Releases, which lists what changed from the release before it. A release that changes Julia, the base image, CUDA, or the compiler and runtime packages calls for revalidation; one that changes only ReactantServer's own packages does not change the numerics stack.","title":"The software stack"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#The-regulated-profile","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"runtime.profile: regulated applies the settings above that have a safe default:\n\nruntime.batch_sizes: largest\nruntime.xla_flags: {xla_gpu_exclude_nondeterministic_ops: true}\nscheduler.coalescing: false\n\nAnything set explicitly wins, so either piece can be turned back off. The profile does not set runtime.numerics, because the right mode is the one the deployment was validated with. Set it as well:\n\nglobal:\n  runtime:\n    profile: regulated\n    numerics: tf32   # or f32: whichever the deployment was validated with\n\nor with environment variables:\n\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_PROFILE=regulated\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_NUMERICS=tf32","title":"The regulated profile"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#Startup-evidence","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"Each worker records its effective settings at startup. Keep these log lines with the validation record:\n\nEffective configuration: every resolved setting, including numerics, profile, batch_sizes, coalescing, xla_flags, and autotune.\nTF32 probe: whether TF32 arithmetic is in use on the device, and under f32, that the pin is bit-exact.\nThe REGULATED PROFILE banner: the precision in effect under the regulated profile, as an info line under f32 or tf32 and as a warning under auto, together with batch_sizes, coalescing, and the XLA flags.\nmodel loaded: per model, the compiled batch sizes actually loaded and the numerics outcome (operations pinned, algorithms rewritten or stripped).","title":"Startup evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"reproducibility/#Checklist","page":"Reproducibility & Regulated Use","text":"Serve from a release image pinned by digest or release tag, with its build attestation verified.\nSet runtime.profile: regulated and pin runtime.numerics to the validated mode.\nRun every worker on the validated GPU class and driver version.\nWith autotuning on, keep the executable cache and the autotune cache on persistent storage, back both up with the validation record, and confirm a restart loads every program from the cache. Otherwise set runtime.autotune: false.\nLeave coalescing off, as the profile sets it. If it is turned back on, confirm for each model that a request's output is bit-identical alone and inside a full batch.\nArchive each worker's startup evidence lines.\nRevalidate when the image, the GPU class, the driver, the numerics mode, or a bundle changes.","title":"Checklist"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/serve/#Serve-a-Model","page":"Serve a Model","text":"This page serves a bundle with ReactantServer from a Julia session and sends it a request. It runs on the CPU, so it works on any machine; the same steps serve on a GPU by changing one line of the node file.","title":"Serve a Model"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/serve/#Install","page":"Serve a Model","text":"Add the server, the client, and (for the setup step) the export tooling and Lux:\n\nusing Pkg\nPkg.add([\"ReactantServer\", \"ReactantServerClient\", \"ReactantServerExport\", \"Lux\"])\n\nStart Julia with --threads=auto,1. The server runs each request's pre- and postprocessing on the default thread pool and keeps its device dispatch loop on the interactive thread, so the two overlap. With a single thread the server still works, just without the overlap.","title":"Install"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/serve/#Setup:-a-bundle","page":"Serve a Model","text":"The server needs a model repository: a directory whose subdirectories are bundles. This exports the small MLP from Export a Model to a Bundle into models/mlp:\n\nusing Lux, Random, ReactantServerExport\n\nmodel = Lux.Chain(Lux.Dense(4 => 8, tanh), Lux.Dense(8 => 3))\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.Xoshiro(0), model)\nexport_bundle(\n    :lux, model, ps, st, randn(Float32, 4, 1);\n    dir = joinpath(\"models\", \"mlp\"), name = \"mlp\", batch_sizes = [1, 8]\n)","title":"Setup: a bundle"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/serve/#Describe-the-node","page":"Serve a Model","text":"A server is configured by a node file, a YAML file that describes one machine: its model repository, ports, runtime backend, and workers. It is only configuration; nothing here uses the ReactantServerNode package, which Run a Node introduces. A single worker on the CPU needs only this:\n\nwrite(\n    \"node.yaml\", \"\"\"\n    model_repo: $(abspath(\"models\"))\n    base_port: 8001            # KServe V2 gRPC\n    metrics_base_port: 8002    # /readyz, /healthz, /metrics\n    global:\n      runtime:\n        backend: cpu           # cuda to serve on a GPU\n      endpoints:\n        host: 127.0.0.1\n    workers:\n      - { name: worker0 }\n    \"\"\"\n)\n\nNode Configuration covers every key, including scheduler tuning, on-demand weights, and environment overrides.","title":"Describe the node"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/serve/#Start-the-server","page":"Serve a Model","text":"serve runs one worker from the node file in this Julia process: it compiles every bundle in the repository, and then starts the gRPC server, so it accepts traffic only once its models are ready. With blocking = false it returns a handle instead of serving until interrupted:\n\nusing ReactantServer\n\nserver = serve(\"node.yaml\"; blocking = false)\n\nHealth and metrics are plain HTTP on the metrics port. /readyz answers 200 once the worker is serving:\n\nusing Downloads\n\nDownloads.request(\"http://127.0.0.1:8002/readyz\").status    # 200","title":"Start the server"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/serve/#Send-a-request","page":"Serve a Model","text":"The server speaks the KServe V2 inference API over gRPC, so any KServe or Triton client can talk to it. ReactantServerClient is the Julia one. KServeModel binds a model on a server, InferInput names an input tensor, infer_sync sends one request, and InferOutput reads a named output back. Tensors keep Julia's layout on both ends, batch axis last, so one item for this model is a (4, 1) matrix:\n\nusing ReactantServerClient\n\nkserve_init()\nmlp = KServeModel(\"grpc://127.0.0.1:8001\", \"mlp\")\nx = reshape(Float32[1, 2, 3, 4], 4, 1)                      # (features, batch)\nresponse = infer_sync(mlp, [InferInput(\"input\", x)])\ny = InferOutput(\"output\", response, Float32)                # (3, 1)\n\nThe served program is the traced Lux model, so it agrees with running the model in this session:\n\ny_local = first(model(x, ps, st))\nmaximum(abs, y .- y_local)                                  # ≈ 0\n\nThis one-shot form suits occasional requests. Run Inference covers batched inference over a dataset and the shared-memory transport.","title":"Send a request"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/julia/serve/#Stop","page":"Serve a Model","text":"kserve_shutdown releases the client, and stop! shuts the server down:\n\nkserve_shutdown()\nstop!(server)\n\nFrom a shell, the blocking form serves until interrupted with Ctrl-C:\n\njulia --project --threads=auto,1 -e 'using ReactantServer; serve(\"node.yaml\")'\n\nA single serve is one worker on one device. To run every GPU on a machine from one node file, with restarts and one client endpoint, use the node supervisor: Run a Node.","title":"Stop"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Bundles-and-model.jl","page":"Bundles","text":"A model is delivered to the server as a self-contained bundle: a directory holding a compiled model (an MLIR module Reactant compiles, a StableHLO program today), its weights, and a manifest. Bundles are produced offline by the conversion tooling and loaded at server startup.","title":"Bundles & model.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Bundle-layout","page":"Bundles","text":"A bundle is a directory containing:\n\nmanifest.yaml - the metadata parsed into a Manifest: I/O specs, dtypes, shapes, and the compiled batch sizes.\nmodel.mlir - the MLIR module Reactant compiles, currently a serialized StableHLO portable artifact (single batch size), or one module per size as model.b{N}.mlir sharing a single weights.safetensors.\nweights.safetensors - the model weights, read with pread at load time (see Weights file layout). A manifest may name another file in the bundle with weights_file (a migrated copy is weights.T.safetensors).\nmodel.jl - optional; registers custom pre/post-processing (see below).\n.cache/ - written by the worker, never by you: the serialized-executable cache (compiled programs keyed by MLIR content, Reactantjll build and device) plus `mlirhashes.json. Safe to delete at any time; the watcher ignores it. Seeruntime.executablecache` in [Node Configuration](nodeconfig.md).\n\nThe directory name is the model name: renaming the directory renames the model, with no edits to the manifest or model.jl (a name declared in either is informational and ignored). Each immediate subdirectory of model_repo that contains a manifest.yaml is a bundle. In dynamic mode a renamed bundle directory (same contents) is detected by the watcher and renamed in place, keeping the compiled executables and resident weights; only new or changed bundles are compiled.","title":"Bundle layout"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Manifest-shape-encoding","page":"Bundles","text":"Each tensor's shape is an einsum-style string of single ASCII letters, one per axis, with a companion dims: map giving the size of every non-batch letter:\n\nexecutable_inputs:\n  - name: input\n    dtype: f32\n    shape: \"chwn\"     # channel, height, width, batch\n    dims:\n      c: 3\n      h: 224\n      w: 224\n\nThe letters n and b are reserved batch markers (at most one occurrence per tensor). Other letters are tensor-scoped (no implicit cross-tensor equality) and must be unique within a single shape. A size of -1 in dims marks a variable axis, a Dim with kind VARIABLE: a dynamic non-batch axis. On client_outputs that pass through model.jl that is how a result with a data-dependent extent is declared, for example the detection count of a postprocessed detector. On executable inputs a variable axis is servable only when the compiled variants are enumerated in a top-level input_shapes block (see write_bundle below); the Object Detection example compiles one weight set for several image shapes this way.\n\nThe per-input batch axis is derived from the position of n/b. When every executable input and output has a batch axis, the scheduler coalesces queued requests along it and pads the dispatch with zero rows up to the smallest compiled size that fits, so a request may carry any number of rows up to the largest of batching.compiled_batch_sizes; otherwise the request's size along that axis must equal one of them. runtime.batch_sizes: largest loads only the largest size (see Node Configuration). Each tensor parses into a TensorSpec with a Dim per axis, and the compiled sizes form the BatchingSpec. The writer stamps format_version: \"2.1\" (\"2.0\" with layout = :legacy); the loader accepts 2.1, 2.0 or 2. Version 2.1 exists so that servers older than 0.2, which would misread weights in the program's axis order, refuse such a bundle instead.\n\nShapes use the Julia column-major convention (the batch dimension is the last axis), which is the reverse of the row-major form a Python/XLA exporter would write. The wire codec handles the conversion, so KServe clients see canonical row-major shapes.","title":"Manifest shape encoding"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Weights-file-layout","page":"Bundles","text":"weights.safetensors stores each weight as the Julia array the server passes to the program. A file has one of two layouts, declared in its own metadata, never inferred from the manifest or the file name:\n\nProgram axis order (axis_order: xla in the safetensors metadata, written by ReactantServerExport 2.0). Each tensor's header shape is the program's shape (the reversed Julia shape) and its data is row-major in that shape, which is byte for byte the Julia array. The server reads each tensor straight into its destination with one pread. numpy, PyTorch and SafeTensors.jl read these tensors in the program's shape.\nLegacy (no axis_order key, written before ReactantServerExport 2.0). Each header shape is the Julia shape and the data is permuted to row-major, so every load permutes it back, an extra full copy of every tensor.\n\nBoth layouts load to identical arrays. Any other axis_order value is refused.","title":"Weights file layout"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Migrating-older-bundles","page":"Bundles","text":"runtime.weights_migration (see Node Configuration) converts legacy bundles at load. In copy mode the worker writes weights.T.safetensors beside the original and adds weights_file: weights.T.safetensors to the manifest, leaving weights.safetensors untouched, so rolling back to an older server needs nothing. In replace mode it first does the same, then sets format_version: \"2.1\", renames the copy over weights.safetensors in one step, and removes the extra name and the weights_file line.\n\nNothing is accepted without proof. A copy must hold the same tensor names, dtypes, byte counts and metadata as the original, each header shape reversed, and every tensor equal byte for byte (so NaN payloads and signed zeros count) to the array the legacy path reads, which is read twice, once by the server and once by SafeTensors.jl. The copy records the original's size, modification time and SHA-256. Before replace swaps it in, the copy is verified again against the original, and the file left under the original's name is verified once more against the original (still open, so its bytes remain readable) before anything else is removed.\n\nFiles are written under temporary names, fsynced, verified from disk, and renamed into place, so a crash at any point leaves a bundle that loads correctly and the migration resumes on the next start. Workers on one node take turns on a lock in the bundle's .cache/, so a bundle is migrated once. A migration that cannot run (no free space, a read-only directory, an invalid copy) is logged and the bundle loads as it was.\n\nIf you redeploy weights.safetensors after a copy migration, the copy is stale: the server notices that the original changed since the copy was made, loads the original, and, with migration on, rebuilds the copy. Comments and formatting in manifest.yaml are kept; only the weights_file and format_version lines change.\n\ntools/migrate_weights.jl runs the same migration offline, for model directories the workers cannot write or to convert ahead of a deploy.\n\nDatatypes are written as manifest tokens (f32, bf16, i64, bool, and so on); see DType for the full mapping between tokens, Julia types, and KServe wire strings. Client-facing tensors must use a dtype that has a KServe wire mapping (FP8 is executable-only).","title":"Migrating older bundles"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Custom-pre/post-processing-with-model.jl","page":"Bundles","text":"A bundle may include a model.jl that calls register_model to attach preprocess and postprocess hooks. Both hooks receive and return a Vector{NamedTensor} (see NamedTensor); omitted hooks default to identity.\n\n# model.jl, inside the bundle directory\nusing ReactantServer\n\nfunction normalize(inputs)\n    # inputs :: Vector{NamedTensor}; transform and return a Vector{NamedTensor}\n    return inputs\nend\n\nfunction to_classes(outputs)\n    # e.g. map logits to class ids\n    return outputs\nend\n\nregister_model(\"resnet50\"; preprocess=normalize, postprocess=to_classes)\n\nThe worker runs the hooks on each request's own task (preprocess before the request is queued, postprocess on the result), crossing the world-age boundary with invokelatest. This means the hooks for different requests run concurrently, on multiple threads, overlapping the GPU execution: keep them free of shared mutable state (or guard it yourself). When model.jl transforms the I/O, declare the client-facing tensors via client_inputs / client_outputs in the manifest; without a model.jl those keys are not permitted and the executable specs are the client-facing specs. See register_model in the API reference for the exact hook signatures.\n\nFor a bundle whose model.jl chains several models with data-dependent logic rather than wrapping one executable, see Meta Models. That feature is experimental and its design is subject to change; prefer a single program with the fixed-size result pattern below where possible.","title":"Custom pre/post-processing with model.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Variable-length-results","page":"Bundles","text":"A StableHLO program has static output shapes, so a model whose result count depends on the data (detections, matches, tokens above a threshold) returns a fixed-size buffer plus a count: the program fills the first n rows of a buffer sized to the configured maximum, zeroes the rest, and returns n as a second output. A model.jl postprocess hook trims the buffer, so clients receive exactly the rows that exist:\n\n# model.jl: DETECTIONS is (columns, max_detections), NUM_DETECTIONS is Int64[n]\nfunction _trim(outputs)\n    by = Dict(t.name => t.data for t in outputs)\n    n = Int(by[\"NUM_DETECTIONS\"][1])\n    return [ReactantServer.NamedTensor(\"OUTPUT__0\", by[\"DETECTIONS\"][:, 1:n])]\nend\n\nregister_model(basename(@__DIR__); postprocess = _trim)\n\nThe manifest declares the executable outputs at their fixed sizes and the trimmed client output with a variable axis (-1) under client_outputs. Inside the program, variable selections become masks over fixed-size arrays rather than findall; the two-stage detectors are the worked example, see Object Detection.","title":"Variable-length results"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Producing-bundles","page":"Bundles","text":"ReactantServerExport produces bundles offline and is kept out of the server's dependency graph. It is not part of the server runtime.\n\nA project that owns a Lux model (or any Reactant-traceable function) uses ReactantServerExport; Lux itself is not a dependency of the package. Export a Model walks through exporting a Lux model end to end.\n\nThe :lux frontend, export_bundle(:lux, model, ps, st, example_input; dir, name, batch_sizes), traces model(x, ps, st) at each requested batch size, taking the first return as the output; the batch dimension is the last Julia axis (the Lux convention) and the leading network axis. A multi-input / multi-output variant takes example_inputs::Tuple and output_select mapping the raw model output to the ordered tuple of arrays to export; per-tensor batch axes default to each array's last Julia axis, overridable with input_batch_axes / output_batch_axes (1-based Julia axes, or nothing to opt one tensor out of batching).\n\nThe :reactant frontend is the generic form: any Reactant-traceable f(inputs..., weights...) with explicit name => array weight pairs, producing a single unbatched model.mlir:\n\nexport_bundle(:reactant, f, (x,), [(\"w1\", w1), (\"b1\", b1)];\n    dir=\"bundles/mlp\", name=\"mlp\")\n\nThe Reactant frontends (:lux, :reactant, and export_two_stage_detector) take compile_options, the options Reactant.@compile accepts, applied when the model is traced to StableHLO. Pass them as a NamedTuple or a Reactant.CompileOptions:\n\nexport_bundle(:lux, model, ps, st, x; dir=\"bundles/net\", name=\"net\",\n    compile_options=(; raise=true))   # raise KernelAbstractions / CUDA.jl kernels to StableHLO\n\nNon-default options are recorded under compile_options in the manifest's provenance. An unknown option name is an error, and the exporter keeps client and drop_unsupported_attributes for itself. A traced program that still contains Reactant-internal enzyme.* ops, which XLA cannot compile (for example with optimize = false), is refused before anything is written. The PyTorch frontends lower through JAX and do not take compile_options.\n\nA PyTorch project also loads PythonCall, which triggers the package extension driving torch.export.export and torchax:\n\nusing ReactantServerExport, PythonCall\nexport_bundle(:pytorch, model, (example_input,);\n    dir=\"bundles/mlp\", name=\"mlp\", batch_sizes=[1, 8])\n\nThe :lux and :pytorch frontends trace once per requested batch size and write a server-loadable bundle. The batch dimension is the last Julia axis (the leading PyTorch axis after the row-major / column-major reversal). export_bundle(:torchscript, ...) exports a TorchScript artifact (a .pt file or a loaded ScriptModule) through the same extension; without PythonCall the :pytorch and :torchscript calls fail with a message directing you to load it.","title":"Producing bundles"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#write_bundle-and-IOSpec","page":"Bundles","text":"write_bundle is the low-level writer: it takes the executable IOSpecs, the StableHLO modules (one per batch size, or text or serialized bytes), and an ordered weights collection whose order becomes the safetensors argument_order, and emits manifest.yaml, the model[.b{N}].mlir files, and weights.safetensors (in the program's axis order unless layout = :legacy; see Weights file layout). read_bundle_weights reads a bundle's weights back in the program's axis order, whichever layout they use. An IOSpec names a tensor, its Julia (column-major) shape, its dtype, the 0-based batch_axis in that Julia shape (by convention the last axis), and optional explicit letters for the non-batch axes (the reserved markers n/b are rejected); a -1 axis size encodes a variable axis. The bundle directory's basename must equal name (the identity rule above).\n\nWith input_shapes given, the writer marks the variable executable-input axes -1 in the manifest and emits one module set per compiled variant as model.v{i}[.b{N}].mlir, all sharing the single weights.safetensors; each variant lists the variable-axis sizes in (input, axis) order, and the variants must share one set of batch sizes. client_inputs / client_outputs (each nothing or a Vector{IOSpec}) declare the wire-facing spec when it differs from the executable spec, for bundles whose model.jl transforms between the two; they are emitted only when given.\n\ncollect_provenance captures best-effort reproducibility metadata for a bundle (repo remote, commit, tree, branch, dirty flag, Julia and exporter versions, and an exported_at timestamp). export_bundle merges it into the manifest's provenance block; on a dirty work tree the uncommitted diff is written to the bundle as working_tree.patch rather than embedded in the manifest, and git_commit plus git apply --binary working_tree.patch reconstructs the exported code exactly.\n\nThe test suite also builds small bundles directly; see test/stablehlo_fixtures.jl.","title":"write_bundle and IOSpec"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Checking-that-a-bundle-is-servable","page":"Bundles","text":"A bundle is served as executable(inputs..., weights...), so the compiled program must take exactly as many arguments as the manifest declares inputs plus the weights file holds tensors. Both of those halves are readable, and the number the executable actually wants is in neither: it lives inside model*.mlir, which is a serialized vhlo artifact rather than text. A bundle can therefore be internally inconsistent while every readable part of it looks correct, and the symptom is that the model registers and serves and then fails every inference with\n\nINVALID_ARGUMENT: Execution supplied 216 arguments but compiled program expected 217\n\nexport_bundle checks this itself and refuses to write a bundle whose graph disagrees with it, naming both numbers and the usual cause. The usual cause is a device-resident value reachable from the traced closure: Reactant lifts every one of those into an argument whether the program reads it or not, so an RNG in layer state (which appears as a leading tensor<2xui64>) or a device-resident configuration value captured by the model becomes an argument no client can supply. Reading such values back to the host before tracing bakes them into the graph as constants instead.\n\nFor a bundle written by other tooling, assert_bundle_arity reads the same three numbers back out of the artifact:\n\nusing ReactantServerExport\n\nassert_bundle_arity(\"export_out/my_model_v1\")          # raises if the bundle is unservable\nr = bundle_arity_report(\"export_out/my_model_v1\")      # the numbers, without raising\nr.servable, r.n_inputs, r.n_weights, r.expected\n\nbundle_arity_report never raises, so it can be run across a directory of bundles to triage them, and bundle_entry_arity reads one module's arity on its own. All three read the artifact rather than the process that produced it, so they hold for a bundle from any writer.","title":"Checking that a bundle is servable"},{"category":"section","location":"bundles/#Related-pages","page":"Bundles","text":"The manifest and boundary types are documented on the API page: Manifest, TensorSpec, Dim, BatchingSpec, load_manifest, DType, and NamedTensor. export_bundle, write_bundle, IOSpec, collect_provenance, assert_bundle_arity, bundle_arity_report, and bundle_entry_arity are documented in the ReactantServerExport docstrings. The tutorials walk the full export-to-serve path, and Node Configuration covers how the server loads and watches a repository of bundles.","title":"Related pages"},{"category":"section","location":"monitoring/#Monitoring","page":"Monitoring","text":"One scrape on :8002 covers everything. With multiple workers, the embedded gateway serves its own gateway_* series and fans out to each worker's metrics endpoint, merging them into a single exposition; with a single worker, :8002 is that worker's own /metrics. Each worker tags its series with worker and gpu labels itself (the gpu value is the physical device behind its CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES), so per-GPU and per-worker breakdowns need no Prometheus relabeling, for example sum by (gpu) (rate(worker_dispatch_total[1m])).\n\nA ready-to-run Prometheus + Grafana stack lives under config/monitoring/ with a seven-dashboard suite. Its Prometheus scrapes host.docker.internal:8002, the metrics port the node container publishes on the host; if the node runs on a different host, edit the target in prometheus.yml to that host's address:8002. Grafana is at http://<host>:3000 (anonymous viewing on; admin / admin to edit) and Prometheus at http://<host>:9090. See config/monitoring/README.md for the compose commands and dashboards.","title":"Monitoring"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/examples/object_detection/#Serve-an-Object-Detector","page":"Serve an Object Detector","text":"This tutorial runs a real model end to end: it exports a torchvision Faster R-CNN, pretrained on COCO, into one ReactantServer bundle, serves it, sends it a photo, and draws the detected objects back onto the image. It runs on the CPU, so it needs no GPU, and the same steps serve on a GPU by dropping one flag. How the detector's data-dependent middle becomes one static program is covered in the Object Detection reference.\n\nYou need a clone of the repository (the example's scripts live in examples/object_detection/) and network access for the first export, which downloads the pretrained weights and the Python packages the PyTorch export uses.\n\nThe demo model is named object_detector, configured by examples/object_detection/detector.convert.yaml:\n\noutput_root: bundles\nreport_path: bundles/conversion_report.md\n\nhandlers:\n  - file: ../../tools/handlers/torchvision_frcnn_detector.jl\n    models: [object_detector]\n    options:\n      weights: DEFAULT     # pretrained COCO weights (downloaded by torchvision on first export)\n      num_classes: 91      # COCO classes including background\n      image_size: 640      # canonical square input edge (divisible by 64)\n      input_dtype: u8      # client sends a uint8 image; stage1 bakes /255 + ImageNet normalize\n      output_cols: 6       # per detection: [x1, y1, x2, y2, score, class]\n\nThe example is split into three single-purpose Julia environments so each loads only what it needs (and they stop invalidating each other's precompilation): export holds the export tooling with PythonCall and torch, server holds ReactantServer, and client holds ReactantServerClient and CairoMakie, with neither Reactant nor torch. Each environment resolves independently the first time you use it:\n\nfor env in export server client; do\n  julia --project=examples/object_detection/$env -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\ndone\n\nThen run the three steps in order (the server stays running; drive it from a second terminal):\n\n# 1. Export the bundle (first time only; writes ./bundles/). Needs network for the COCO weights.\njulia --project=examples/object_detection/export \\\n    examples/object_detection/export/export.jl\n\n# 2. Serve it on the CPU (blocks; Ctrl-C to stop). On a GPU host, drop --cpu and pick a device\n#    with CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0.\njulia --project=examples/object_detection/server \\\n    examples/object_detection/server/serve.jl --cpu\n\n# 3. In another terminal: send an image and draw the result -> ./detections.jpg\njulia --project=examples/object_detection/client \\\n    examples/object_detection/client/detect.jl\n\nPass your own image to step 3 as the first argument (a local path). The server port defaults to 8080; set OD_PORT (and OD_HOST for the client) to change it on both step 2 and step 3.","title":"Serve an Object Detector"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/examples/object_detection/#Export","page":"Serve an Object Detector","text":"The export writes the object_detector bundle to examples/object_detection/bundles/, and skips the work when the bundle already exists; delete bundles/ to export again. The first run downloads the pretrained COCO weights and, through CondaPkg, the Python packages the export uses. Behind a proxy that intercepts TLS, set SSL_CERT_FILE to a CA bundle that trusts it; by default the script uses the system bundle.","title":"Export"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/examples/object_detection/#Serve","page":"Serve an Object Detector","text":"The server compiles the detector before it accepts requests: about half a minute on the CPU of a large workstation, and longer on a laptop.","title":"Serve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/examples/object_detection/#Client","page":"Serve an Object Detector","text":"The client resizes the image to 640x640, sends it, and writes examples/object_detection/detections.jpg with the boxes and labels drawn. Without an image argument it downloads a test photo (https://ultralytics.com/images/bus.jpg).\n\nEach output row is [x1, y1, x2, y2, score, class], with boxes in the 640x640 input's pixels and class a COCO category id. The model drops detections scoring below 0.05, and the client draws those scoring 0.5 or more.","title":"Client"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#On-demand-Weights","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"Because only one model executes at a time, the GPU does not need every model's weights resident simultaneously. A GPU sized for a handful of models can therefore serve a much larger catalog, paying a small transfer cost only when a cold model is first called. This page is the operational guide to the weight memory system: the two memory tiers, the self-sizing probe that budgets them, residency control, node-shared host weights, BFC fragmentation and compaction, and the observability that confirms it all. Dispatch and batching are the job of Scheduling; the reverse proxy and its placement policy live on Multi-GPU Gateway.","title":"On-demand Weights"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Two-tiers-of-weight-memory","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"Host RAM (a budgeted cache). runtime.host_weight_budget sets how much host RAM may hold copies of weights between GPU loads. The default is 0: no copies, so a model reloaded onto the GPU reads its weights file from disk. Give it a budget when models are swapped, so reloads copy from RAM instead (see Host RAM budget and Use Cases). A model with residency: system is always kept in host RAM.\nGPU (managed working set). Pinned models keep their weights on the GPU for the server's lifetime. Every other model is loaded onto the GPU on demand when a request arrives, kept resident afterward so repeat requests are free, and evicted under a configured GPU byte budget by the cost-aware admission described in Memory fragmentation and compaction. Eviction frees the device memory immediately through an explicit PJRT buffer release (ReactantServer.free_weights!).\n\nWhen the weights are already in RAM, an on-demand GPU load is a single host-to-device transfer (ReactantServer.transfer_to_device) rather than a reload from disk, so its cost is the model's weight size over the host-to-device bandwidth.","title":"Two tiers of weight memory"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Enabling-it","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"The main setting is runtime.device_weight_fraction, the share of the BFC arena (device_memory_fraction * device memory) that holds resident model weights, pinned plus on-demand. It defaults to 1.0 (use the whole arena, GPU only) and must be greater than 0: models that do not fit in it are swapped on and off the GPU. You normally do not set it at all. (In 0.1 the three device settings were mem_fraction, weight_cache_fraction and weight_cache_wiggle_fraction; they are now device_memory_fraction, device_weight_fraction and device_headroom_fraction, and the old names are rejected at startup.)\n\nglobal:\n  runtime:\n    backend: cuda\n    device_weight_fraction: 1.0    # default: use the whole arena, self-sized (see below)\n    device_headroom_fraction: 0.1  # default: keep 10% of the arena free as headroom\n\nThese are the device_weight_fraction / device_headroom_fraction fields of RuntimeConfig, also settable via INFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_DEVICE_WEIGHT_FRACTION and INFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_DEVICE_HEADROOM_FRACTION environment variables. The cache is GPU-only; on CPU there is no arena, so weights stay resident regardless.","title":"Enabling it"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Self-sizing:-pinned-scratch-headroom","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"The fraction is a ceiling on weight memory, not a promise it all fits as cache: each execution also needs transient device memory (activations, conv/GEMM workspace, IO) on top of resident weights, and pinned models occupy the arena too. The worker resolves the on-demand budget for you at startup with ReactantServer.weight_budget. It probes each model once in isolation (loads and runs it), reads the allocator's peak device usage to measure the worst-case scratch, and sets\n\non_demand_budget = min(fraction*arena, (1-headroom)*arena - max_scratch) - pinned_weights\n\nso pinned weights reserve their share, the measured scratch and the headroom are held back, and the rest is the weight cache. When everything fits, the cache simply holds all weights resident (no eviction); when it does not, it is memory-safe. The probe is bounded one-time startup work and doubles as an execution smoke test; the resolved budget is logged. Pinned weights or scratch large enough to leave no room are logged as a warning, since no sizing can fix that.\n\nThe measurement is exact per model when the backend can reset the allocator's high-water mark, which the Reactant backend can on a CUDA device (through the allocator's ClearMemoryStats): before each model the peak is reset, so peak - in_use_before is that model's own transient, and nothing that happened earlier, in particular XLA's autotuning scratch during compile (many times a model's real run scratch), can inflate it. Pinned models are measured too. A model that cannot be run at startup falls back to the compiler's static scratch accounting (XLA's compiled memory statistics), padded. The same measurement runs when the directory watcher loads a model into a running worker, and the budget is re-resolved on the spot, so neither a fresh autotune nor a hot-loaded model requires a restart to be sized correctly. Without a resettable peak (any non-CUDA device) the probe falls back to an ordering-based estimate that is exact for a clean session but inherits whatever moved the peak before it ran.","title":"Self-sizing: pinned + scratch + headroom"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Pinning-hot-models","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"A model that must never pay the on-demand transfer cost can be pinned to stay GPU-resident for the server's lifetime. Pinned models are exempt from eviction; the budget bounds only the unpinned working set.\n\nscheduler:\n  models:\n    resnet50:\n      residency: device      # pin_to_gpu: true is a back-compat alias\n\nThis is the residency field of ModelSchedConfig (unpinned, system, or device; an unspecified model is swapped on demand with host copies governed by the host RAM budget, or pinned in RAM when that budget is unlimited; see ResidencyState). Pin the latency-sensitive or highest-traffic models, and let the long tail load on demand.","title":"Pinning hot models"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Sharing-host-weights-across-same-node-workers","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"With several workers on one node, runtime.shared_host_weights: true backs each model's host copy (pinned or cached) with a node-shared POSIX shared-memory region so the workers share one copy. The default PrivateWeightStore gives each worker its own copy; SharedWeightStore places one content-addressed region per model in /dev/shm, coordinated peer-to-peer through an advisory flock, so same-node workers attach one copy and each transfers it to its own device. host_materialize routes through the configured store, which is what makes this a configuration decision rather than a code change.\n\nEach shared copy is labeled with the version of the weights file the worker actually opened (its device, inode, size and modification time), so workers never share a copy of one version under another version's label. Deploy new weights by writing a new file and renaming it over the old one: a worker that already loaded the model keeps serving the old bytes until it reloads, and its shared copy stays labeled as the old version. Writing into a loaded weights file in place (for example cp onto it, which truncates it first) is detected and fails that model's load instead of copying mixed bytes; weights are read with ordinary file reads rather than a memory mapping, so a truncated file is an error rather than a crashed worker. When the node cannot back a new shared copy (/dev/shm full, or the container's memory limit reached), the worker logs a warning and gives that model a private copy instead of failing.\n\nThe regions and their lock files are created with mode 666 by default so containers running as unrelated UIDs can share them; that is world-writable, so set runtime.shared_host_weights_mode: \"660\" on production and multi-user systems (the server warns at startup when the shared store runs with the 666 default). Both fields belong to RuntimeConfig, with INFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_SHARED_HOST_WEIGHTS and INFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_SHARED_HOST_WEIGHTS_MODE environment overrides.","title":"Sharing host weights across same-node workers"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Host-RAM-budget","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"runtime.host_weight_budget caps the host RAM that holds copies of weights between GPU loads:\n\nglobal:\n  runtime:\n    host_weight_budget: 48g   # default 0; or 512m, 1.5t, unlimited\n\nSizes take an optional unit, case insensitive: b, k, m, g, t, or the long forms (gb, gib); every unit is binary, so 5g and 5GB are both 5 GiB. The default 0 keeps no copies: weights are in host RAM only while a load reads them and transfers them to the device, which suits a deployment whose models all stay on the GPU. unlimited keeps every model's copy with no cap (the behavior before 0.2).\n\nThe host tier is a cache. A GPU load uses the model's cached host copy when there is one, and otherwise reads the weights file and keeps the copy if the budget has room, evicting the least recently loaded copies first. Only a load from the cache counts as use: requests served by a model already on the device do not, so a model that stays on a GPU for hours ages out of host RAM (nothing needs its host copy while it sits on the device), and the models the GPUs load most often stay cached. Being on a device gives no protection. A model with residency: system keeps its guarantee: it stays in RAM, is never evicted, and counts against the budget. When pinned models alone exceed the budget they load anyway and the worker warns that it is overcommitted. A load that misses the cache reads the whole model and queues every transfer at once, the same as an unbudgeted load; those temporary arrays are freed by the GC and are not counted.\n\nHow the budget is shared depends on shared_host_weights:\n\nShared (true): one budget for the whole node. Every worker may use all of it, each copy lives once in /dev/shm however many workers load from it, and the copies are the ledger: a worker sums the regions under a node-wide lock (/dev/shm/rsw-budget.lock, held only for that bookkeeping, never across a disk read) and evicts copies no worker is using. A worker holds a copy only while transferring from it, so a copy in use is never evicted. Copies survive worker restarts, so the cache is warm after a restart, and each worker trims the node to the budget at startup.\nPrivate (false): each worker keeps its own copies and gets an even share, host_weight_budget ÷ host_weight_budget_workers, with no coordination. host_weight_budget_workers defaults to 1; the node supervisor sets it to the node's worker count, so set it yourself only when running several workers without the supervisor.\n\nAt startup, after measuring every model, the worker loads as many on-demand models onto the GPU as fit, largest first, so a deployment whose models all fit never reads a weights file after startup. After that, a model reloaded from disk because it had no host copy logs a model weights reloaded from disk warning, once per model, so slow swaps have a visible cause.\n\nThe budget applies to GPU workers; a CPU worker keeps every model's weights resident, so the setting is ignored there (with a warning when it is not 0).","title":"Host RAM budget"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Memory-fragmentation-and-compaction","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"When you size a GPU to squeeze the largest possible working set onto it, fragmentation becomes a real constraint. The device pool is one BFC (best-fit-with-coalescing) arena, claimed up front when runtime.preallocate: true. As the weight cache loads and evicts models of different sizes over time, the freed regions return to the arena's free list but do not always sit next to each other, so the arena can hold plenty of free bytes in total yet have no single gap large enough for the next model's weights. The load then fails or forces extra eviction even though the memory is technically there. The tighter you pack the GPU, the sooner this bites.\n\nPinned models are not the problem here: they are loaded once at startup, before any on-demand traffic, so they sit at the base of the arena and never move. Fragmentation accumulates only in the on-demand working set above them. Compaction targets exactly that region. It frees every resident on-demand weight buffer at once, so the allocator coalesces the now-contiguous free space back into one large region above the pinned base. Pinned models are left in place: they are never freed and never re-read from disk. Host floors (the system-pinned RAM copies) are also left untouched, so when an on-demand model reloads it is a fast host-to-device transfer rather than a disk re-materialization (ReactantServer.compact! implements this on the worker).","title":"Memory fragmentation and compaction"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Admission:-evicting-only-what-a-load-needs","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"Before a worker loads a model it checks, from the allocator's own statistics, that the model's weights and the scratch of the heaviest resident execution will both fit, in bytes and contiguously, and evicts only what is needed to make that true. It decides before it uploads because a refused device allocation is slow to discover: XLA's allocator waits about 10 seconds for memory to be released before it reports one.\n\nFeasibility. A model that cannot fit even with every evictable model gone (weights plus the device-pinned models plus scratch exceed the device) is rejected at once, without evicting anything.\nRoom for the weights. Free memory must hold the weights plus the largest scratch any resident model needs, the largest free block must hold all of the weights, and the on-demand byte budget must allow them. \"Hold\" follows the allocator's split rule: it splits a free block for a tensor only when the block is at least twice the tensor or the remainder is at least 128 MiB, so a block of exactly the weights' size can be too small. Admission replays that rule over the model's tensors in upload order. A byte shortfall evicts the cheapest set of models that covers it; a contiguity shortfall evicts the cheapest single model at a time.\nUpload, then the scratch guarantee: the largest free block must still hold the largest scratch of every resident model, the new one included, so every resident model can run. Admission evicts until it does.\n\nWhich models to evict is a cost decision. Each model's priority is its expected reload time per byte, scaled by how often it has been dispatched since it was loaded and aged by how long ago it was last used (GreedyDual-Size-Frequency). A costly, frequently used model stays; one nobody has called in a while goes, and among equals the least recently used goes first, as with LRU. Choosing a set rather than evicting one model at a time means one large idle model is evicted in place of two smaller busy ones. Models with requests already queued are evicted only when nothing else frees enough. The search is allocation-free and bounded: measured at 64 resident models it takes about 6 to 22 microseconds.\n\nCompaction (below) is the fallback when eviction alone cannot make room: the worker frees every evictable model, reloads the most valuable of them largest first together with the new model, and retries once; if that fails too the request fails with an admission error naming the reason. A former resident is reloaded only if the whole refill, replayed under the allocator's split rule, still leaves the scratch block free; one that fails to reload anyway stays evicted rather than failing the request. Device-pinned models never move, so a pinned model that splits the device can make a large model unplaceable; place pinned models with that in mind. Without device statistics (the CPU backend) only the byte budget applies.\n\nWhatever drives compaction frees the on-demand region the same way; what differs is when it fires and what gets reloaded eagerly. Underneath, compaction is a worker control RPC, CompactMemory on the ControlService, that frees the on-demand region and reloads a list of models (empty means free only); both the standalone trigger and the gateway use it. There are two ways to drive it, matched to the two deployment shapes.","title":"Admission: evicting only what a load needs"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Standalone-worker-(no-gateway)","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"A worker runs compaction itself on a cadence counted in weight-cache loads, set under scheduler::\n\nscheduler:\n  compaction_interval: 200   # compact every 200 on-demand loads (0 disables, the default)\n\nA load is what places a variable-size weight block on the device, so loads are what fragment the arena; a dispatch to an already-resident model does not, which is why the cadence counts loads rather than requests or time. The worker trigger always refills lazily: it frees the on-demand region and lets models come back as requests arrive. It is off by default, and a gateway-fronted worker should leave it off, so the gateway is the sole authority on compaction in a gateway deployment (next section). It is the compaction_interval field of SchedulerConfig, also exposed as INFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_COMPACTION_INTERVAL.","title":"Standalone worker (no gateway)"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Behind-a-gateway","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"In a gateway deployment workers leave scheduler.compaction_interval at 0. By default the gateway leaves compaction to each worker's admission as well. It can instead tie compaction to placement changes, the fleet-level event that churns worker memory: when the lpt_packing scheduler repacks and moves a model from one worker to another, the new worker loads it and the old worker's copy goes cold. Two settings under the gateway's scheduling: block control it:\n\nscheduling:\n  mode: lpt_packing\n  compaction_mode: off         # off (default) | lazy | preload\n  compaction_interval: 1       # every placement-changing repack (see below)\n\ncompaction_mode selects whether a repack compacts each worker whose placement changed, and what that worker reloads afterwards:\n\noff (the default) sends nothing on a repack. Each worker's admission evicts only what a load needs and compacts by itself only when eviction cannot make a model fit.\nlazy frees the worker's on-demand region and reloads nothing. Models the worker no longer serves are dropped immediately; the ones it still serves reload on their next request, so each pays its upload only if it is actually used.\npreload frees the region and then immediately reloads the models the repack just assigned to that worker, largest first, so the new placement is warm before traffic arrives. The worker's queue waits once while that set uploads. The gateway computes the per-worker list from the placement, which is why this is a gateway setting.\n\nRenamed in 0.2: eager is now lazy, scheduled is now preload, and on_demand is now off (they were the same behavior). The old names fail at startup with the replacement in the error; there is no alias, so update gateway.yml or REACTANT_GATEWAY_SCHEDULING_COMPACTION_MODE before upgrading.\n\ncompaction_interval is the cadence in repacks (default 1, so every placement-changing repack compacts). Because the demand signal is smoothed, most repacks recompute the same placement and do nothing; the counter advances on every repack but the fan-out only fires on the first placement-changing repack at or after the interval, so it can land a little later than exactly N. Both settings are also exposed as REACTANT_GATEWAY_SCHEDULING_COMPACTION_MODE and REACTANT_GATEWAY_SCHEDULING_COMPACTION_INTERVAL. A single client CompactMemory call to the gateway also fans out to every worker on demand, independent of the repack cadence, for a one-off fleet defragment.","title":"Behind a gateway"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Cost-and-when-to-enable","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"When every model fits on the GPU nothing churns, so compaction has no working set to defragment. When it does run it has a cost: freeing the on-demand region drops models that were warm, so they pay a host-to-device reload (on next request for lazy, or up front for preload).\n\nThe default, off, compacts only when a worker's admission cannot otherwise place a model, so models that are still warm are not dropped on every placement change. Watch worker_weight_fallback_compactions_total for how often that happens and worker_weight_admission_failures_total for models that could not be placed at all. lazy compacts every affected worker on every placement-changing repack (compaction_interval: 1), which pays a one-time reload on the first request per moved model; raise compaction_interval to fire it less often, or use preload to warm the new placement up front instead.","title":"Cost and when to enable"},{"category":"section","location":"on_demand_weights/#Observability","page":"On-demand Weights","text":"The ReactantServer.Scheduler exposes weight-cache residency and load/evict counters alongside its dispatch metrics; read them with ReactantServer.scheduler_metrics (ReactantServer.weight_cache_stats is the underlying snapshot). Use them to confirm that hot models stay resident and that the eviction rate is acceptable for your budget. Coalescing (packing many requests into one execution) amortizes a one-time on-demand transfer across every item in the batch, so on-demand loading and batching reinforce each other.\n\nEach compaction also logs a memory compacted line with the device free space and on-demand budget before and after, plus the largest free block and fragmentation before and after, and the weight cache tracks a compactions counter. Compare the before/after largest free block to confirm that compaction actually recovered contiguous space on your hardware: it relies on the allocator returning freed buffers to the arena and coalescing them, which the log lets you verify rather than assume. Models named for reload (preload, or a CompactMemory reload list) are reloaded largest first, so the refill packs tightly instead of leaving gaps.\n\nWith JULIA_DEBUG=ReactantServer, every on-demand load and eviction also logs a memory: weight load or memory: weight evict line with raw byte counts: the model's allocator-rounded weight footprint, its largest single weight tensor, its measured scratch, its average load time, and the device's bytes in use, largest free block and fragmentation before and after. These lines are meant to be parsed into a fragmentation-over-time series.\n\nThe worker also exports the BFC allocator's live numbers as Prometheus gauges (aggregated through the gateway's /metrics): worker_device_memory_in_use_bytes, worker_device_memory_free_bytes, worker_device_memory_peak_in_use_bytes (the session high-water, your empirical scratch + resident ceiling), and worker_device_memory_pool_bytes. The peak gauge is reset once compilation finishes and again before each probed model, so after startup it tracks execution only; a rising peak over a long uptime is the signal to revisit the budget.\n\nFragmentation is observable directly: worker_device_memory_largest_free_block_bytes is the largest single allocation the allocator can satisfy right now, and worker_device_memory_fragmentation is 1 - largest_free_block / free_bytes (0 when the free memory is one block, approaching 1 as it splinters). A model whose largest weight tensor or execution scratch exceeds the largest free block cannot be placed even when the free byte count looks ample, which is the condition compaction (above) repairs. Allocation failures are expensive to discover: XLA's allocator waits about 10 seconds for memory to be released before it reports one.\n\nUnder a host RAM budget, worker_host_weight_budget_bytes is the budget the worker enforces (the node's with shared host weights, its share otherwise), worker_host_weight_cached_bytes the host RAM it counts, and worker_host_weight_hits_total and worker_host_weight_misses_total how many loads came from a cached copy or read the weights file.\n\nPer model, worker_model_scratch_bytes is the scratch measured at startup and worker_model_load_seconds the moving average of its on-demand load time. worker_weight_load_bytes_total divided by worker_weight_load_seconds_total gives the effective host-to-device bandwidth of weight loads.","title":"Observability"},{"category":"section","location":"design/architecture/#Architecture","page":"Architecture","text":"This page describes how the pieces of ReactantServer fit together: the packages, the processes they run as, the path a request takes, and how weights reach a compiled program. Each component has its own page for the detail, linked from the section that introduces it.","title":"Architecture"},{"category":"section","location":"design/architecture/#Packages","page":"Architecture","text":"The project is a set of packages, split so that only the worker and the export tooling load Reactant and XLA; the client, the gateway, and the supervisor install without them:\n\nReactantServerCore: the shared, Reactant-free code the other packages build on: the KServe V2 messages and wire format, and the manifest and node file formats.\nReactantServer: the worker, which runs models through Reactant.\nReactantServerGateway: the reverse proxy that puts several workers behind one endpoint.\nReactantServerClient: the client. It is Reactant-free, so it installs on any machine that sends requests.\nReactantServerNode: the node supervisor, which runs a machine's workers and gateway as subprocesses.\nReactantServerExport: offline export of Lux and PyTorch models into bundles, tracing them with Reactant. It is not a workspace member; the worker reads the bundles it writes, never the package.\n\nThe API page lists every package's documented names.","title":"Packages"},{"category":"section","location":"design/architecture/#Processes","page":"Architecture","text":"A worker drives one device. It serves the full KServe V2 surface on its own, so a single worker needs nothing else.\n\nA node with several devices runs one worker process per device, started and restarted by the node supervisor. With two or more workers the supervisor also starts a gateway process, which gives clients one endpoint and routes each model's requests to the workers that serve it. The gateway forwards inference requests as raw bytes and never decodes a tensor. Deployment describes the supervisor, and Multi-GPU Gateway describes how the gateway places models and routes requests.","title":"Processes"},{"category":"section","location":"design/architecture/#A-request's-path","page":"Architecture","text":"A client sends a KServe V2 ModelInfer request. Tensors travel inline in the request or, when client and worker share a host, in a shared-memory region the client registered beforehand (Client Usage).\nOn a multi-worker node, the gateway picks a worker for the model and forwards the request.\nThe worker decodes the request on the request's own task and runs the bundle's preprocess hook there, if it has one, then queues the request with the scheduler.\nA single dispatch loop picks the next model to run, coalesces that model's queued requests into one execution at a compiled batch size, and runs it on the device. The device runs one execution at a time (Scheduling).\nEach request's task receives its slice of the output, runs the bundle's postprocess hook, and encodes the response.\n\nPre and postprocessing run on the request tasks rather than in the dispatch loop, so the CPU work of many requests runs in parallel with the device execution.\n\nA meta model replaces steps 3 to 5 with a Julia run hook that calls other models; each call re-enters the scheduler as an ordinary request (Meta Models).","title":"A request's path"},{"category":"section","location":"design/architecture/#Weights-as-arguments","page":"Architecture","text":"A bundle holds one compiled program per batch size and one set of weights (Bundles). The weights are passed to each program as arguments rather than compiled into it. That is what lets every batch size share one copy of the weights, and lets a worker move a model's weights onto the device when the model is needed and evict them when it is not, without recompiling (On-demand Weights).","title":"Weights as arguments"},{"category":"section","location":"design/architecture/#Model-lifecycle","page":"Architecture","text":"The set of models a worker serves can change while it runs. By default the worker watches the model repository and loads, reloads, and unloads bundles as they change; the model_control_mode key in Node Configuration chooses between that, a fixed startup set, and control by an external system over gRPC.\n\nThe project's goals, audience, and non-goals are on the Philosophy page.","title":"Model lifecycle"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/multi_gpu/#Scale-to-Many-GPUs","page":"Scale to Many GPUs","text":"The container from Serve on One GPU scales to a multi-GPU host unchanged: grant it more GPUs and the supervisor inside runs one worker per GPU, puts the embedded gateway in front of them, and keeps the same public ports. Clients do not change. What you choose is how models are spread across the GPUs, and that depends on whether your constraint is compute or memory.\n\nSituation Shape Optimizes for\nModels fit on every GPU, and you need throughput Replicated Spreading each model's load across GPUs\nModels do not all fit on every GPU Distributed Serving more models than any one GPU holds\n\nBoth shapes are the same image and the same node file; they differ in the gateway's scheduling mode and the workers' scheduling discipline.","title":"Scale to Many GPUs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/multi_gpu/#Grant-every-GPU","page":"Scale to Many GPUs","text":"Run the image as before, granting all GPUs:\n\npodman run -d --name reactantserver \\\n  --device nvidia.com/gpu=all \\\n  --ipc=host --pids-limit=-1 \\\n  -p 8001:8001 -p 8002:8002 \\\n  -v $PWD/models:/var/lib/reactantserver/models \\\n  -v reactant-compile-cache:/var/cache/reactant-compile \\\n  --stop-timeout 30 \\\n  ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver:0.1\n\nWith Docker, use --gpus all. The logs show one [workerN] per GPU and a [gateway]; the workers listen on internal ports (8080 and up), and the gateway takes 8001 and 8002. Wait for curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:8002/readyz as before: the gateway reports ready once a worker does.","title":"Grant every GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/multi_gpu/#Replicated:-every-model-on-every-GPU","page":"Scale to Many GPUs","text":"By default every worker serves every model in the repository, and the gateway spreads each model's requests across all of them (round_robin). That is the replicated shape, and it needs no configuration. Each GPU holds a full copy of every model's weights, so it fits only when the whole repository fits on one GPU.\n\nSeveral workers on one host each hold a host-RAM copy of every model's weights as well, unless they share one. Turn that on in the node file:\n\nglobal:\n  runtime:\n    backend: cuda\n    shared_host_weights: true\n    shared_host_weights_mode: \"660\"\n\nshared_host_weights keeps one copy of each model's weights in /dev/shm for all of the node's workers, which is another reason for --ipc=host (or a --shm-size larger than the weights). See On-demand Weights.","title":"Replicated: every model on every GPU"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/multi_gpu/#Distributed:-each-model-on-a-few-GPUs","page":"Scale to Many GPUs","text":"When the models do not all fit on every GPU, or when you want each model's requests to concentrate on few GPUs so they coalesce into full batches, use the gateway's lpt_packing mode. It places each model on a fixed number of GPUs, balancing measured compute against each GPU's weight memory, and repacks as the load changes.\n\nlpt_packing needs the workers on the fifo or edf discipline, since placement decisions move to the gateway; this example uses fifo. Both settings can be passed as environment variables:\n\npodman run -d --name reactantserver \\\n  --device nvidia.com/gpu=all \\\n  --ipc=host --pids-limit=-1 \\\n  -p 8001:8001 -p 8002:8002 \\\n  -v $PWD/models:/var/lib/reactantserver/models \\\n  -v reactant-compile-cache:/var/cache/reactant-compile \\\n  -e INFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_DISCIPLINE=fifo \\\n  -e REACTANT_GATEWAY_SCHEDULING_MODE=lpt_packing \\\n  -e REACTANT_GATEWAY_SCHEDULING_DEFAULT_REPLICAS=1 \\\n  --stop-timeout 30 \\\n  ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver:0.1\n\nINFERENCE_SERVER_* variables override node file keys for the workers, and REACTANT_GATEWAY_* variables override the embedded gateway's settings. default_replicas: 1 places each model on one GPU, the shape that coalesces best; a number greater than one, or all, replicates every model that many times.\n\nThe gateway waits for every worker to finish compiling before it starts, then logs each placement as an lpt_packing: repack line. The gateway_model_replicas and gateway_placement_weight series on :8002/metrics show where each model landed:\n\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8002/metrics | grep -E '^gateway_(model_replicas|placement_weight)'","title":"Distributed: each model on a few GPUs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/multi_gpu/#Give-a-busy-model-more-GPUs","page":"Scale to Many GPUs","text":"Per-model settings need a gateway.yml, mounted at /etc/reactantserver/gateway.yml. Once the file exists, it owns the gateway's worker list, so it lists the workers' endpoints too:\n\n# gateway.yml for a 4-GPU node\nendpoints: [\"127.0.0.1:8080\", \"127.0.0.1:8081\", \"127.0.0.1:8082\", \"127.0.0.1:8083\"]\nmetrics_endpoints: [\"127.0.0.1:9100\", \"127.0.0.1:9101\", \"127.0.0.1:9102\", \"127.0.0.1:9103\"]\nworker_names: [worker0, worker1, worker2, worker3]\nscheduling:\n  mode: lpt_packing\n  default_replicas: 1\n  models:\n    big-model:\n      replicas: 2          # this model on two GPUs\n\nEach replica puts the model's full weights on its GPU, so size replica counts against GPU memory; the gateway warns at each repack when a placement does not fit.\n\nA running gateway can also be retuned without a restart, including a model's replica count, over its control plane. Multi-GPU Gateway documents every scheduling setting, the routing policies, and the runtime control RPCs.","title":"Give a busy model more GPUs"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/container/multi_gpu/#Beyond-one-host","page":"Scale to Many GPUs","text":"Each node exposes the interfaces a multi-node control plane needs: the KServe V2 data plane, the worker control RPCs, and health and metrics over HTTP. The project does not ship a multi-node control plane. The simplest multi-node setup is a standalone gateway pointed at every node's workers, as in Front Workers with a Gateway; Deployment describes the endpoint contract.","title":"Beyond one host"},{"category":"section","location":"client/#Client-Usage","page":"Client Usage","text":"ReactantServerClient is the inference client for a ReactantServer worker or the Gateway. It builds KServe V2 requests against ReactantServerCore's protobuf messages and forwards them over gRPC. It depends only on ReactantServerCore and the gRPC layer, so it carries no Reactant/XLA dependency and installs quickly on a plain client machine. (The same KServe V2 wire protocol means any Triton or KServe gRPC client also works; this package is the convenient Julia option.)\n\nRun Inference walks through using it: one-shot requests, batched inference over a dataset, and the shared-memory transport. This page is the reference for how the client behaves.","title":"Client Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"client/#One-shot-requests","page":"Client Usage","text":"KServeModel takes either (host, port, model_name) or a url of the form grpc://host:port (or bare host:port); grpcs/https selects a secure channel. max_batch_size caps how many items the batched drivers coalesce per request, and deadline is the per-request timeout in seconds (10 by default). infer_sync(model, inputs) sends one request built from InferInput tensors, and InferOutput reads a named output back. Tensors use Julia's column-major layout with the batch axis last on both ends; the wire codec converts to KServe's row-major shapes.\n\nThe one-shot path encodes tensor data element by element as typed contents, so it emits a one-time performance warning (once per session) and suits small, occasional requests. For datasets, use the IO-driven drivers below: they stage through the buffer pool and ship raw bytes on every transport.","title":"One-shot requests"},{"category":"section","location":"client/#Batched-inference-over-a-dataset","page":"Client Usage","text":"For many items, implement AbstractInferenceIO and use infer_async (concurrent) or infer_sync(model, io) (serial). The driver stages each chunk through a shared staging pool, an InferenceBufferPool wrapping ReactantServerCore.BufferPool. The first call to each server probes it with the IsSameIPCNamespace RPC: if the server confirms it shares the client's IPC namespace, inputs travel over the system shared-memory data plane with zero extra copies on the wire; otherwise, or if the server does not implement the probe, the driver uses inline transport. Inline sends one raw little-endian byte blob per input (raw_input_contents) that aliases the staging pool directly, so no typed per-element encoding happens on the client either; what shared memory still buys is skipping the transfer itself, since the server reads the registered region instead of receiving the payload.\n\nThe shared_memory keyword on KServeModel controls this choice:\n\n:auto (default) probes and uses shared memory when available.\n:on forces it, and errors loudly if the server is in a different namespace.\n:off always sends inline; the probe is not sent.\n\nShared memory is not supported on macOS yet: the server cannot reopen a region the client creates. There :auto sends inline without probing and warns once per process, and :on throws an ArgumentError when the model is constructed.\n\nThere is no silent runtime fallback once shared memory is chosen. A stale registration (the server restarted and no longer knows the region) is re-registered and retried once in-band; if that fails, the client latches that endpoint to inline transport and a background task re-probes it every shm_reprobe_interval seconds, restoring shared memory when the server recovers.\n\nYour IO implements length, item_input_bytes, infer_encode_chunk! (request the chunk's input buffers with scratch, write into them, and return them tagged with their tensor names), and infer_decode_chunk! (consume the response). The pool's fixed-slot allocator hands every chunk a disjoint slot, so concurrent infer_async calls are safe. kserve_init(; pool_bytes, n_slots, shm_reprobe_interval) sizes the staging pool (256 MiB by default) and its number of slots (8 by default), which bounds how many chunks can be in flight against one pool; kserve_shutdown() tears the pool and the gRPC subsystem down.\n\nRun Inference builds a complete IO for a small model.\n\nscratch is the same buffer-request interface meta models use through call.scratch; see the Meta Models page. The lower-level path is still supported: carve a subslot, pool_view it, and return an InferInput(name, sub, shape, T) descriptor; both forms may be mixed in the returned vector.\n\nOutputs can also be read back through shared memory: return a non-empty output_specs vector of OutputSpec(name, dtype, per_item_dims) entries and the driver asks the server to write each declared output into the staging slot, then copies the bytes into the response before infer_decode_chunk! sees it, so InferOutput works the same on either transport. The default (empty) keeps every output inline. A non-empty vector also opts the IO into explicit-output mode: the request asks for exactly those outputs, so declare every output the IO consumes. Outputs with data-dependent shapes cannot be sized ahead of time and must stay inline.","title":"Batched inference over a dataset"},{"category":"section","location":"client/#Validating-an-IO-against-a-model","page":"Client Usage","text":"Because an IO hard-codes a model's tensor names, dtypes, and shapes, it can silently drift from the served model. validate_io dry-runs an IO against the model's true I/O spec without sending an inference request: it runs infer_encode_chunk! and infer_decode_chunk! once over synthetic, spec-shaped data and surfaces name, dtype, shape, and indexing mismatches as errors. Get the spec from model_io_spec(model) against a running server (the ModelMetadata RPC; it throws if the server is unreachable or does not implement metadata, as a gateway does not, so point it at a worker), or from manifest_io_spec(path) offline against a model's manifest.yaml, suitable for a build-time check. Reported shapes are Julia column-major (batch axis last), matching the axis order you build arrays in.\n\nvalidate_io(model, io)                              # online: fetches the spec from the server\nvalidate_io(manifest_io_spec(\"manifest.yaml\"), io)  # offline: no server needed\n\nThe harness runs your real methods, so call it on a representative or dummy IO (it may write into the IO's buffers at positions 1:items). For example, validating an IO that writes a length-4 output against a model whose output is actually length 3 reports the shape mismatch, and an infer_decode_chunk! that indexed out[:, j] past the real batch size surfaces as an indexing error, both without sending a request.","title":"Validating an IO against a model"},{"category":"section","location":"client/#Bounds-checks-in-hot-paths","page":"Client Usage","text":"validate_io can only catch an out-of-range index if the access is bounds-checked. Julia's built-in @inbounds elides the check unconditionally, so an @inbounds access stays unchecked even inside the dry run. If you want @inbounds performance in production but a robust check during validation, write the access with @infer_inbounds instead. It elides the check in normal use, but because validate_io runs your methods inside with_bounds_checks, an @infer_inbounds access is bounds-checked during the dry run and an out-of-range index is caught rather than silently read. This is opt-in: using @infer_inbounds is the user's responsibility, and bare @inbounds remains unchecked unless Julia is started with --check-bounds=yes (which Pkg.test does).\n\n# Bounds-checked under validate_io, elided in production:\n@infer_inbounds buf[(4k - 3):(4k)] .= io.inputs[i]\n\n# Force the checked behavior anywhere, not just in validate_io:\nwith_bounds_checks() do\n    run_my_io_step()\nend","title":"Bounds checks in hot paths"},{"category":"section","location":"client/#Retries","page":"Client Usage","text":"A worker or gateway at its concurrency cap (endpoints.max_concurrent_requests, see Node Configuration) sheds requests with RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED. By default the client retries: ReactantServerClient.RetryPolicy waits initial_backoff seconds and grows the interval by factor each attempt (1s, 2s, 4s, ...) up to max_backoff, until the model's deadline budget is spent. The per-attempt deadline sent to the server shrinks to the remaining budget, so retries never push total wall time past the original deadline. jitter (full jitter: wait uniform in [0, backoff]) keeps concurrent chunk requests from synchronizing into a retry storm, and min_budget (seconds) is the smallest remaining budget worth another attempt. retry_unavailable also retries UNAVAILABLE (no replica reachable). Deadline, NOT_FOUND, and INVALID_ARGUMENT errors always fail fast. Pass a RetryPolicy(...) as the retry keyword to KServeModel; ReactantServerClient.RetryPolicy(enabled = false) turns retries off, so every failure is returned to the caller.\n\nEvery documented name in ReactantServerClient, including the signatures covered here, is collected on the API page.","title":"Retries"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.1/#Release-0.1.1","page":"0.1.1","text":"ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver:0.1.1, published 2026-10-14 from ReactantServer-v0.1.1 (commit d0e4d5ac4554c500380c109d6b475fe33e3efa42).\n\nCompared with 0.1.0, only ReactantServer's own packages changed; the numerics stack is the same.\n\nChanged 0.1.0 0.1.1\nReactantServer 0.1.0 0.1.1\nReactantServerCore 1.2.0 1.3.0","title":"Release 0.1.1"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.1/#Julia-and-the-image","page":"0.1.1","text":"Component Version\nJulia 1.12.7\nLLVM (Julia's) 18.1.7\nBase image nvidia/cuda:13.1.2-cudnn-devel-ubuntu24.04\nBase image digest sha256:08d6facb6a72f918beb3ffe5b57cff72402a203f44428831262e95c7466d4188\nOperating system Ubuntu 24.04\nPlatform linux/amd64","title":"Julia and the image"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.1/#CUDA","page":"0.1.1","text":"Component Version\nNVIDIA driver 590.44.01 or newer\nGPU compute capability 7.5 or newer\nCUDA toolkit 13.1.1\ncuDNN 9.21.0\nNCCL 2.28.9\nNVSHMEM 3.3.20\nCompute capabilities built sm_75, sm_80, sm_90, sm_100, compute_120","title":"CUDA"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.1/#Compiler-and-runtime-packages","page":"0.1.1","text":"Component Version\nReactant 0.2.287\nReactantCore 0.1.23\nReactant_jll 0.0.410+0\nEnzyme 0.13.205\nEnzymeCore 0.8.21\nEnzyme_jll 0.0.294+0\nLLVM 9.13.2\nLLVMExtra_jll 0.0.47+0\nGPUCompiler 1.23.0","title":"Compiler and runtime packages"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.1/#ReactantServer-packages","page":"0.1.1","text":"Component Version\nReactantServer 0.1.1\nReactantServerCore 1.3.0\nReactantServerGateway 0.1.0\nReactantServerNode 0.1.0","title":"ReactantServer packages"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.1/#Digests","page":"0.1.1","text":" Digest\nImage sha256:ba640d6a1b048ae9bc728a47ade971318d37fa8cfbcc6e2539e5d096b07cb0d5\nBase image, linux/amd64 sha256:c8365d2cde6d16aad4e9981ebfaff1b8556152b85f51082316a9667f87a49604\nCUDA artifact (Reactant_jll tree hash) 12ec5519bfb17608aa57b3cd1c9167d460275962\nCUDA artifact built from Reactant.jl a9df0cede5cf5745b685909f542f31a4b68d985b\n\nPull by digest with ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver@sha256:ba640d6a1b048ae9bc728a47ade971318d37fa8cfbcc6e2539e5d096b07cb0d5. The image also records its ReactantServer package versions and the commit it was built from in its labels (podman inspect).","title":"Digests"},{"category":"section","location":"security/#Security","page":"Security","text":"ReactantServer is designed to run on a trusted network behind your own perimeter. Be aware of the following before exposing any endpoint:\n\nCleartext h2c. All gRPC traffic (worker and gateway) is cleartext h2c. TLS settings are parsed by the gateway config but not yet enforced; a configured cert triggers a startup warning.\nNo authentication or authorization on the KServe data plane, the worker control-plane RPCs (residency and policy), the gateway's own scheduling control plane (GatewayControlService, which can repack the fleet and change a model's replica count and routing at runtime), or the Prometheus metrics listener (which binds 0.0.0.0:8002 by default). The control plane is mutating, so treat access to the gRPC ports as administrative access.\nTrusted bundles. A bundle's optional model.jl executes arbitrary Julia in the server process, so bundles are trusted input. Only serve bundles you built or audited; see Bundles.\nShared memory is a local trust boundary. Client-registered regions and the optional node-shared host-weight store live in /dev/shm; the shared weight regions default to mode 666 (world-writable) for friction-free sharing. Set runtime.shared_host_weights_mode: \"660\" on production or multi-user systems.","title":"Security"},{"category":"section","location":"transformers/#Transformer-Text-Models-(BERT)","page":"Transformer Text Models","text":"BERT-family text models are the canonical case for the plain bundle path with a model.jl that owns tokenization. The transformer itself is dense tensor math that torch.export traces cleanly to a single static StableHLO program, but two things around it are not tensor math: turning a string into token ids, and turning raw logits into the shape a caller wants. ReactantServer keeps both in the bundle's model.jl, so the traced graph is exactly the encoder (plus any in-graph numeric head), the client sends raw UTF-8 text, and no Python tokenizer or activation library is needed at serve time.\n\nThis page walks through exporting four public HuggingFace checkpoints that cover the common text-serving shapes. For the bundle/manifest/model.jl contract itself see Bundles & model.jl; for the PyTorch export mechanics see the export_bundle(:pytorch, ...) section there.\n\ntip: Runnable example\nexamples/transformers/ in the repository is a complete, runnable version of this walkthrough: Serve Transformer Text Models exports the four bundles, serves them, and queries all four over KServe V2.","title":"Transformer Text Models (BERT)"},{"category":"section","location":"transformers/#The-four-models","page":"Transformer Text Models","text":"Bundle HuggingFace checkpoint torch class In-graph head model.jl\nsplade naver/splade-cocondenser-ensembledistil AutoModelForMaskedLM log1p/relu/mask/max term scores tokenize; sparsify to CSR\nembedding sentence-transformers/all-MiniLM-L6-v2 AutoModel masked mean-pool + L2 normalize tokenize; passthrough\ncross_encoder cross-encoder/ms-marco-MiniLM-L-6-v2 AutoModelForSequenceClassification none (raw logit) tokenize pair; add sigmoid\nsentiment distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english AutoModelForSequenceClassification none (raw logits) tokenize; add softmax + argmax\n\nThe four cover the common text-serving shapes: learned sparse term expansion (SPLADE), a dense 384-dim unit-norm sentence embedding, query/document relevance scoring, and binary sentiment classification. All four share the bert-base-uncased WordPiece vocab (30522 tokens), so one tokenizer serves every bundle: the code in ReactantServer.BertText, the vocab as a per-bundle vocab.txt.","title":"The four models"},{"category":"section","location":"transformers/#Tokenization-lives-in-the-bundle","page":"Transformer Text Models","text":"Clients send raw UTF-8 bytes; the bundle's model.jl decodes them and tokenizes in Julia with ReactantServer.BertText, a self-contained BERT WordPiece (uncased) reimplementation that depends on nothing but Base. It matches the HuggingFace Rust tokenizer, including the awkward corners: unassigned codepoints stay in the word, ASCII symbols like  +  =  ^    count as punctuation, LongestFirst pair truncation, and special-token literals in raw text.\n\nShared code lives in the package and per-model configuration is baked into model.jl: the only tokenizer asset the export driver copies into a bundle is the checkpoint's vocab.txt (loaded at serve time relative to model.jl's directory). A bundle exported this way needs a server that has BertText, so the bundle and the server version are coupled.\n\npreprocess turns the wire tensors into the executable's token-id inputs. The single-sequence models (splade, embedding, sentiment) call encode_text_batch and emit input_ids + attention_mask; the cross encoder is a text pair (one query scored against N keys) and calls encode_pair_batch, adding token_type_ids. Both decode the padded UInt8 rows, encode with the BERT template, and pad the batch up to the smallest compiled sequence bucket that fits:\n\nconst BT = ReactantServer.BertText\nconst TOKENIZER = BT.load_tokenizer(joinpath(@__DIR__, \"vocab.txt\"))\n\nfunction preprocess(inputs::Vector{NamedTensor})\n    byname = Dict(t.name => t for t in inputs)\n    input_ids, attention_mask = BT.encode_text_batch(TOKENIZER,\n        byname[\"texts\"].data::Matrix{UInt8},             # (max_bytes, batch) col-major\n        vec(byname[\"text_lens\"].data);\n        max_len=MAX_LEN, buckets=SEQ_BUCKETS, lens_name=\"text_lens\")\n    return NamedTensor[NamedTensor(\"input_ids\", input_ids),\n                       NamedTensor(\"attention_mask\", attention_mask)]\nend\n\nThe BertText surface is a small set of composable helpers:\n\nHelper What it does\nload_tokenizer(vocab_path) Load a HuggingFace vocab.txt (one token per line, line number minus 1 is the id); returns the BertTokenizer the other helpers take.\nwire_text(bytes) / wire_texts(texts, lens) Decode one UTF-8 tensor (the cross encoder's shared query) or a (max_bytes, batch) matrix of zero-padded rows using the per-row byte lengths.\nencode_single(t, text) / encode_pair(t, a, b) Encode one text as [CLS] text [SEP], or a pair as [CLS] a [SEP] b [SEP] with HF LongestFirst truncation; the pair form also returns token_type_ids.\nencode_text_batch / encode_pair_batch The preprocess entry points: decode the wire rows, encode each, and pad to the smallest bucket that fits.\npad_batch(encoded, buckets) / seq_bucket(n, buckets) Pack per-row token ids into (seq, batch) matrices at the smallest compiled sequence length that holds them; pad_batch errors clearly if an input exceeds the largest bucket.\n\nA bundle that needs a shape these two do not cover can drop a level and compose the same pieces: wire_texts decodes the padded rows to Strings, encode_single / encode_pair encode one text, and pad_batch packs the result into (seq, batch) matrices at the right bucket.","title":"Tokenization lives in the bundle"},{"category":"section","location":"transformers/#The-raw-logits-/-classifier-rule","page":"Transformer Text Models","text":"The two classifiers (cross_encoder, sentiment) emit raw logits from the traced graph; the activation is applied in postprocess, which returns both the raw logits and the probabilities. Keeping the sigmoid/softmax out of the graph follows the same rule as every other classification bundle in the package, and lets a caller read either value:\n\nfunction postprocess(out::Vector{NamedTensor})           # sentiment\n    logits = out[1].data::Matrix{Float32}                # (2, batch): rows are classes\n    probs = ReactantServer.NNlib.softmax(logits; dims=1)\n    label_id = Int32[argmax(view(logits, :, b)) - 1 for b in 1:size(logits, 2)]\n    return NamedTensor[NamedTensor(\"logits\", logits),\n                       NamedTensor(\"probs\", probs),\n                       NamedTensor(\"label_id\", label_id)]\nend\n\nThe cross encoder's postprocess is the same shape with a sigmoid instead: prob = sigmoid.(logits) over the per-pair score vector. By contrast, SPLADE's log1p/relu/mask/max and the embedding model's masked mean-pool + L2 normalization stay in the traced graph: they define the model's numeric output (and, for SPLADE, do the (batch, seq, vocab) -> (batch, vocab) reduction), rather than being a presentation-layer activation. SPLADE's postprocess then only sparsifies the dense term-score vector to a CSR triple, applying the Python server's keep rule (round(x, decimals=2) != 0, half-to-even ties, which torch.round semantics match Julia's default rounding) and emitting 0-based vocab indices, unrounded weights, and row offsets; the embedding postprocess is a passthrough.","title":"The raw-logits / classifier rule"},{"category":"section","location":"transformers/#The-wire-contract","page":"Transformer Text Models","text":"Because model.jl reshapes the wire I/O, the manifest declares client_inputs/client_outputs (what the caller sends and receives) distinct from executable_inputs/executable_outputs (the token tensors and raw logits the traced program sees). The export driver states this with IOSpecs passed to export_bundle(:pytorch, ...). Their shapes are in Julia (column-major) order with the batch axis last, the reverse of the wire shapes in the table below: texts is [-1, 1] with batch_axis=1, the Julia (max_bytes, batch) matrix model.jl reads. For example the sentiment bundle:\n\nexport_bundle(:pytorch, model, (input_ids, attention_mask);\n    dir, name,\n    input_names  = [\"input_ids\", \"attention_mask\"],\n    output_names = [\"logits\"],\n    axis_letters = Dict(\"input_ids\" => ['s'], \"attention_mask\" => ['s'], \"logits\" => ['c']),\n    batch_sizes = [1, 8],\n    matmul_precision = \"highest\",\n    client_inputs = [\n        IOSpec(\"texts\", UInt8, [-1, 1]; batch_axis=1, letters=['c']),\n        IOSpec(\"text_lens\", Int32, [1]; batch_axis=0),\n    ],\n    client_outputs = [\n        IOSpec(\"logits\", Float32, [2, 1]; batch_axis=1, letters=['c']),\n        IOSpec(\"probs\", Float32, [2, 1]; batch_axis=1, letters=['c']),\n        IOSpec(\"label_id\", Int32, [1]; batch_axis=0),\n    ],\n    provenance = _prov(...))\n\nmatmul_precision = \"highest\" is required on the PyTorch path: JAX freezes float32 matmul precision at trace time based on the export host, so a CPU trace without it would bake a lower precision. See Bundles & model.jl for the full IOSpec and manifest encoding.\n\nThe client-facing shapes below are in row-major (KServe/client) order; the wire codec converts to the Julia column-major convention at the boundary.\n\nBundle Client inputs Client outputs\nsplade texts UINT8 [batch, max_bytes] (zero-padded rows), text_lens INT32 [batch] indices INT32 [K], values FP32 [K], row_offsets INT64 [batch+1] (CSR, 0-based vocab ids)\nembedding texts, text_lens (as above) embedding FP32 [batch, 384] (unit-norm)\ncross_encoder query UINT8 [q_bytes] (shared across all keys), keys UINT8 [batch, max_bytes], key_lens INT32 [batch] logits FP32 [batch] (raw), prob FP32 [batch] (sigmoid)\nsentiment texts, text_lens logits FP32 [batch, 2] (raw), probs FP32 [batch, 2] (softmax), label_id INT32 [batch] (0 NEGATIVE, 1 POSITIVE)\n\nAll inputs are raw UTF-8 bytes; the bundle tokenizes them. Note the two text encodings in one table: the single-sequence models read one (max_bytes, batch) matrix of zero-padded rows plus one length per row, while the cross encoder's query is a single unpadded byte vector shared by every (query, key) pair.","title":"The wire contract"},{"category":"section","location":"transformers/#Sequence-length-and-batching","page":"Transformer Text Models","text":"Each bundle compiles a single sequence length of 512 and batch sizes [1, 8], i.e. two programs per bundle (model.b1.mlir, model.b8.mlir), all batch variants sharing one weights.safetensors. Every request pads to 512, which is bit-identical to a tighter bucket because every op is attention-mask aware; the tradeoff is wasted compute on short inputs in exchange for far fewer programs and lower compile time and command-buffer count. The constant lives in two places that must agree: SEQ_LEN in the export driver and SEQ_BUCKETS = (512,) in each model.jl. To trade compute back for more programs, add sequence buckets or batch sizes in the driver (export_bundle supports multiple input shapes over one weight set) and bump SEQ_BUCKETS/max_batch to match. An over-long text fails in preprocess with a clear error naming the encoded length and the largest compiled bucket, instead of a BoundsError while filling the padded matrices.","title":"Sequence length and batching"},{"category":"section","location":"transformers/#Running-the-example","page":"Transformer Text Models","text":"examples/transformers/ in the repository exports all four models, serves them, and queries them; Serve Transformer Text Models walks through running it.","title":"Running the example"},{"category":"section","location":"transformers/#See-also","page":"Transformer Text Models","text":"Serve Transformer Text Models to run the example end to end\nBundles & model.jl for the bundle contract, IOSpec, and the manifest encoding\nObject Detection for exporting a detector's data-dependent middle as one static program\nClient Usage for building requests with InferInput/InferOutput","title":"See also"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.0/#Release-0.1.0","page":"0.1.0","text":"ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver:0.1.0, published 2026-10-11 from ReactantServer-v0.1.0 (commit 8a2c1eefbea0087f08d99a030b007019d50ed527).\n\nThis is the first container release.","title":"Release 0.1.0"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.0/#Julia-and-the-image","page":"0.1.0","text":"Component Version\nJulia 1.12.7\nLLVM (Julia's) 18.1.7\nBase image nvidia/cuda:13.1.2-cudnn-devel-ubuntu24.04\nBase image digest sha256:08d6facb6a72f918beb3ffe5b57cff72402a203f44428831262e95c7466d4188\nOperating system Ubuntu 24.04\nPlatform linux/amd64","title":"Julia and the image"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.0/#CUDA","page":"0.1.0","text":"Component Version\nNVIDIA driver 590.44.01 or newer\nGPU compute capability 7.5 or newer\nCUDA toolkit 13.1.1\ncuDNN 9.21.0\nNCCL 2.28.9\nNVSHMEM 3.3.20\nCompute capabilities built sm_75, sm_80, sm_90, sm_100, compute_120","title":"CUDA"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.0/#Compiler-and-runtime-packages","page":"0.1.0","text":"Component Version\nReactant 0.2.287\nReactantCore 0.1.23\nReactant_jll 0.0.410+0\nEnzyme 0.13.205\nEnzymeCore 0.8.21\nEnzyme_jll 0.0.294+0\nLLVM 9.13.2\nLLVMExtra_jll 0.0.47+0\nGPUCompiler 1.23.0","title":"Compiler and runtime packages"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.0/#ReactantServer-packages","page":"0.1.0","text":"Component Version\nReactantServer 0.1.0\nReactantServerCore 1.2.0\nReactantServerGateway 0.1.0\nReactantServerNode 0.1.0","title":"ReactantServer packages"},{"category":"section","location":"releases/0.1.0/#Digests","page":"0.1.0","text":" Digest\nImage sha256:d9a0178657d47330b08613a9f4888cc3aa933957bbd2e16d21322cc576cc0405\nBase image, linux/amd64 sha256:c8365d2cde6d16aad4e9981ebfaff1b8556152b85f51082316a9667f87a49604\nCUDA artifact (Reactant_jll tree hash) 12ec5519bfb17608aa57b3cd1c9167d460275962\nCUDA artifact built from Reactant.jl a9df0cede5cf5745b685909f542f31a4b68d985b\n\nPull by digest with ghcr.io/enzymead/reactantserver@sha256:d9a0178657d47330b08613a9f4888cc3aa933957bbd2e16d21322cc576cc0405. This image predates the version labels later images carry.","title":"Digests"},{"category":"section","location":"node_config/#Node-Configuration","page":"Node Configuration","text":"A node file describes one machine: its model repository and its workers, one per device. Each worker reads the same file, resolves its own entry by name, and serves every bundle in the repository. Under the node supervisor the workers: list is optional, and the supervisor creates one worker per detected device (Deployment). The gateway in front of a multi-worker node is configured separately, by its own gateway.yml (Gateway configuration).","title":"Node Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"node_config/#Top-level-keys","page":"Node Configuration","text":"# One shared bundle repository. Each immediate subdirectory containing a manifest.yaml is a\n# bundle; its directory name is the model name.\nmodel_repo: /var/lib/reactantserver/models\n\n# Worker at index i binds base_port + i unless it sets an explicit `port:`.\nbase_port: 8080\n\n# Optional Prometheus metrics port per worker (metrics_base_port + i): worker0 -> 9100,\n# worker1 -> 9101, and so on. Each worker serves /metrics, /healthz, /readyz on it.\n# Absent disables per-worker metrics.\nmetrics_base_port: 9100\n\n# Supervisor-only: `auto` (the default), an integer device count, or an explicit device list\n# (ordinals or GPU UUIDs). The supervisor synthesizes one worker per selected device.\n# gpus: auto\n\nglobal:    # defaults merged into every worker (any block may be overridden per worker)\nworkers:   # optional under the supervisor; one entry per GPU\nmodels:    # optional; pins the named models to device memory on the listed workers\n\nmodel_repo (required) is the shared bundle repository. Each immediate subdirectory that contains a manifest.yaml is a bundle, and its directory name is the model name. It resolves into every worker's model_dirs (a single entry).\nbase_port is the first worker's gRPC port. Worker at index i binds base_port + i (worker0 -> base_port) unless that worker sets an explicit port:.\nmetrics_base_port (optional) derives each worker's Prometheus metrics port the same way: metrics_base_port + i. Absent, per-worker metrics are disabled.\ngpus (optional, supervisor-only) selects how many workers the supervisor synthesizes when no workers: list is present: auto (the default) enumerates the visible devices, an integer is a device count, and a list gives explicit device selectors (ordinals or GPU UUIDs). The REACTANT_GPUS environment variable overrides this key.\nglobal holds the defaults merged into every worker; any block may be overridden per worker.\nworkers lists the worker entries (optional under the supervisor).\nmodels optionally pins the named models to device memory on the listed workers.","title":"Top-level keys"},{"category":"section","location":"node_config/#Global-settings","page":"Node Configuration","text":"The global: block holds defaults merged into every worker; a worker entry may override any of these blocks. The sub-blocks map onto the resolved ServerConfig:\n\nglobal:\n  cache_dir: /var/cache/reactantserver\n  model_control_mode: dynamic  # dynamic (watch the repo) | static | explicit\n  model_poll_seconds: 15.0     # repository poll interval in dynamic mode\n  runtime:                     # -> RuntimeConfig\n    backend: cuda              # cpu or cuda\n    device_memory_fraction: 0.9  # share of GPU memory claimed for the pool (weights, scratch, I/O)\n    preallocate: true          # claim the pool up front (GPU only)\n    allow_cpu_fallback: false\n    numerics: auto             # f32 | auto | tf32; see below\n    executable_cache: true     # cache compiled programs under each bundle's .cache/; see below\n    profile: default           # default | regulated; see below\n    batch_sizes: all           # all | largest compiled batch size per model (profile default)\n    xla_flags: {}              # XLA DebugOptions overrides, e.g. xla_gpu_exclude_nondeterministic_ops: true\n    device_weight_fraction: 1.0    # GPU arena share for resident weights (pinned + on-demand), (0, 1]\n    device_headroom_fraction: 0.1  # GPU arena share kept free; drives startup auto-sizing\n    autotune: true             # XLA GPU compile autotuner; false = default kernels, no trials\n    # autotune_cache: false    # persistent per-fusion autotune cache; omit to inherit Reactant's\n    # autotune_cache_dir: /cache/autotune  # cache directory; omit to inherit Reactant's defaults\n    shared_host_weights: false # one shm-backed host copy of each model on this node's workers\n    shared_host_weights_mode: \"666\"  # octal permissions for the shared regions; \"660\" preferred\n    weights_migration: off     # off | copy | replace legacy-order weights files at load; see below\n    host_weight_budget: 0      # host RAM for weight copies (0b, 512m, 5g... or unlimited); see Use Cases\n    # host_weight_budget_workers: 1  # private copies: workers splitting the budget (set by the supervisor)\n  scheduler:                   # -> SchedulerConfig\n    discipline: fair           # fair | fifo | edf; fifo or edf behind a gateway in lpt_packing\n    ema_halflife_seconds: 30.0\n    max_queue_depth: 1024      # per-model queue cap; a full model rejects new requests\n    dispatch_timeout_seconds: 30.0\n    compaction_interval: 0     # defragment device memory every N on-demand weight loads; 0 disables\n    coalescing: true           # merge queued requests into one dispatch (profile default)\n    models: {}                 # per-model overrides -> ModelSchedConfig (see below)\n  endpoints:                   # -> EndpointsConfig\n    host: 0.0.0.0              # bind all interfaces so the gateway and clients can reach the worker\n    max_concurrent_requests: 64  # in-flight RPC cap; 0 = uncapped; shed with RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED\n  grpc:                        # -> GrpcConfig\n    max_recv_msg_bytes: 536870912  # 512 MiB; max inbound gRPC message\n    max_send_msg_bytes: 536870912  # 512 MiB; max outbound gRPC message\n\nEach sub-block corresponds to a typed config struct (RuntimeConfig, SchedulerConfig, ModelSchedConfig, EndpointsConfig, GrpcConfig); the whole resolved global: is the ServerConfig a worker serves from. The API page lists every field and its default.\n\nruntime.numerics sets the precision of f32 matrix multiplications and convolutions:\n\nauto (the default) uses TF32 on GPUs that support it (NVIDIA compute capability 8.0 and up) and strips it where it would not compile.\nf32 pins full f32 on every target: TF32 DotAlgorithms are rewritten to f32, every algorithm-free f32 dot_general and convolution gets precision_config = HIGHEST, and NVIDIA_TF32_OVERRIDE=0 is set as well.\ntf32 compiles like auto, but startup fails unless TF32 is in effect: on hardware without it, with NVIDIA_TF32_OVERRIDE=0 set, or when the startup probe matmul does not run in TF32.\n\nOn CUDA workers a startup probe logs whether TF32 arithmetic is in use, and under f32 proves the pin bit-exact. Each \"model loaded\" log line records the outcome per model: operations pinned, and algorithms rewritten or stripped.\n\nruntime.executable_cache (default true) stores every compiled program under the bundle's .cache/ directory and loads it on later starts, turning a 5 to 30 second compile per program into a load of well under 100 ms. Entries are partitioned by the Reactantjll build, the platform, and the device kind and compute capability, none of which XLA checks on load, and named by the MLIR source's content hash and a hash of the compile options (including `xlaflagsandautotune) and the numerics policy, so changing any of those compiles fresh..cache/mlir_hashes.jsonrecords the hash of everymodel*.mlir; a module whose content changes has its programs dropped on the next load, while a weights-only update keeps them. The directory watcher never reacts to anything under.cache/`, so cache writes cannot reload the model that produced them. The bundle directory must be writable by the worker; when it is not, the cache logs a warning and every program is compiled as before.\n\nruntime.batch_sizes selects which of a bundle's compiled batch sizes are loaded. all (the default) loads every model.b{N}.mlir the manifest declares, and the scheduler picks the largest size a dispatch can fill. largest loads only the largest declared size, per input-shape variant, and never reads the others (they need not be present); every dispatch runs that program, padded with zero rows and sliced back per request. Unbatched bundles are unaffected. Workers with different settings can share a bundle directory.\n\nruntime.weights_migration converts bundles whose weights.safetensors predates ReactantServer 0.2 (legacy axis order, which costs an extra full copy of every tensor on each load) to the program's axis order. off (the default) loads them as they are. copy writes a verified weights.T.safetensors beside the original and points the manifest's weights_file at it; the original is untouched, and servers older than 0.2 ignore the new field and keep loading it. replace then swaps the copy in as weights.safetensors and marks the manifest format_version: \"2.1\", which older servers refuse; use it once every server reading the directory runs 0.2 or later. The bundle directory must be writable. A migration never fails a model: on any error it is logged and the bundle loads as it was. See Migrating older bundles.\n\nruntime.xla_flags is a mapping of XLA DebugOptions field names (no leading --) to values, applied to every compile. At startup each name is checked against the XLA linked into the worker and each value against the field's type (booleans, integers, numbers, strings, and enum fields by enumerator name), so a typo fails startup instead of being ignored. An explicit xla_gpu_autotune_level here wins over autotune: false.\n\nruntime.profile applies a named set of defaults. regulated defaults batch_sizes to largest, adds xla_gpu_exclude_nondeterministic_ops: true to xla_flags, and defaults scheduler.coalescing to false; anything set explicitly wins. It does not change numerics.\n\nscheduler.coalescing (default true) lets the scheduler merge queued requests to the same model into one dispatch. With false, each dispatch serves exactly one request; see Scheduling & Coalescing.\n\nReproducibility & Regulated Use explains how numerics, batch_sizes, coalescing, executable_cache, xla_flags, and profile affect a model's outputs, and which values a validated deployment should use.\n\nmodel_control_mode sets how the loaded model set evolves:\n\ndynamic (the default) watches the repository and loads, unloads, and reloads bundles online as their files change, including the weights, the MLIR, the manifest, and model.jl. A bundle must look the same on two consecutive polls before it loads, so a half-written bundle never does, and a reload swaps one model while every other model keeps serving. A renamed directory with unchanged contents renames the live model in place, without recompiling.\nstatic loads the startup set once and never changes it.\nexplicit cedes the lifecycle to an external control plane: the worker takes no action of its own, and a model serves only while the control plane holds it resident through the worker control RPCs (ModelControlStatus, SetModelResidency, SetModelPolicy).\n\nmodel_poll_seconds is the repository poll interval in dynamic mode.\n\nruntime.device_ordinal is not something you normally set here: the node resolution derives it from the worker's gpu: key (defaulting to 0), and under the supervisor each worker sees a single device through its own CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES.\n\nThe global.grpc block is the single node-level place for gRPC message-size limits: every worker reads it directly, and the supervisor also mirrors it into the embedded gateway (as REACTANT_GATEWAY_GRPC_MAX_RECV_MSG_BYTES / REACTANT_GATEWAY_GRPC_MAX_SEND_MSG_BYTES), so one block sizes the whole node. The defaults are 512 MiB each, and these are decode/encode caps, not allocations: raising them costs nothing until a message that large actually arrives. Per-component environment overrides still apply, with the gateway's own REACTANT_GATEWAY_GRPC_MAX_RECV_MSG_BYTES winning over the mirrored value: INFERENCE_SERVER_GRPC_MAX_RECV_MSG_BYTES for a worker, REACTANT_GATEWAY_GRPC_MAX_RECV_MSG_BYTES for the gateway.\n\nscheduler.discipline selects the dispatch policy: fair (the default), fifo, or edf. Workers behind a gateway in lpt_packing mode must run fifo or edf. Scheduling describes each discipline and when to use it.","title":"Global settings"},{"category":"section","location":"node_config/#Workers","page":"Node Configuration","text":"workers:\n  - { name: worker0 }\n  - { name: worker1 }\n\nname is the worker's identity in logs, metrics, and the models: map. The listen port is base_port + index unless the worker sets an explicit port:. A worker entry may also carry override blocks (for example a runtime: block) that deep-merge over global:.\n\ngpu: N pins a worker to a specific visible device. Under the supervisor, devices are otherwise assigned in order (Deployment describes how they are detected); a worker run by hand with serve uses device 0 unless it sets gpu:.","title":"Workers"},{"category":"section","location":"node_config/#Device-pinning-(the-models:-map)","page":"Node Configuration","text":"models:\n  resnet50: [worker0, worker1]   # hot on both GPUs\n  vsq_coral: [worker0]           # hot on worker0 only\n\nEvery worker loads (and can serve) every bundle in model_repo; the gateway discovers what each worker serves and schedules requests across them. The optional top-level models: map is a per-model override that pins the named models to device memory on the listed workers for the lowest latency. It translates into scheduler.models.<name>.residency: device on those workers, merging into any per-model block already set under global: (an explicit residency wins; the map only fills an unspecified one). Unlisted models load to the device on demand, from a host RAM copy when runtime.host_weight_budget keeps one (Use Cases). Omit the block entirely to keep every model on-demand.\n\nTo load only a subset of the repository on a worker, the resolved config also supports a models_include allowlist (empty means load all); set it with the INFERENCE_SERVER_MODELS_INCLUDE override below.","title":"Device pinning (the models: map)"},{"category":"section","location":"node_config/#Per-model-scheduler-overrides","page":"Node Configuration","text":"Tune individual models under scheduler.models, which builds the ModelSchedConfig entries:\n\nscheduler:\n  models:\n    resnet50:\n      weight: 2.0                  # relative compute share (default 1.0)\n      residency: device            # keep weights GPU-resident for the server's lifetime\n      max_batch_size: 8            # cap on rows coalesced per dispatch (default uncapped)\n    yolo:\n      residency: unpinned          # no host floor; re-materialized from disk on each load\n\nweight sets the model's fair share (consulted only by the fair discipline). residency sets the model's residency floor: unpinned, system, or device (pin_to_gpu: true is a back-compat alias for residency: device). Models without an explicit residency are swapped on demand, keeping host RAM copies within runtime.host_weight_budget, or pinned in host RAM when that budget is unlimited; see Use Cases.\n\nmax_batch_size caps how many rows the scheduler coalesces into one dispatch of the model. It does not change compiled shapes: the dispatch sizes come from the batch sizes the bundle was compiled for (a partial fill still pads up to the smallest compiled size), the batch axis comes from the bundle manifest, and a single request larger than the cap is still served in one dispatch because requests are never split.","title":"Per-model scheduler overrides"},{"category":"section","location":"node_config/#Gateway-configuration","page":"Node Configuration","text":"The gateway does not read the node file at all: it is configured by its own standalone gateway.yml, which carries its listen addresses, limits, and a flat endpoints: list of worker host:port addresses that may span any number of nodes (see Multi-GPU Gateway). A gateway.yml looks like:\n\nlisten:\n  grpc: \"0.0.0.0:8001\"\n  metrics: \"0.0.0.0:8002\"\ngrpc:\n  max_recv_msg_bytes: 536870912   # 512 MiB\n  max_send_msg_bytes: 536870912\n  max_concurrent_requests_per_worker: 64  # inbound cap is this x worker count; 0 = uncapped.\n                                          # Sized above the outbound stream limit so a startup\n                                          # burst has headroom rather than being shed early\nworker_client:\n  request_timeout_seconds: 60\n  max_concurrent_streams: 32      # outbound in-flight RPCs the gateway multiplexes to one worker\nlogging:\n  level: \"info\"\n  format: \"json\"\nscheduling:\n  mode: round_robin               # round_robin | least_outstanding | lpt_packing\n  work_basis: compute             # what \"least busy\" measures: compute | items | requests\n  # lpt_packing only; see Multi-GPU Gateway for the full set and the runtime control plane.\n  default_replicas: 1             # GPUs per model (a number, or \"all\")\n  routing_fill_mode: run          # run (default) | spread | inflight\nendpoints:                        # worker host:port addresses, across any number of nodes\n  - \"10.0.0.1:8080\"\n  - \"10.0.0.2:8080\"\n\nThe gateway is also configured by REACTANT_GATEWAY_* environment overrides (REACTANT_GATEWAY_WORKERS replaces the endpoint list, comma separated). The embedded gateway under the supervisor is launched from defaults plus those synthesized environment variables, so a single node needs no gateway.yml at all. See Multi-GPU Gateway for the scheduling modes, the runtime control plane, and the lpt_packing-specific knobs.","title":"Gateway configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"node_config/#Environment-overrides","page":"Node Configuration","text":"Any worker value can be overridden per process by an environment variable of the form INFERENCE_SERVER_<SECTION>_<FIELD>, for example:\n\nINFERENCE_SERVER_ENDPOINTS_PORT=9100\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_BACKEND=cpu\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_DEVICE_WEIGHT_FRACTION=0.8\n\nBooleans parse as 1, true, yes, or on (case-insensitive). List-valued overrides (INFERENCE_SERVER_MODEL_DIRS, INFERENCE_SERVER_MODELS_INCLUDE) are colon-separated. Overrides are applied on top of the resolved node file, and the effective configuration, including which overrides were applied, is logged at startup.\n\nVariable Overrides Values\nINFERENCE_SERVER_CACHE_DIR cache_dir path\nINFERENCE_SERVER_MODEL_POLL_SECONDS model_poll_seconds seconds\nINFERENCE_SERVER_MODEL_CONTROL_MODE model_control_mode static | dynamic | explicit\nINFERENCE_SERVER_MODEL_DIRS model_dirs colon-separated paths\nINFERENCE_SERVER_MODELS_INCLUDE models_include colon-separated model names\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_BACKEND runtime.backend cpu | cuda\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_DEVICE_ORDINAL runtime.device_ordinal device index\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_DEVICE_MEMORY_FRACTION runtime.device_memory_fraction (0, 1]\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_PREALLOCATE runtime.preallocate bool\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_ALLOW_CPU_FALLBACK runtime.allow_cpu_fallback bool\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_DEVICE_WEIGHT_FRACTION runtime.device_weight_fraction (0, 1]\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_DEVICE_HEADROOM_FRACTION runtime.device_headroom_fraction [0, 1)\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_AUTOTUNE runtime.autotune bool\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_AUTOTUNE_CACHE runtime.autotune_cache bool\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_AUTOTUNE_CACHE_DIR runtime.autotune_cache_dir path\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_NUMERICS runtime.numerics f32 | auto | tf32\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_EXECUTABLE_CACHE runtime.executable_cache bool\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_PROFILE runtime.profile default | regulated\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_BATCH_SIZES runtime.batch_sizes all | largest\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_SHARED_HOST_WEIGHTS runtime.shared_host_weights bool\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_SHARED_HOST_WEIGHTS_MODE runtime.shared_host_weights_mode octal string\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_WEIGHTS_MIGRATION runtime.weights_migration off | copy | replace\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_HOST_WEIGHT_BUDGET runtime.host_weight_budget byte size, e.g. 0b, 5g\nINFERENCE_SERVER_RUNTIME_HOST_WEIGHT_BUDGET_WORKERS runtime.host_weight_budget_workers worker count\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_DISCIPLINE scheduler.discipline fair | fifo | edf\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_EMA_HALFLIFE_SECONDS scheduler.ema_halflife_seconds seconds\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_RECENCY_PENALTY_CAP scheduler.recency_penalty_cap 0..1\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_COALESCING_DISCOUNT scheduler.coalescing_discount 0..1\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_COST_EMA_ALPHA scheduler.cost_ema_alpha 0..1\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_MAX_QUEUE_DEPTH scheduler.max_queue_depth integer\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_DISPATCH_TIMEOUT_SECONDS scheduler.dispatch_timeout_seconds seconds\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_COMPACTION_INTERVAL scheduler.compaction_interval integer\nINFERENCE_SERVER_SCHEDULER_COALESCING scheduler.coalescing bool\nINFERENCE_SERVER_ENDPOINTS_HOST endpoints.host address\nINFERENCE_SERVER_ENDPOINTS_PORT endpoints.port port\nINFERENCE_SERVER_ENDPOINTS_METRICS_PORT endpoints.metrics_port port\nINFERENCE_SERVER_ENDPOINTS_MAX_CONCURRENT_REQUESTS endpoints.max_concurrent_requests integer\nINFERENCE_SERVER_GRPC_MAX_RECV_MSG_BYTES grpc.max_recv_msg_bytes bytes\nINFERENCE_SERVER_GRPC_MAX_SEND_MSG_BYTES grpc.max_send_msg_bytes bytes","title":"Environment overrides"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/examples/transformers/#Serve-Transformer-Text-Models","page":"Serve Transformer Text Models","text":"This tutorial exports four public HuggingFace BERT-family checkpoints (a SPLADE sparse encoder, a sentence embedder, a cross-encoder reranker, and a sentiment classifier) into ReactantServer bundles, serves all four, and queries them with raw text. It runs on the CPU, so it needs no GPU, and the same steps serve on a GPU by dropping one flag. Why tokenization lives in each bundle's model.jl, and the wire contract the client follows, are covered in the Transformer Text Models reference.\n\nYou need a clone of the repository (the example's scripts live in examples/transformers/) and network access for the first export, which downloads the checkpoints and the Python packages the PyTorch export uses.\n\nexamples/transformers/ is split into three single-purpose Julia environments so each loads only what it needs (and they stop invalidating each other's precompilation): export holds the export tooling with PythonCall and torch, server holds ReactantServer, and client holds only ReactantServerClient, with neither Reactant nor torch. Each environment resolves independently the first time you use it:\n\nfor env in export server client; do\n  julia --project=examples/transformers/$env -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\ndone\n\nThen run the three steps in order (the server stays running; drive it from a second terminal):\n\n# 1. Export the bundles (first time only; writes ./bundles/). Needs network for the checkpoints.\njulia --project=examples/transformers/export examples/transformers/export/export_stablehlo.jl\n\n# 2. Serve them on the CPU (blocks; Ctrl-C to stop). On a GPU host, drop --cpu and pick a device\n#    with CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=0.\njulia --project=examples/transformers/server examples/transformers/server/serve.jl --cpu\n\n# 3. In another terminal: query all four models.\njulia --project=examples/transformers/client examples/transformers/client/query.jl\n\nThe server port defaults to 8080; set TX_PORT (and TX_HOST for the client) to change it on both step 2 and step 3.","title":"Serve Transformer Text Models"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/examples/transformers/#Export","page":"Serve Transformer Text Models","text":"The export writes one bundle per model to examples/transformers/bundles/, and skips any bundle that already exists; delete bundles/ to export again. The first run downloads the four checkpoints from HuggingFace and, through CondaPkg, the Python packages the export uses. Behind a proxy that intercepts TLS, set SSL_CERT_FILE to a CA bundle that trusts it; by default the script uses the system bundle.","title":"Export"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/examples/transformers/#Serve","page":"Serve Transformer Text Models","text":"The server compiles all four models before it accepts requests. If it fails with EACCES on Reactant's compile cache, set REACTANT_CACHE_DIR to a writable directory.","title":"Serve"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/examples/transformers/#Client","page":"Serve Transformer Text Models","text":"The client sends raw UTF-8 text to all four models and prints what each returns: cosine similarity between sentence embeddings, SPLADE's top expansion terms, the cross-encoder's ranking of candidate answers, and sentiment labels with their probabilities. The Transformer Text Models reference describes the wire format the client uses.","title":"Client"},{"category":"section","location":"","page":"Home","text":"<div id=\"landing\" class=\"landing\">\n<header class=\"landing-hero\">\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__text\">\n    <p class=\"landing-name\" style=\"--landing-name-chars: 17\">ReactantServer.jl</p>\n    <h1 class=\"landing-title\">Production inference for Reactant-compiled models</h1>\n    <p class=\"landing-tagline\">KServe V2 over gRPC from one GPU to many, with compiled XLA models, Julia-first pre and postprocessing, and the most models per card.</p>\n    <div class=\"landing-actions\">\n<a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--brand\" href=\"tutorials/\">Tutorials</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"api/\">API Reference</a>\n      <a class=\"landing-btn landing-btn--alt\" href=\"https://github.com/EnzymeAD/ReactantServer.jl\">View on GitHub</a>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div class=\"landing-hero__image\">\n    <div class=\"landing-hero__image-container\">\n      <img src=\"assets/logo.svg\" alt=\"ReactantServer.jl\" width=\"320\" height=\"320\">\n    </div>\n  </div>\n</header>\n<section class=\"landing-features\" aria-label=\"Features\">\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"client/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">⚡</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">KServe V2, natively</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Speaks the KServe V2 inference API over gRPC, so standard Triton and KServe clients connect unchanged.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"tutorials/export/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🚀</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">XLA under the hood</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Models compile ahead of time through Reactant and XLA into device executables; the runtime is device agnostic, CUDA today with CPU for development.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"bundles/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🧩</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Julia-first</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">A bundle's model.jl registers pre and postprocessing in plain Julia, and every convention follows Julia's, column-major with the batch axis last.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"on_demand_weights/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">💾</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">On-demand weights</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">Weights stay in host RAM and stream to the GPU under a byte budget with cost-aware eviction, so a card serves more models than fit in VRAM.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"scheduling/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🔀</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">A coalescing scheduler</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">A deficit-weighted, cost-aware scheduler merges same-model requests into one execution at a compiled batch size.</p>\n  </a>\n  <a class=\"landing-feature\" href=\"bundles/\">\n<div class=\"landing-feature__icon\">🔁</div>\n<h2 class=\"landing-feature__title\">Hot reload</h2>\n<p class=\"landing-feature__details\">In dynamic mode the server watches the model repository and reloads bundles online, with no restart.</p>\n  </a>\n</section>\n</div>","title":"Home"},{"category":"section","location":"#Why-ReactantServer?","page":"Home","text":"The server is built for workloads where many models share a GPU, such as computer vision and scientific computing. Every model is compiled ahead of time through Reactant and XLA into an optimized executable for the target device, and compiled models are fast. The runtime is device-agnostic: CUDA today, with CPU for development.\n\nThe design balances two resources that pull against each other. Compute is managed by a cost-aware scheduler that coalesces concurrent requests for the same model into one execution. Memory is managed by the on-demand weights cache, which keeps weights in host RAM and moves them onto the GPU within a byte budget. Scheduling policy, pinned models, and multi-GPU placement can all be changed at runtime, without a restart.\n\nThe server is a set of packages, split so that only the worker and the export tooling load Reactant and XLA; the client, the gateway, and the supervisor install without them. Architecture describes each package.","title":"Why ReactantServer?"},{"category":"section","location":"#Get-started","page":"Home","text":"The Tutorials follow a model from export to inference, with two ways to serve it in between:\n\nFrom Julia. Every package is in the General registry, so a Julia environment is all you need, on any machine, with or without a GPU. Start with Serve a Model.\nWith the container image. The supported deployment: a host needs only the NVIDIA driver and a container runtime. Start with Serve on One GPU.\n\nTo see real models, Serve an Object Detector and Serve Transformer Text Models take PyTorch models through export, serving, and inference.\n\nThe server is designed for a trusted network, so read Security before exposing an endpoint.","title":"Get started"},{"category":"section","location":"#Reference","page":"Home","text":"Bundles: the bundle format, the manifest's named-axis notation, and the export frontends.\nNode Configuration: the one typed YAML file that describes a machine.\nScheduling: the cost-aware worker scheduler and batch coalescing.\nOn-demand Weights: host-RAM weights, the byte budget, and fragmentation-aware admission.\nMulti-GPU Gateway: the reverse proxy and its scheduling modes.\nClient Usage: the Reactant-free client and its shared-memory transport.\nMeta Models (experimental): chaining models with data-dependent Julia between stages.\nDeployment: how a node behaves, the container image and its settings, and the multi-node contract.\nThe API: every documented name, collected automatically.","title":"Reference"}]
}
